window.BANK_P=[{"id":"P|FPF|F01","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F01","t":"Route of administration","s":"A 32-year-old female with acute gastroenteritis has <b>severe, continuous vomiting</b> and cannot tolerate oral medications. She has a severe <b>needle phobia</b> and strongly refuses intravenous or intramuscular injections. The physician needs to administer a systemic antiemetic.<br>Which route of drug administration is most appropriate for this patient?","lec":"L1","sl":12,"c":"Choose the route from the <b>patient's state</b>, not the drug. Vomiting or nil-by-mouth removes the oral route; a refusal of needles removes IV, IM and SC. What is left that still gives a <b>systemic</b> effect is rectal, and the exam reaches for it every time these two constraints appear together. Contrast with transdermal, which also needs no cooperation but is deliberately the slowest route on the list.","o":{"A":"Sublingual","B":"Oral","C":"Transdermal","D":"Rectal","E":"Inhalation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Sublingual avoids the needle and avoids swallowing, so it is the best of the wrong answers — but it is reserved for small, lipid-soluble, potent molecules that dissolve in a few minutes, and it is hard to keep a tablet under the tongue of someone who is actively retching.","B":"Ruled out by the first constraint. A patient vomiting continuously will either not swallow the dose or will bring it straight back up before absorption.","C":"A patch needs no cooperation, but absorption across intact skin is the <b>slowest</b> of all routes — useless when the patient is vomiting now.","D":"Correct. A suppository bypasses the stomach entirely and needs no needle, so it satisfies both constraints at once. Slide 12 defines suppositories as bullet-shaped solids that melt at body temperature for rectal insertion; rectal bioavailability is 30-100% and first-pass metabolism is partly avoided, which is enough to reach the chemoreceptor trigger zone.","E":"Inhalation delivers to the lung. There is no inhaled antiemetic in routine use, and the route asks for a coordinated breath the patient cannot reliably give."},"also":"AUTH 4 and BM29 Q1 — the same 'cannot take anything by mouth' logic; BM28 Q4 asks the route a suppository uses","img":"slides_p/L1_12.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F02","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F02","t":"Modified-release dosage forms","s":"A 72-year-old male with severe dysphagia following an ischemic stroke requires maintenance therapy for his chronic conditions. The physician wants to prescribe a medication that releases the active drug slowly over a prolonged period to maintain stable plasma concentrations and allow once-daily dosing. Which of the following dosage forms should <b>NOT</b> be crushed or split before administration through a nasogastric (NG) tube due to the risk of dose dumping and toxicity?","lec":"L1","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11 splits tablets into <b>uncoated, coated, and modified-release</b>. The suffixes worth recognising on sight are <b>ER, XL, SR, CR, LA</b> — all mean a release-controlling structure you must not destroy. The mirror-image trap is the <b>enteric-coated</b> tablet: crushing that one does not cause dose dumping, it exposes the drug to gastric acid and destroys it (or exposes the stomach to the drug). Both are 'do not crush', for opposite reasons.","o":{"A":"Immediate-release (IR) compressed tablet","B":"Extended-release (ER/XL) matrix tablet","C":"Soluble effervescent tablet","D":"Oral suspension","E":"Uncoated chewable tablet"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"An IR tablet is designed to disintegrate at once anyway, so crushing it changes nothing about the release profile.","B":"Correct. The whole point of an ER/XL matrix is that the polymer skeleton meters the drug out over hours. Crush it and the entire day's dose is released and absorbed at once — <b>dose dumping</b> — pushing the plasma level straight past the therapeutic window into toxicity.","C":"An effervescent tablet is <b>meant</b> to be dissolved in water before administration; that is its normal route through an NG tube.","D":"A suspension is already liquid. Shake and give.","E":"A chewable tablet is designed to be broken up in the mouth, so mechanical disruption is exactly what it expects."},"also":"AX [58] L29-1 — the elderly patient whose tablets must be crushed, the same question from the other side","img":"slides_p/L1_11.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F03","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F03","t":"Prescription writing","s":"A 62-year-old male with type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension visits the outpatient clinic for a routine follow-up. The physician writes a prescription for his antihypertensive medication as follows: <b>\"Amlodipine 5 mg PO BID PC and Metformin 500 mg PO TID AC\"</b> Which of the following describes the correct dosing schedule and route of administration for these two medications?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"Slide 18 lists the transcription abbreviations. Learn them as three axes: <b>route</b> (PO, SL, PR, IV, IM, SC), <b>frequency</b> (OD/qd once, BID twice, TID three times, QID four times, q4h every four hours, hs at bedtime, prn as needed), and <b>food</b> (ac before, pc after, hs at bedtime). The examiner's favourite confusion is <b>ac vs pc</b> — <i>ante</i> comes before, <i>post</i> comes after. Note that the clinical realism is deliberately off here (amlodipine is genuinely a once-daily drug); the item is testing the abbreviations, not the prescribing.","o":{"A":"Amlodipine by mouth twice daily before meals; Metformin by mouth three times daily after meals.","B":"Amlodipine by mouth once daily at bedtime; Metformin by mouth twice daily before meals.","C":"Amlodipine by mouth twice daily after meals; Metformin by mouth three times daily before meals.","D":"Amlodipine placed under the tongue twice daily after meals; Metformin by mouth three times daily as needed.","E":"Amlodipine by mouth twice daily as needed; Metformin by mouth three times daily at bedtime."},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Reverses both meal instructions. <i>pc</i> is after food and <i>ac</i> is before food, not the other way round.","B":"<i>OD/hs</i> would be once daily at bedtime — neither abbreviation appears in this prescription.","C":"Correct. <b>PO</b> = per os, by mouth. <b>BID (b.i.d.)</b> = twice daily, <b>TID (t.i.d.)</b> = three times daily. <b>PC (p.c., post cibum)</b> = after meals, <b>AC (a.c., ante cibum)</b> = before meals. So: amlodipine by mouth twice daily after meals, metformin by mouth three times daily before meals.","D":"<i>PO</i> is by mouth and swallowed; <b>SL</b> is the abbreviation for sublingual. <i>prn</i> is the one that means as needed.","E":"<i>prn</i> (as needed) and <i>hs</i> (at bedtime) are not written here either."},"also":"BM32 1-3 and BM31 Q2 — both ask you to convert a written instruction into the abbreviation","img":"slides_p/L1_18.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F04","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F04","t":"First-pass metabolism","s":"A patient is given an oral drug that has a <b>high first-pass metabolism</b>. If the drug is administered via the <b>sublingual</b> route instead of orally, which of the following pharmacokinetic parameters will most likely increase?","lec":"L2","sl":7,"c":"Bioavailability is defined on slide 7 as the fractional extent to which a dose reaches the systemic circulation. Two things lower it — <b>incomplete absorption</b> and <b>first-pass metabolism</b> — and only the oral route suffers both. The routes that escape first pass are <b>IV, sublingual/buccal, inhaled, transdermal</b>, and <b>rectal partially</b> (the lower rectal veins drain systemically, the upper drain to the portal vein). Keep F strictly separate from Vd, CL and t1/2: F is about <i>how much gets in</i>, the others are about <i>what happens after</i>.","o":{"A":"Volume of distribution (Vd)","B":"Half-life (t1/2)","C":"Bioavailability (F)","D":"Rate of elimination","E":"Drug clearance (CL)"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Vd is a property of how the drug partitions between plasma and tissue once it is <b>in</b> the body. Changing the door it came in through does not change that partitioning.","B":"t1/2 = 0.693 x Vd / CL. Neither term is altered by the route, so the half-life is unchanged.","C":"Correct. Sublingual absorption drains into veins that return to the superior vena cava, so the dose reaches the systemic circulation <b>without passing through the liver first</b>. Less drug is destroyed before it arrives, so the fraction reaching the circulation intact — F — rises. This is the whole reason a high-first-pass drug is formulated sublingually.","D":"The rate of elimination is set by the eliminating organs, not by the route of entry. If anything the <i>total</i> amount cleared per hour rises simply because more drug got in — but the elimination process itself is unchanged.","E":"Clearance describes the volume of plasma cleared per unit time by liver and kidney. Bypassing the portal circulation changes how much drug arrives, not the organ's capacity to clear it."},"also":"BM31 Q4 and Q8 — the highest and the lowest first-pass routes; BM32 1-1 is the sublingual nitroglycerin version","img":"slides_p/L2_7.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F05","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F05","t":"Onset by route","s":"A 45-year-old female presents to the emergency department with <b>status epilepticus</b>. The medical team is unable to establish intravenous (IV) access due to severe peripheral venous collapse. To achieve rapid systemic drug absorption and bypass first-pass hepatic metabolism <b>without using an IV line</b>, which of the following <b>non-parenteral</b> routes of administration provides the fastest onset of action for a lipid-soluble benzodiazepine formulation?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11's route table is worth memorising as a ranking. Onset, fastest to slowest: <b>IV → inhaled → sublingual/intranasal → IM → SC → oral → rectal → transdermal</b>. Read the question for its two filters — here 'non-parenteral' kills IV/IM/SC/intra-articular, and 'bypass first pass' kills oral — and whatever survives both is the answer. Note the pairing with F04: the same anatomical fact (mucosal veins drain systemically) explains both the speed and the higher bioavailability.","o":{"A":"Oral administration via NG tube","B":"Subcutaneous (SC) injection into the deltoid region","C":"Intra-articular injection","D":"Transdermal patch application","E":"Sublingual (SL) / Intranasal (IN) administration"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Oral is both the slowest of the enteral options and the one route that is fully exposed to first pass — the two things the question explicitly rules out.","B":"SC is a <b>parenteral</b> route, which the stem excludes, and it is slow: subcutaneous tissue is poorly perfused compared with muscle.","C":"Intra-articular is a <b>local</b> route into a joint space, given precisely so the drug does <i>not</i> go systemic. It is also parenteral.","D":"Transdermal is the slowest route in the whole table; a patch takes hours to build a plasma level and is designed for maintenance, never rescue.","E":"Correct. The sublingual and nasal mucosae are thin, richly vascular, and drain directly into the systemic circulation. A lipid-soluble benzodiazepine crosses them in minutes and reaches the brain without a first pass — which is exactly why intranasal midazolam is the standard no-IV-access rescue for seizures."},"also":"AX [58] L29-2 — fastest onset without first-pass metabolism, asked directly","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F06","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F06","t":"Volume of distribution","s":"A 24-year-old female is prescribed an antibiotic with a <b>volume of distribution (Vd) of 400 L</b>. Which of the following statements best describes the pharmacokinetic property of this drug?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"Anchor the number to body-fluid anatomy from slide 12: <b>plasma ~5 L</b>, <b>extracellular ~15 L</b>, <b>total body water ~42 L</b> in a 70 kg adult. Then Vd &le; 5 L = plasma only; 5-15 L = extracellular; ~42 L = total body water; <b>&gt; 42 L = tissue sequestration</b>, and hundreds of litres means fat or deep tissue binding (fluoxetine reaches ~2,450 L). The clinical consequence: a drug with a huge Vd <b>cannot be dialysed off</b>, because only the small plasma fraction is accessible.","o":{"A":"The drug is highly bound to plasma albumin.","B":"The drug is likely restricted to the vascular compartment.","C":"The drug is highly sequestered in peripheral tissues (e.g., fat).","D":"The drug has a very low molecular weight and is water-soluble.","E":"The drug will be cleared rapidly by the kidneys through simple filtration."},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Heavy albumin binding <b>holds the drug in the plasma</b> and therefore gives a <i>low</i> Vd — heparin's 4 L is the worked example. This is the opposite of 400 L.","B":"A drug confined to the vascular compartment has a Vd of about <b>5 L</b>, the plasma volume. 400 L is eighty times that.","C":"Correct. Vd is an <b>apparent</b> volume: the volume that <i>would</i> be needed to hold the whole dose at the measured plasma concentration. A figure of 400 L is far larger than total body water (~42 L), which can only mean the plasma concentration is very low because the drug has left the blood and accumulated in tissue and fat.","D":"Small, water-soluble molecules stay in body water and give a Vd at or below ~42 L. Extensive tissue sequestration needs <b>lipid solubility</b>, not water solubility.","E":"Vd says nothing about the speed of elimination — that is <b>clearance</b>. In fact a huge Vd usually makes elimination <i>slower</i>, because most of the drug is out in tissue where the kidney cannot reach it."},"also":"BM32 2-2 and BM29 Q5 — the plasma-restricted anticoagulant, the exact mirror image of this item; AUTH 10 asks the dialysis consequence","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F07","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F07","t":"Enzyme induction","s":"A 62-year-old male on <b>Warfarin</b> for atrial fibrillation is started on <b>Rifampin</b> for the treatment of tuberculosis. Two weeks later, his International Normalized Ratio (INR) drops from <b>2.5 to 1.2</b> (subtherapeutic). What is the most likely mechanism of this drug interaction?","lec":"L2","sl":22,"c":"Slide 22 names <b>rifampin</b> on the inducer list beside the combined oral contraceptive failure it causes. Sort the interaction by direction and by clock: an <b>inducer</b> (rifampin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, St John's wort, chronic alcohol) lowers the level of its victim over <b>days to weeks</b> because enzyme has to be made; an <b>inhibitor</b> (macrolides, azoles, ritonavir, grapefruit juice, cimetidine) raises it within <b>hours</b> because the existing enzyme is blocked at once. The trap on stopping is the reverse: finish the rifampin and the induced enzyme decays, warfarin accumulates, and the INR climbs into the bleeding range.","o":{"A":"Rifampin inhibits CYP450 enzymes, decreasing warfarin metabolism.","B":"Rifampin induces CYP450 enzymes, increasing warfarin metabolism.","C":"Rifampin competes with warfarin for plasma protein binding sites.","D":"Rifampin inhibits the absorption of warfarin in the small intestine.","E":"Rifampin acts as a Vitamin K agonist in the liver."},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Inhibition would slow warfarin's breakdown, raise its plasma level and drive the INR <b>up</b>, risking bleeding. The INR fell, so the arrow points the other way.","B":"Correct. Rifampin is the archetypal <b>enzyme inducer</b> — it upregulates CYP3A4 and CYP2C9 transcription, so more enzyme is available to metabolise warfarin, plasma warfarin falls and the INR falls with it. The <b>two-week</b> delay is the tell: induction requires new protein synthesis, so it builds over 1-2 weeks and washes out just as slowly.","C":"Displacement from albumin transiently <b>increases</b> free warfarin and would push the INR up, not down — and the effect is short-lived, not a steady two-week drift.","D":"Rifampin does not block warfarin absorption. Bile-acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine are the drugs that do that.","E":"Vitamin K opposes warfarin, but rifampin has no vitamin K activity; and this would be a pharmacodynamic antagonism, not the enzyme mechanism the timing points to."},"also":"AUTH 9 asks the time course of this exact pair; BM30 Q24 (losartan) and AX [58] L-DI-2 are the same mechanism with different victims","img":"slides_p/L2_22.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F08","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F08","t":"Ion trapping","s":"A 50-year-old patient with an accidental overdose of a <b>weak acid</b> drug (e.g., Aspirin) is brought to the ER. To enhance the renal excretion of this drug, the physician administers <b>Sodium Bicarbonate</b> to alkalinize the urine. What is the principle behind this intervention?","lec":"L2","sl":25,"c":"Two lines cover every version of this question. <b>Like is trapped in unlike</b>: a weak <b>acid</b> is trapped in <b>alkaline</b> urine (alkalinise with bicarbonate to clear aspirin, phenobarbital, methotrexate), and a weak <b>base</b> is trapped in <b>acidic</b> urine (amphetamine, quinidine). And <b>only the non-ionised form crosses membranes</b> — slide 6's Henderson-Hasselbalch treatment is the same equation used for gastric absorption, so the reasoning transfers directly.","o":{"A":"Increasing the non-ionized form of the drug to prevent reabsorption.","B":"Increasing the ionized form of the drug to trap it in the renal tubule.","C":"Decreasing the non-ionized form of the drug to promote reabsorption.","D":"Decreasing the ionized form of the drug to enhance filtration.","E":"Directly neutralizing the drug in the plasma."},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Internally contradictory. The <b>non-ionised</b> form is the lipid-soluble one, and it is precisely the form that <i>is</i> reabsorbed across the tubular epithelium.","B":"Correct. By Henderson-Hasselbalch, a weak acid in an alkaline medium gives up its proton and becomes the <b>ionised, water-soluble anion</b>. Ionised molecules cannot cross the lipid membrane of the tubular cell, so once filtered they are <b>trapped in the tubular lumen</b> and carried out in the urine. This is <b>ion trapping</b>, and it is the basis of urinary alkalinisation in salicylate poisoning.","C":"Reabsorption is what we are trying to prevent, not promote — and less non-ionised drug means <i>less</i> reabsorption anyway.","D":"Glomerular filtration depends on molecular size and protein binding, not on charge — and the manoeuvre increases the ionised fraction, not decreases it.","E":"Bicarbonate does not chemically inactivate aspirin. It works by shifting a pH-dependent equilibrium in the tubule, and the plasma alkalinisation additionally pulls salicylate out of the CNS."},"also":"AX [58] L26-1 — which drug's clearance rises most when the urine is alkalinised","img":"slides_p/L2_25.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F09","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F09","t":"Time to steady state","s":"A 45-year-old male with chronic kidney disease (CKD) requires an intravenous dose of a drug that follows first-order kinetics. If the drug's half-life (t1/2) is normally 4 hours but increases to <b>8 hours</b> due to his renal impairment, how long will it take for the drug to reach a new steady-state concentration after starting a continuous infusion?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"Slide 28's rule: time to steady state depends <b>only on half-life</b>, never on the dose or the infusion rate. Raise the rate and you reach a <i>higher</i> plateau, but you reach it at the same moment. The accumulation ladder is worth knowing by heart — <b>1 t1/2 = 50%, 2 = 75%, 3 = 87.5%, 4 = 94%, 5 = 97%</b>. If you need the level sooner than 4-5 half-lives allows, the only lever is a <b>loading dose</b>, which fills Vd immediately.","o":{"A":"32-40 hours","B":"16-20 hours","C":"8-10 hours","D":"4-5 hours","E":"24-26 hours"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Steady state is reached after <b>4-5 half-lives</b>, and the half-life in this patient is 8 hours, so 4 x 8 = 32 h to 5 x 8 = 40 h. The trap is using the <i>normal</i> 4 h half-life and answering 16-20 hours; the stem gives you the impaired figure on purpose.","B":"Two half-lives, ~75%. Clinically useful, but not yet steady state.","C":"About one half-life — only ~50% of steady state.","D":"This is roughly one half-life of the <i>healthy</i> patient — two errors compounded.","E":"Between three and four half-lives (~88-94%). Close, but the conventional teaching figure is 4-5 half-lives."},"also":"AX [55] L26-4 asks the rule directly; BM29 Q12 and BM28 Q12 are the numeric versions","img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F10","t":"Time to steady state","s":"A 52-year-old male is initiated on a continuous intravenous infusion of a cardioactive medication <b>without a loading dose</b>. The drug follows first-order elimination kinetics with an elimination half-life (t1/2) of <b>8 hours</b>. Approximately how long will it take for the drug's plasma concentration to reach <b>75–80%</b> of its steady-state concentration (Css)?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"Read the <b>percentage</b> in the stem before reaching for the 4-5 half-lives rule — F09 and F10 use the identical half-life and want different answers purely because one asks for full steady state and the other for 75-80%. The ladder again: <b>50 / 75 / 87.5 / 94 / 97%</b> at 1 to 5 half-lives. The phrase 'without a loading dose' is there to confirm you must wait for accumulation rather than assume the level was filled at time zero.","o":{"A":"8 hours","B":"24 hours","C":"16 hours","D":"32 hours","E":"40 hours"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"One half-life gives <b>50%</b> of Css.","B":"Three half-lives would be 24 hours and ~87.5%.","C":"Correct. Each half-life closes half the remaining gap to the plateau: after one you are at 50%, after <b>two</b> at <b>75%</b>. Two half-lives of 8 hours is 16 hours.","D":"Four half-lives, ~94% — this is the figure conventionally called steady state, not 75-80%.","E":"Five half-lives, ~97%; effectively complete."},"also":"F09 in this same set — same half-life, different target percentage","img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F11","t":"Maintenance dose","s":"A 58-year-old female is started on an oral medication with a target steady-state concentration (Css) of <b>10 mg/L</b>. The drug clearance (CL) is <b>4L/h</b>, and oral bioavailability (F) is <b>0.8</b>. To maintain this concentration, what daily maintenance dose (MD) must be administered?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"The two dosing formulas on slides 28-29 differ in which parameter they use, and that is the whole examinable point. <b>Loading dose = Css x Vd / F</b> — it fills a <i>volume</i>, so it uses Vd and is unchanged by renal or hepatic impairment. <b>Maintenance dose = Css x CL / F</b> — it replaces what is <i>removed</i>, so it uses CL and must be cut when clearance falls. Divide by F in both. Watch the units: CL in L/h gives a dose per hour, so multiply by 24 for a daily dose.","o":{"A":"400 mg/day","B":"1,200 mg/day","C":"768 mg/day","D":"960 mg/day","E":"480 mg/day"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"10 x 4 x 24 x 0.8 = 768 is the F-multiplied error; 400 does not arise from any correct step.","B":"Correct. MD = <b>Css x CL / F</b> = 10 mg/L x 4 L/h / 0.8 = 50 mg/h. Over 24 hours: 50 x 24 = <b>1,200 mg/day</b>. Sanity check: the IV requirement is 960 mg, and because only 80% of an oral dose gets in, you must give 960 / 0.8 = 1,200 mg.","C":"This is the classic error: <b>multiplying</b> by F instead of dividing. Bioavailability is a loss, so an F below 1 must make the required dose <i>larger</i>, never smaller.","D":"10 x 4 x 24 = 960 mg is the correct <b>intravenous</b> dose. It forgets that 20% of an oral dose never arrives.","E":"This is 10 x 4 x 24 x 0.5 — the wrong F, or a half-day."},"also":"BM32 2-5, BM30 Q12 and BM28 Q10 are the loading-dose half of the same pair; BM30 Q13 is the infusion-rate version","img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F12","t":"Potency and efficacy","s":"In a laboratory experiment, Drug A and Drug B are both agonists at the same receptor. Drug A produces a maximal response at a <b>lower concentration</b> than Drug B, but Drug B produces a <b>higher overall maximal response</b> than Drug A. Which of the following is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":8,"c":"Slide 8 defines both terms. Fix them to the axes and you can never mix them up: <b>potency = the x-axis (dose)</b>, further <b>left</b> = more potent = lower EC50; <b>efficacy = the y-axis (Emax)</b>, <b>higher</b> plateau = more effective. Clinically, efficacy is what matters at the bedside — a more potent drug just means a smaller number on the prescription, whereas a more efficacious drug can do something the other one cannot do at any dose. Compare morphine (high efficacy) with buprenorphine (potent, but a partial agonist with a ceiling).","o":{"A":"Drug A is more potent and has higher efficacy than Drug B.","B":"Drug A is less potent but has higher efficacy than Drug B.","C":"Drug A is less potent and has lower efficacy than Drug B.","D":"Drug A is more potent but has lower efficacy than Drug B.","E":"Drug A is a competitive antagonist of Drug B."},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The first half is right, the second is not: B reaches the higher ceiling, so B has the greater efficacy.","B":"Both halves inverted. A needs <i>less</i> drug, so A is the more potent.","C":"Contradicts the stem, which says A acts at the lower concentration.","D":"Correct. <b>Potency</b> is read off the concentration axis — A works at a lower concentration, so A is more potent (lower EC50, curve further left). <b>Efficacy</b> is read off the response axis — B climbs higher, so B has the greater Emax. The two properties are independent, and this item exists to prove it.","E":"The stem calls both drugs <b>agonists</b>. A competitive antagonist produces no response of its own; it shifts the agonist's curve to the right without lowering Emax."},"also":"AX [57] L23-1 — two full agonists with different EC50s; F13 in this set is the graphical version","img":"slides_p/L3_8.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F13","t":"Dose-response curves","s":"An animal study was performed to compare two new vasodilator drugs (A and B) to a standard agent (C). The results of vasorelaxation measurements are shown in the graph below.<div style=\"margin-top:12px\"><img src=\"figs_p/fpf_doseresponse.webp\" alt=\"Graded log dose-response curves for vasodilators A, B and C\" style=\"max-width:100%;border-radius:10px;background:#fff\"></div>","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11 shows this exact figure type and asks the same question. Work it in two passes and never in one: first rank the curves <b>left to right</b> for potency (here <b>A &gt; B &gt; C</b>), then rank the plateaus <b>bottom to top</b> for efficacy (here <b>B &asymp; C &gt; A</b>). Then test each option against the two rankings separately. In a compound statement every clause must be true — the commonest way to lose this mark is accepting an option because its first half is right.","o":{"A":"Drug B is the least potent.","B":"Drug C is the most potent.","C":"Drug A is the most effective.","D":"Drug A is more potent than drug B and more effective than drug C.","E":"Drug B is more potent than drug C and more effective than drug A."},"a":"E","r":{"A":"<b>C</b> is the least potent — its curve sits furthest to the right, so it needs the largest dose. B lies between A and C.","B":"Reversed. The <b>leftmost</b> curve is the most potent, and that is <b>A</b>.","C":"A is the <i>least</i> effective: its plateau is the lowest of the three, which is what makes it look like a partial agonist on this plot.","D":"The first half is true (A is more potent than B), but the second is false — A's ceiling is <i>below</i> C's, so A is not more effective than C. One false half makes the option false.","E":"Correct. Both halves hold. On the dose axis B lies to the <b>left of C</b>, so B is the more potent of that pair. On the response axis B plateaus <b>above A</b>, so B is the more effective of that pair. Note the deliberate design: A wins on potency, B wins on efficacy, and the only true statement compares each drug with the one it actually beats."},"f":["image"],"also":"BM30 F1-Q3 — the same three vasodilators, same graph, as a past paper; BM32 3-3, BM31 Q15 and BM30 Q16 are the same figure with different letters","img":"slides_p/L3_11.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F14","t":"First-in-human dose selection","s":"A novel anti-inflammatory compound has completed repeat-dose toxicology studies in the most relevant animal species. The development team has calculated the <b>human equivalent dose (HED)</b> from the <b>no-observed-adverse-effect level (NOAEL)</b> and is preparing a first-in-human trial. Which strategy provides the most scientifically appropriate basis for selecting the initial human dose?","lec":"L4","sl":19,"c":"Slide 19's <b>TGN1412</b> case study is the reason this question exists. The anti-CD28 superagonist passed toxicology in rodents and macaques, was dosed in six healthy volunteers at a small fraction of the animal NOAEL, and caused a cytokine storm within minutes — because the human receptor responded at a concentration the animal NOAEL said nothing about. The lesson the regulators drew is precisely option A: NOAEL &rarr; HED &rarr; safety factor, then sanity-check against <b>pharmacological</b> activity and take <b>MABEL</b> when it is lower. Note that HED, NOAEL and MABEL are not named on the BM33 slides themselves; the site is testing the principle the TGN1412 slide teaches.","o":{"A":"Integrate HED, safety factor, exposure, pharmacology, and MABEL.","B":"Use the animal HED after confirming that the toxicology studies were adequately conducted.","C":"Select a fixed fraction of the animal NOAEL without considering predicted human pharmacologic activity.","D":"Choose the lowest technically measurable dose because laboratory feasibility alone provides sufficient protection in first-in-human studies.","E":"Start at the animal NOAEL-equivalent exposure because cross-species conversion makes additional safety and pharmacologic considerations unnecessary."},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. No single number is trusted on its own. The regulatory approach converts NOAEL to HED, then divides by a <b>safety factor</b> (conventionally 10), then cross-checks the result against predicted human <b>exposure</b> and <b>pharmacology</b> — and for a biological with a steep or immune-mediated effect, against <b>MABEL</b> (minimum anticipated biological effect level), taking whichever is lower.","B":"Starting at the full HED leaves <b>no margin at all</b> for species differences. A well-conducted toxicology study tells you what happened in that species; it does not tell you the human is the same animal.","C":"A fixed fraction of NOAEL is a purely toxicological calculation. It ignores the possibility that the compound is <b>pharmacologically active in humans far below</b> any dose that was toxic in the animal — which is the failure mode MABEL exists to catch.","D":"Analytical feasibility is a laboratory constraint, not a safety argument. A dose chosen because the assay can see it may be either uselessly low or dangerously high.","E":"The strongest wrong answer, because it states the fallacy outright: cross-species conversion does <b>not</b> remove uncertainty, it is only the first step in quantifying it."},"also":"BM32 4-1, BM30 Q40 and BM29 Q24 — the purpose of preclinical testing and of Phase 1","img":"slides_p/L4_19.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F15","t":"Aspirin adverse effects","s":"A 4-year-old boy is diagnosed with <b>varicella (chickenpox)</b> and presents with a high-grade fever. Which of the following medications must be strictly avoided in this patient to prevent the development of <b>Reye's syndrome</b>?","lec":"L5","sl":21,"c":"Slide 21 is the NSAID adverse-effect slide, and aspirin appears on slide 20 as the <b>salicylate</b> among the nonselective COX inhibitors. Reye's syndrome itself is not printed on any BM33 slide — it is assumed background — so hold it as a rule rather than looking for it in the deck: <b>never aspirin in a febrile child with a viral illness</b>. Group it with aspirin's other named cautions: gastric ulceration, the antiplatelet effect that lasts the life of the platelet, and the bronchospasm of AERD.","o":{"A":"Chlorpheniramine","B":"Acetaminophen","C":"Ibuprofen","D":"Acyclovir","E":"Aspirin"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Chlorpheniramine is a first-generation antihistamine given for the itch of chickenpox; it is unrelated to Reye's.","B":"Paracetamol is the <b>antipyretic of choice</b> in a febrile child and carries no Reye's risk.","C":"Ibuprofen is a non-salicylate NSAID and is not implicated in Reye's syndrome. (It is relatively avoided in varicella for a different reason — a possible association with necrotising soft-tissue infection — but that is not what this question asks.)","D":"Aciclovir is the antiviral used <i>for</i> varicella. It has no link to Reye's syndrome.","E":"Correct. The triad is <b>child + viral illness (varicella or influenza) + aspirin</b>. Salicylate injures hepatic mitochondria in this setting, producing the acute encephalopathy and fatty liver of Reye's syndrome. Aspirin is therefore contraindicated under about 16 years of age except for specific indications such as Kawasaki disease."},"f":["completed"],"also":"F16 in this set is the other classic aspirin catastrophe; BM31 Q37 and AUTH 30 cover NSAID safety more broadly","img":"slides_p/L5_21.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F16","t":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease","s":"A 35-year-old female with a history of <b>asthma</b> and <b>nasal polyps</b> experiences severe wheezing and a runny nose shortly after taking <b>aspirin</b> for a headache. Which of the following conditions does this patient most likely have?","lec":"L5","sl":23,"c":"Slide 23 draws the arachidonic-acid fork and lists exactly what leukotrienes do in asthma — bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion, mucosal oedema, inflammatory-cell infiltration. AERD is that slide made clinical: shut the <b>COX</b> arm with a nonselective NSAID and substrate is diverted into the <b>LOX</b> arm. Two consequences worth carrying: the safe analgesics are <b>paracetamol</b> and, usually, a <b>selective COX-2 inhibitor</b> (COX-1 is the culprit arm); and the treatment is a <b>leukotriene receptor antagonist</b> such as montelukast. Classify it as a <b>type B, non-immune (pseudoallergic)</b> reaction in the L3B scheme — there is no IgE and no prior sensitisation.","o":{"A":"Allergic Rhinitis to pollen","B":"Anaphylactic reaction to Paracetamol","C":"Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus","D":"Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)","E":"Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease (AERD)"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Pollen allergy does not start within minutes of swallowing aspirin. The drug is the trigger the stem hands you.","B":"She took aspirin, not paracetamol — and anaphylaxis would bring urticaria, angioedema and hypotension, not this respiratory picture. (Paracetamol is in fact the safe analgesic here.)","C":"Nothing in the vignette points to diabetes, and it would not begin minutes after a tablet.","D":"Reflux can aggravate asthma, but it does not produce a rhinitis and it has no temporal link to an aspirin dose.","E":"Correct. Asthma + nasal polyps + a respiratory reaction to aspirin or any NSAID is <b>Samter's triad</b>, now called AERD. It is <b>not</b> an IgE allergy: blocking COX-1 shunts arachidonic acid down the lipoxygenase arm, and the resulting surge of <b>cysteinyl leukotrienes</b> causes the bronchoconstriction and rhinorrhoea. That is why the reaction generalises to <i>every</i> nonselective NSAID rather than to one drug."},"f":["completed"],"also":"AUTH 27 (montelukast) and AUTH 28 give the two arms of this cascade; BM29 S2-Q38 is the 'which NSAID is safe' version","img":"slides_p/L5_23.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F17","t":"First-generation H1 antagonists","s":"A 23-year-old female presents to a pharmacy seeking medication to prevent <b>motion sickness</b> prior to a boat trip. She is recommended a <b>1st-generation H1 receptor antagonist</b>. Which property enables 1st-generation H1 antagonists to prevent motion sickness, unlike 2nd-generation agents?","lec":"L5","sl":10,"c":"Slide 10 lists the first generation — <b>chlorpheniramine, dimenhydrinate, diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, cinnarizine, flunarizine, promethazine</b> — and their therapeutic uses; slide 11 lists the second — <b>cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine, desloratadine, levocetirizine, bilastine</b>. One property separates them and explains every difference: <b>CNS penetration</b>. It gives generation 1 its uses (motion sickness, vertigo, sedation, antiemesis) and its side effects (drowsiness, antimuscarinic dryness); its absence makes generation 2 the right choice for plain allergic rhinitis in someone who has to drive. The exam's favourite trap is the reverse case — a patient who must <i>stay alert</i>, where the answer flips to the second generation.","o":{"A":"High selectivity for peripheral H1 receptors","B":"Inhibition of gastric H2 receptors decreasing acid volume","C":"Potent antagonism at 5-HT3 receptors in the solitary tract","D":"Penetration into the CNS to block central H1 and M1 receptors","E":"Direct blockade of peripheral alpha1-adrenergic receptors"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Peripheral selectivity is the defining feature of the <b>2nd</b> generation — and it is exactly why they do <i>not</i> work for motion sickness.","B":"H2 blockade acts on gastric parietal cells (ranitidine, famotidine) and has nothing to do with the vestibular apparatus.","C":"5-HT3 antagonism is ondansetron's mechanism, used for chemotherapy-induced vomiting — not the vestibular pathway, and not an antihistamine property.","D":"Correct. First-generation agents are lipophilic and are not pumped back out by P-glycoprotein, so they cross the blood-brain barrier. Once central they block <b>H1</b> and, importantly, <b>muscarinic M1</b> receptors in the vestibular nuclei and the vomiting centre, damping the vestibular input that drives motion sickness. The same CNS entry is what causes their sedation and their antimuscarinic dry mouth.","E":"Some first-generation agents do have weak alpha-blocking activity, which explains a little postural hypotension — but it is peripheral and irrelevant to motion sickness."},"also":"BM31 Q40 and BM30 Q45 (the Parkinson's twist) are the motion-sickness version; BM31 Q43 and BM30 Q46 want the non-sedating agent instead","img":"slides_p/L5_10.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F18","t":"Methotrexate mechanism","s":"A patient taking weekly <b>methotrexate</b> for psoriasis presents with a sore throat and painful oral ulcers. Laboratory tests show a slightly <b>elevated MCV</b>. Which enzyme's inhibition is directly responsible for these findings?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"Slide 10 puts methotrexate at the head of the antimetabolites as the <b>folate antagonist</b> (given PO, IV, IM and intrathecally). Read the toxicity straight off the mechanism: block FH4 and every rapidly dividing tissue suffers, which is why slide 12 lists <b>myelosuppression, megaloblastic anaemia, mucositis, nephrotoxicity at high dose, hepatic fibrosis and pulmonary toxicity</b>. Note the dosing tell in the stem — methotrexate for psoriasis and rheumatoid arthritis is <b>weekly</b>, and daily dosing by mistake is a classic fatal prescribing error.","o":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Thymidylate synthase","C":"Xanthine oxidase","D":"Cyclooxygenase-2","E":"Adenosine deaminase"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Methotrexate is a competitive inhibitor of <b>dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)</b>, the enzyme that regenerates dihydrofolate (FH2) into the usable tetrahydrofolate (FH4). Without FH4 the cell cannot make thymidylate or purines, so the fastest-dividing tissues fail first: the oral mucosa ulcerates (<b>stomatitis</b>) and erythroid precursors arrest their nuclear maturation while the cytoplasm keeps growing, giving <b>megaloblastic, high-MCV</b> red cells.","B":"Thymidylate synthase is inhibited <i>downstream</i> and by a different drug — <b>5-fluorouracil</b>. Methotrexate reduces its cofactor supply rather than blocking the enzyme.","C":"Xanthine oxidase is the target of <b>allopurinol</b>, and the enzyme that activates 6-mercaptopurine — the reason allopurinol forces a 6-MP dose reduction.","D":"COX-2 is the NSAID target. Blocking it causes no marrow or mucosal toxicity of this kind.","E":"Adenosine deaminase is inhibited by <b>pentostatin</b> in hairy cell leukaemia."},"also":"F19 and F20 in this set complete the methotrexate trio; BM32 S2-4 and BM31 S2-Q25 cover the xanthine oxidase distractor","img":"slides_p/L6_10.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F19","t":"Methotrexate rescue","s":"If a patient on methotrexate develops severe bone marrow suppression (pancytopenia) due to an accidental overdose, which <b>'active' form of folate</b> is preferred for immediate rescue?","lec":"L6","sl":12,"c":"The single distinction that carries this question: <b>folic acid needs DHFR, folinic acid does not</b>. Anything that must be reduced by the blocked enzyme is useless as an antidote. Keep the timing straight too — after high-dose methotrexate, leucovorin is deliberately <b>delayed</b> so the tumour is exposed first, then normal marrow and mucosa are rescued. And note the companion measure on slide 12's nephrotoxicity line: methotrexate is a weak acid, so the urine is <b>alkalinised</b> to keep it ionised and prevent it crystallising in the tubule.","o":{"A":"Leucovorin (Folinic acid)","B":"Folic acid (Vitamin B9)","C":"Cobalamin (Vitamin B12)","D":"Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6)","E":"Thiamine (Vitamin B1)"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Leucovorin (folinic acid, 5-formyl-FH4)</b> is already reduced, so it enters the folate pool <b>downstream of DHFR</b> and bypasses the block entirely. This is why 'leucovorin rescue' works, and why it is given routinely a set number of hours after high-dose methotrexate.","B":"Folic acid is the <b>inactive, un-reduced</b> vitamin. It still needs DHFR to become FH4 — and DHFR is the very enzyme methotrexate has blocked, so it cannot rescue an overdose.","C":"B12 deficiency also causes a megaloblastic anaemia, but it is a different cofactor and does nothing about a blocked DHFR.","D":"Pyridoxine is the rescue for <b>isoniazid</b> toxicity, not methotrexate.","E":"Thiamine treats Wernicke's encephalopathy and has no role here."},"also":"AUTH 32 asks this rescue directly; F08 in this set is the urinary-alkalinisation principle behind the same drug","img":"slides_p/L6_12.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F20","t":"Methotrexate and folate supplementation","s":"A medical student is asked why <b>folic acid is usually omitted on the day of methotrexate administration</b> in some protocols. What is the theoretical concern regarding co-administration on the same day?","lec":"L6","sl":10,"c":"This is the practical counterpart to F19: <b>folic acid</b> is routine supplementation on non-methotrexate days, <b>folinic acid (leucovorin)</b> is rescue after the dose. Both come from the same fact — methotrexate acts by starving the cell of reduced folate — but they are used at different times and for different reasons, and swapping them is the error the question is hunting. Note the hedge in the stem ('theoretical'): the clinical significance of same-day folate is debated, and many rheumatology protocols do not bother separating them.","o":{"A":"Enhanced renal clearance of methotrexate leading to subtherapeutic levels","B":"Increased risk of acute hepatotoxicity due to synergistic effects","C":"Chemical complexation in the gastric lumen preventing absorption","D":"Competitive inhibition reducing the cellular uptake of methotrexate","E":"Induction of cytochrome P450 enzymes accelerating methotrexate metabolism"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Folic acid does not alter renal handling of methotrexate. What does matter renally is urine pH.","B":"The opposite is true: folate supplementation is given precisely to <b>reduce</b> methotrexate's hepatotoxicity and mucositis.","C":"There is no chelation or complexation between the two in the stomach. That mechanism belongs to tetracyclines with calcium or iron.","D":"Correct. Folic acid and methotrexate are structural analogues and enter the cell through the same carrier — the <b>reduced folate carrier</b> — and compete for the same intracellular polyglutamation. Give a large folate dose at the same moment and it competes methotrexate away from uptake, so the theoretical concern is a <b>loss of efficacy</b>, not added toxicity. Folate is therefore given on the <i>other</i> days, where it reduces mucositis and hepatotoxicity without blunting the weekly dose.","E":"Methotrexate is cleared largely <b>unchanged by the kidney</b>, so CYP induction is not a meaningful route for this drug at all."},"also":"F18 and F19 in this set — the mechanism and the rescue","img":"slides_p/L6_10.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F21","t":"Anthracycline toxicity","s":"A 65-year-old man is admitted to the hospital for management of <b>Hodgkin lymphoma</b>. He is started on the <b>ABVD</b> chemotherapy regimen, which includes doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine. Which adverse effect may the patient experience due to <b>doxorubicin</b>?","lec":"L6","sl":9,"c":"Slide 9 places doxorubicin among the <b>cytotoxic antibiotics / anthracyclines</b> and names bone-marrow suppression and cardiotoxicity. ABVD is worth memorising as a toxicity map, because the exam asks it letter by letter: <b>A</b>driamycin (doxorubicin) &rarr; heart; <b>B</b>leomycin &rarr; lung; <b>V</b>inblastine &rarr; marrow (and vincristine &rarr; nerve); <b>D</b>acarbazine &rarr; nausea. The general principle to carry: most cytotoxics are limited by myelosuppression, so the ones with a <b>different</b> dose-limiting organ — anthracycline heart, bleomycin lung, cisplatin kidney and ear, vincristine nerve, cyclophosphamide bladder — are the ones worth learning by name.","o":{"A":"Pneumonitis","B":"Hepatic fibrosis","C":"Skin pigmentation","D":"Cardiomyopathy","E":"Secondary ovarian cancer"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Pneumonitis and pulmonary fibrosis are the signature toxicity of the <b>B</b> in ABVD — <b>bleomycin</b>.","B":"Hepatic fibrosis belongs to long-term <b>methotrexate</b>, which is not in this regimen.","C":"Skin and nail hyperpigmentation is a recognised bleomycin effect (with flagellate erythema); it is not doxorubicin's characteristic problem.","D":"Correct. <b>Doxorubicin</b> is an anthracycline, and its dose-limiting toxicity is a <b>dilated cardiomyopathy</b> caused by iron-dependent free-radical injury to cardiac muscle. It is <b>cumulative-dose dependent</b>, which is why a lifetime total is tracked, ejection fraction is monitored, and dexrazoxane is used as a cardioprotectant.","E":"Secondary malignancy after Hodgkin treatment is driven mainly by <b>alkylating agents</b> and radiotherapy — the classic being AML — not by the anthracycline."},"also":"BM30 F2-Q2 — the identical ABVD stem as a past paper; BM31 S2-Q19 and BM30 S2-Q19 take the vinca alkaloid instead","img":"slides_p/L6_9.webp"},{"id":"P|FPF|F22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"FPF","n":"F22","t":"Ketoconazole","s":"A 35-year-old woman receiving <b>ketoconazole</b> for Cushing syndrome develops <b>low testosterone</b> levels. What explains this adverse effect?","lec":"L7","sl":21,"c":"Slide 21 states it directly: ketoconazole in high dose inhibits the synthesis of <b>gonadal and adrenal steroid hormones</b>, is used for Cushing's syndrome, and is watched for hepatic toxicity. Sort the Cushing's drugs by <b>where</b> they act and the whole group becomes easy: <b>synthesis inhibitors</b> — ketoconazole (17-alpha-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase), metyrapone (11-beta-hydroxylase, slide 20), aminoglutethimide (cholesterol &rarr; pregnenolone, slide 24) — versus the <b>receptor antagonist</b> mifepristone. Only the synthesis inhibitors drop androgens as collateral damage, which is exactly what this vignette shows.","o":{"A":"Increased LH secretion","B":"Increased prolactin secretion","C":"Inhibition of steroid hormone synthesis","D":"Glucocorticoid receptor blockade","E":"Aldosterone receptor antagonism"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"More LH would drive the Leydig cell <b>harder</b> and raise testosterone. In practice LH does rise here, as a compensatory response to the low testosterone — it is a consequence, not the cause.","B":"Hyperprolactinaemia can suppress the gonadal axis, but ketoconazole does not raise prolactin. That is the mechanism of dopamine antagonists such as risperidone and metoclopramide.","C":"Correct. In high doses ketoconazole inhibits several <b>cytochrome P450 steroidogenic enzymes</b> — chiefly <b>17-alpha-hydroxylase</b> and <b>17,20-lyase</b> — in both the adrenal cortex and the gonad. That is the therapeutic action in Cushing syndrome (cortisol falls) and the unwanted one in the same breath (androgens and testosterone fall too, causing gynaecomastia and reduced libido).","D":"Receptor blockade at the glucocorticoid receptor is <b>mifepristone</b>. Blocking a receptor does not lower the hormone's concentration; ketoconazole lowers the hormones themselves.","E":"Aldosterone receptor antagonism is <b>spironolactone</b>, which does cause gynaecomastia — but by blocking the androgen receptor, not by blocking synthesis, and it is a different drug."},"also":"BM32 S2-1, BM31 S2-Q4 and BM30 S2-Q16 — Cushing's syndrome treated with the same drug","img":"slides_p/L7_21.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|1-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"1-1","t":"Route of administration","s":"A 65-year-old man with chronic angina feels a sharp chest pain while jogging in a park, which he recognises as an angina attack. Which route should he use for his nitroglycerin?","lec":"L1","sl":14,"c":"<b>Sublingual</b> and <b>rectal</b> are the two enteral routes that partly or wholly escape <b>first-pass metabolism</b>; plain oral does not. Sublingual is the exam's default answer whenever a stem combines <i>rapid onset</i> + <i>out of hospital</i> + <i>a drug with heavy first-pass loss</i> (nitroglycerin is the archetype).","o":{"A":"Intravenous","B":"Subcutaneous","C":"Oral","D":"Transdermal patch","E":"Sublingual"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"IV nitroglycerin exists and is fast, but it is an inpatient route. He is in a park.","B":"Subcutaneous absorption is slow and it is not a route used for nitroglycerin.","C":"Swallowed nitroglycerin is almost completely destroyed by first-pass hepatic metabolism — its oral bioavailability is so poor that the oral route is useless for an acute attack.","D":"A patch is for <b>prophylaxis</b>: transdermal delivery is deliberately slow and prolonged, so onset is far too late for a pain that is happening now.","E":"Correct. Sublingual nitroglycerin is absorbed straight through the sublingual mucosa into systemic circulation, so it <b>bypasses first-pass metabolism</b> and works within a minute or two — which is what an acute attack needs. The lecturer also gave a practical reason when he went over this item: a man out jogging is not carrying an IV line, but he can carry a tablet."},"also":"Ax [58] — 'fastest onset, no first-pass' = sublingual","img":"slides_p/L1_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|1-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"1-2","t":"Dosage forms","s":"A drug must be absorbed in the small intestine and must avoid being degraded by stomach acid. Which solid dosage form should be chosen?","lec":"L1","sl":11,"c":"Solid dosage forms on slide 11: <b>uncoated/film-coated</b> (plain), <b>enteric-coated (EC)</b> = acid-resistant, dissolves in the small intestine, <b>extended-release (ER/XR/SR)</b> = slow release over time, <b>sugar-coated (SCT)</b> = taste/odour masking. Enteric-coated, SR and CR tablets must <b>never be crushed or chewed</b> — a favourite question in its own right.","o":{"A":"Hard gelatin capsule","B":"Sugar-coated tablet","C":"Effervescent tablet","D":"Enteric-coated tablet","E":"Sustained-release tablet"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A hard gelatin shell opens in the stomach. Its advantage is that it needs no disintegration step, so it acts faster than a coated tablet — the opposite of what is wanted here.","B":"A sugar coat masks bitterness and smell. It dissolves in the stomach and gives no acid protection.","C":"An effervescent tablet becomes a <b>solution</b> the moment it hits water — the fastest-releasing form there is, and the least protected from acid.","D":"Correct. An <b>enteric coat</b> is acid-resistant by design: it stays intact at gastric pH and dissolves only once it reaches the higher pH of the small intestine. That is exactly the stem's requirement — protect the drug from acid, release it in the intestine.","E":"Sustained-release controls the <b>rate</b> of release over time. That is a duration property, not an acid-protection property; an SR tablet is not necessarily enteric."},"also":"Ax [55,57] — 'preparation that starts release at the intestine' = enteric-coated","img":"slides_p/L1_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|1-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"1-3","t":"Prescription writing","s":"Three medicines are to be prescribed:<br><b>Medicine A</b> — 30 minutes before breakfast, lunch and dinner<br><b>Medicine B</b> — once daily at bedtime<br><b>Medicine C</b> — twice daily, morning and evening<br>Which set of Latin abbreviations transcribes all three correctly?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"The transcription abbreviations (slide 18, the <i>sig.</i> line): frequency — <b>od</b> once daily, <b>bid</b> twice, <b>tid</b> three times, <b>qid</b> four times, <b>hs</b> at bedtime, <b>prn</b> as needed, <b>stat</b> immediately. Relation to food — <b>ac</b> before, <b>pc</b> after. Route — <b>po</b> by mouth. Quantity — <b># n</b> = number of units dispensed. A '#28' with a bid order means 14 days' supply; that arithmetic is itself examinable.","o":{"A":"A: tid pc · B: hs · C: bid","B":"A: tid ac · B: hs · C: bid","C":"A: qid ac · B: prn · C: tid","D":"A: bid ac · B: od · C: tid","E":"A: tid ac · B: prn · C: qid"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"<b>pc</b> = <i>post cibum</i>, AFTER food. The stem says 30 minutes <i>before</i> meals, so ac is required.","B":"Correct. <b>tid</b> = three times a day (the three meals), <b>ac</b> = <i>ante cibum</i>, before food — together they encode '30 minutes before each meal'. <b>hs</b> = <i>hora somni</i>, at bedtime. <b>bid</b> = twice a day, which is the morning/evening pair.","C":"<b>qid</b> is four times a day, but there are only three meals; and <b>prn</b> means 'as needed', which is not a fixed bedtime dose.","D":"<b>bid</b> is twice daily — it cannot cover three meals. <b>od</b> (once daily) is right in frequency but loses the 'at bedtime' instruction that <b>hs</b> carries.","E":"The first part is right, but <b>prn</b> is 'as needed' rather than every night, and <b>qid</b> is four times daily rather than twice."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q2; BM30 SUM1 Q4 — the same abbreviation-decoding item, three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L1_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-1","t":"Drug interactions","s":"Drug A and drug B are given together. The plasma concentration of drug A is unchanged, but the patient develops severe hypotension. What kind of interaction is this?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"The single most useful discriminator in this whole topic: <b>pharmacokinetic interaction → the plasma concentration moves</b>; <b>pharmacodynamic interaction → the concentration is unchanged but the response changes</b>. Read the stem for whether a level was measured and what it did. Pharmacodynamic combinations then subdivide into addition (1+1=2), synergism (1+1&gt;2), potentiation (1+0&gt;1) and antagonism (1+1&lt;2).","o":{"A":"A pharmacokinetic interaction at excretion","B":"A pharmacokinetic interaction at absorption","C":"A pharmacokinetic interaction at distribution","D":"A pharmacokinetic interaction at metabolism","E":"A pharmacodynamic interaction"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"An excretion interaction (probenecid holding back penicillin) again raises the concentration.","B":"An absorption interaction (e.g. an antacid binding a tetracycline) would change how much drug got in, and therefore the concentration.","C":"A distribution interaction — one drug displacing another from albumin — changes the FREE concentration. It is still a concentration change.","D":"A metabolism interaction (a CYP inducer or inhibitor) is the commonest pharmacokinetic interaction of all, and it works precisely by moving the plasma level.","E":"Correct, and the giveaway is the phrase <b>concentration is unchanged</b>. Pharmacokinetics is what the body does to the drug, and every pharmacokinetic interaction shows up as an altered concentration. If the level is the same but the <i>effect</i> is bigger, the two drugs are interacting at the level of response — pharmacodynamically."},"f":["completed"],"also":"Ax [57] — 'A alters B's action without altering its concentration at the site of action'","img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-2","t":"Volume of distribution","s":"A new anticoagulant acts entirely within the plasma. What volume of distribution would you expect it to have?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"<b>Vd is an apparent volume</b>, not an anatomical one — it is the volume that would be needed to hold the whole dose at the measured plasma concentration (Vd = Dose / C₀). Read it as a location signal: <b>Vd ≤ 5 L → confined to plasma</b>; <b>6–15 L → extracellular fluid</b>; <b>&gt;15 L → mostly in tissue, little in plasma</b>. Vd is also what sets the <b>loading dose</b> (LD = Vd × Css).","o":{"A":"15 L","B":"3 L","C":"40 L","D":"100 L","E":"300 L"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Vd 6–15 L means the drug has spread through the <b>extracellular</b> fluid — plasma plus interstitial fluid. Ibuprofen sits here (0.15 L/kg ≈ 10.5 L). That is more than plasma alone.","B":"Correct. Plasma is about <b>4.8 L</b> in a 70 kg adult (8% of total body water). A drug that never leaves the plasma must therefore have a Vd of roughly that size — 3 L is the only option in the right order of magnitude. Heparin is the textbook example: Vd 0.06 L/kg ≈ 4 L.","C":"About 40 L is total body water — the drug would be distributed through every compartment, intracellular fluid included.","D":"Vd well above total body water means extensive tissue binding, so very little drug is left in plasma — the opposite of the stem.","E":"A very large Vd is the signature of a highly lipophilic, tissue-sequestered drug: fluoxetine is 35 L/kg ≈ 2450 L in a 70 kg person."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q10 (Vd sets the loading dose); BM29 SUM1 Q5 — three cohorts on this one number","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-3","t":"First-order elimination","s":"A patient takes an overdose of a drug that follows first-order elimination. 40 hours later, 97% of the drug has been eliminated. What is the drug's half-life?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"Memorise the ladder: <b>1 t½ = 50%, 2 = 75%, 3 = 87.5%, 4 = 93.75%, 5 = 96.875% (≈ 97%)</b>. '97% eliminated' therefore always means <b>5 half-lives</b>, and the same 5 half-lives is also the time to reach <b>steady state</b> on repeated dosing. Divide the elapsed time by 5 and you have the answer. This item has now appeared with 40 h (→ 8 h) and with 25 h (→ 5 h).","o":{"A":"8 hours","B":"5 hours","C":"2 hours","D":"10 hours","E":"15 hours"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Count the halvings: 50 → 75 → 87.5 → 93.75 → <b>96.875 ≈ 97%</b> eliminated. That is <b>5 half-lives</b>. So t½ = 40 ÷ 5 = <b>8 hours</b>.","B":"5 hours would be the answer if the elapsed time were 25 h (25 ÷ 5 = 5) — which is exactly how BM28 asked the identical item. Check the number in the stem before answering from memory.","C":"2 hours would mean 20 half-lives had passed — essentially every trace of drug would be gone.","D":"10 hours gives 4 half-lives, which is 93.75% eliminated, not 97%.","E":"15 hours gives fewer than 3 half-lives — only about 85% eliminated."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q14 (40 h → 8 h); BM28 SUM1 Q9 (25 h → 5 h) — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-4","t":"Zero-order elimination","s":"A hospital patient's plasma drug concentration is measured hourly. It is found to fall by a fixed, constant amount each hour regardless of how high the level is. Which drug behaves like this?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>First-order</b>: rate ∝ concentration, a constant <i>fraction</i> is removed per unit time, half-life is constant, applies to most drugs at most doses. <b>Zero-order</b>: rate is independent of concentration, a constant <i>amount</i> is removed per unit time, there is no fixed half-life, and a small dose increase can push the level up dramatically. Learn the three examples by name — <b>aspirin, phenytoin, ethanol</b> — because the stem gives you kinetics and asks for the drug.","o":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Propranolol","C":"Phenytoin","D":"Amoxicillin","E":"Atenolol"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Metformin is renally cleared with first-order kinetics.","B":"Propranolol shows first-order kinetics: a constant <i>fraction</i> per hour, so the fall is proportional to the level and the half-life is constant.","C":"Correct. A <b>constant amount per unit time</b> is the definition of <b>zero-order</b> elimination: the metabolising enzymes are saturated, so the rate no longer depends on concentration. Slide 27 names the three classics — <b>aspirin, phenytoin and ethanol</b>.","D":"Amoxicillin is first-order — 'most drugs at most doses', as the slide puts it.","E":"Atenolol is first-order and renally eliminated."},"also":"BM32 SUM 1 recalled this with the 'decreasing by a fixed, constant amount' wording","img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-5","t":"Loading dose","s":"A 60 kg patient is to be given an IV drug, aiming for a plasma concentration of 12 mg/L. The drug's Vd is 0.5 L/kg, its half-life 7 h and its clearance 2.24 L/h. What loading dose is needed?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"The two dose formulas, and what each depends on:<br><b>Loading dose = Vd × Css</b> (÷ F if given orally) — governed by <b>volume of distribution</b>, which is why renal failure leaves the LD unchanged.<br><b>Maintenance dose rate = Css × CL</b> — governed by <b>clearance</b>, which is why renal failure cuts the MD.<br>The examiners routinely hand you every parameter and let you choose wrongly, so decide which formula applies before touching the numbers.","o":{"A":"27 mg","B":"360 mg","C":"144 mg","D":"720 mg","E":"84 mg"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"27 mg is roughly Css × CL (12 × 2.24), which is an infusion RATE in mg/h, not a loading dose.","B":"Correct. <b>LD = Vd × Css</b>. Vd = 0.5 L/kg × 60 kg = <b>30 L</b>; 30 L × 12 mg/L = <b>360 mg</b>. Note that half-life and clearance are decoys — neither belongs in a loading-dose calculation.","C":"144 mg would come from using 12 L instead of 30 L — a units slip on the weight-based Vd.","D":"720 mg is double: it comes from using 1.0 L/kg rather than 0.5 L/kg.","E":"84 mg has no derivation from these numbers; it is close to Css × t½, which is not a formula."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q12 (theophylline, 525 mg); BM29 SUM1 Q11 — three cohorts of the same calculation","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|2-6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"2-6","t":"Tolerance","s":"A 65-year-old man has taken a sleeping pill every night for 2 months. The tablets are gradually losing their usual effect. What is this phenomenon called?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"Slide 17's four look-alikes, separated by <b>time course</b> and <b>subject</b>:<br><b>Tachyphylaxis</b> — rapid, minutes to hours, a few doses.<br><b>Tolerance</b> — gradual, days to weeks, needs dose escalation.<br><b>Resistance</b> — the microbe or tumour, not the patient.<br><b>Intolerance</b> — an exaggerated effect at a small dose.<br>'2 months' is the examiners' signal word for tolerance, and it has been asked with that exact duration in three cohorts.","o":{"A":"Tachyphylaxis","B":"Drug tolerance","C":"Drug resistance","D":"Drug intolerance","E":"Receptor desensitisation"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"<b>Tachyphylaxis</b> is the trap, and it is about SPEED: a rapid, often within-minutes-to-hours fall in response after a few closely spaced doses. It does not describe a two-month decline.","B":"Correct. <b>Tolerance</b> is a gradual loss of effect over repeated dosing across days to weeks, needing a larger dose for the same response. Two months of nightly use is exactly that timescale.","C":"<b>Drug resistance</b> is a property of an organism or a tumour — bacteria stop responding to an antibiotic. A sleeping pill in a human is not a resistance scenario.","D":"<b>Drug intolerance</b> is an abnormally LOW threshold — an ordinary effect appears at an unusually small dose. That is the opposite of what is happening here.","E":"Receptor desensitisation is a plausible <i>mechanism</i> contributing to tolerance, but the question asks what the clinical phenomenon is called."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q24; BM30 SUM1 — 'sleeping pills for 2 months', three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|3-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"3-1","t":"Nuclear receptors","s":"Adapalene acts through a heterodimeric intranuclear receptor. What must such a drug do?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>The single most repeated pharmacodynamics item in this course — five cohorts.</b> The stem names a lipophilic drug (adapalene, dexamethasone, a retinoid, a steroid) and the words <i>intranuclear</i>, <i>nuclear</i> or <i>heterodimeric</i> receptor; the answer is always the option about <b>mRNA / transcription / protein synthesis</b>. Every distractor is a membrane mechanism. Homodimer vs heterodimer does not change the answer — steroid receptors homodimerise, retinoid/thyroid/vitamin-D receptors heterodimerise with RXR, but both regulate transcription.","o":{"A":"Regulate mRNA transcription, and hence protein synthesis","B":"Interact with a Gi-protein-coupled receptor","C":"Open a voltage-gated calcium channel","D":"Activate phospholipase C","E":"Block a potassium channel"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. An intranuclear receptor is a <b>ligand-activated transcription factor</b>. The lipophilic drug crosses the membrane, binds the receptor, the complex dimerises and binds a response element on DNA, and the result is altered <b>mRNA transcription</b> — hence altered protein synthesis. Onset is slow (hours) because new protein has to be made.","B":"A Gi-coupled receptor is a <b>membrane</b> receptor that lowers cAMP. Wrong compartment entirely.","C":"Ion channels are membrane proteins that act in milliseconds. Nuclear receptors act in hours.","D":"Phospholipase C is the effector of Gq-coupled <b>membrane</b> receptors, generating IP₃ and DAG.","E":"Also a membrane ion-channel action, and not how a transcription factor works."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q17; BM30 SUM1 Q17; BM29 SUM1 Q15 (dexamethasone); BM28 SUM1 Q15 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|3-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"3-2","t":"Second messengers","s":"Which mechanism is involved in hepatic glycogenolysis in response to noradrenaline?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"The three G-protein arms, which this whole lecture keeps returning to:<br><b>Gs</b> → adenylyl cyclase <b>↑</b> → cAMP ↑ → PKA (β receptors)<br><b>Gi</b> → adenylyl cyclase <b>↓</b> → cAMP ↓ (α2, M2)<br><b>Gq</b> → phospholipase C → IP₃ + DAG → Ca²⁺ ↑ (α1, M1/M3)<br>Whenever a stem pairs a catecholamine with a metabolic effect, run it through this table.","o":{"A":"Alpha-2 receptor blockade","B":"Elevation of cAMP","C":"Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase","D":"Elevation of cytosolic calcium","E":"Reduction of phospholipase C activity"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Blockade of anything cannot be the mechanism by which an agonist produces an effect. α2 is also Gi-coupled, so blocking it would if anything raise cAMP indirectly — the option is a distractor built out of the right vocabulary in the wrong direction.","B":"Correct. Noradrenaline acting at hepatic <b>β-adrenoceptors</b> couples through <b>Gs</b>, which activates <b>adenylyl cyclase</b> and raises <b>cAMP</b>. cAMP activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates and activates glycogen phosphorylase — glycogen is broken down to glucose.","C":"Inhibiting adenylyl cyclase LOWERS cAMP. That is the Gi pathway, and it would oppose glycogenolysis.","D":"Calcium does contribute through the α1/Gq arm in hepatocytes, but the answer the examiners want — and the one that has been keyed for five cohorts — is the cAMP pathway.","E":"Reducing PLC activity would reduce IP₃ and DAG signalling, again the wrong direction."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q16; BM30 SUM1 Q15; BM28 SUM1 Q14 — asked in five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|3-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"3-3","t":"Dose-response curves","s":"Drug A and drug C act at the same receptor. On a graded log dose-response plot, drug C's curve is shifted to the right of drug A's, in parallel, and reaches the <b>same maximum</b>. What does this tell you?","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"<b>A dose-response figure appears in every single cohort</b>, so make the reading automatic:<br>• <b>Left–right = POTENCY</b> (EC50/ED50). Further left = more potent.<br>• <b>Up–down = EFFICACY</b> (Emax, the ceiling).<br>• <b>Parallel rightward shift, same ceiling</b> = <b>competitive</b> antagonist (surmountable).<br>• <b>Ceiling pushed down</b> = <b>non-competitive/irreversible</b> antagonist (insurmountable).<br>• A curve that plateaus below the others = <b>partial agonist</b>.<br>The original BM32 figure could not be recovered, so the geometry is stated in words here.","o":{"A":"Drug A is a partial agonist and drug C is a full agonist","B":"Drug A and drug C have equal potency","C":"Drug C is more efficacious than drug A","D":"Drug C is an irreversible antagonist","E":"Drug A is more potent than drug C, but the two have equal efficacy"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"A partial agonist by definition cannot reach the full agonist's maximum. Equal maxima rules this out.","B":"Equal potency would put the two curves on top of each other. A rightward shift is precisely a difference in potency.","C":"Efficacy is the height of the plateau. The stem says the maxima are the same, so efficacy is equal.","D":"An irreversible (non-competitive) antagonist <b>lowers the ceiling</b>. Here the ceiling is unchanged, which is the signature of competitive/reversible behaviour, not irreversible.","E":"Correct. Read the two axes separately. <b>Left–right = potency</b>: the curve further left needs less drug for the same effect, so drug A is more potent (lower EC50). <b>Up–down = efficacy</b>: both reach the same ceiling, so Emax — and therefore efficacy — is equal."},"f":["image","completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q15; BM30 SUM1 Q16; BM28 SUM1 Q13; BM30 Formative 1 Q3 — a figure item every year","img":"slides_p/L3_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|3-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"3-4","t":"Severe cutaneous drug reactions","s":"A man with trigeminal neuralgia is prescribed carbamazepine. After one week he develops a flu-like fever, inflamed skin, and detachment of about <b>5%</b> of his body surface. What reaction is this?","lec":"L3B","sl":6,"c":"<b>Detached body surface area is the whole answer:</b> <b>SJS &lt; 10%</b>, <b>SJS/TEN overlap 10–30%</b>, <b>TEN &gt; 30%</b>. A positive <b>Nikolsky sign</b> (skin shears off under lateral pressure) confirms epidermal detachment but does not distinguish the two. Carbamazepine is the drug the examiners use, and its partner item is the <b>HLA-B*15:02</b> screening question before first-time use.","o":{"A":"A type A reaction","B":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)","C":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome","D":"Delayed urticaria","E":"An immediate (type I) hypersensitivity response"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A <b>type A</b> reaction is dose-related, predictable and an extension of the drug's own pharmacology. SJS is <b>type B</b> — bizarre, immunologically mediated, not dose-related.","B":"<b>TEN requires &gt; 30%</b> detachment (10–30% is the SJS/TEN overlap). This is the intended trap — the option is right about the disease family and wrong about the threshold.","C":"Correct, and the number is what decides it. <b>SJS is &lt; 10% body-surface detachment</b>; 5% is SJS. The prodromal flu-like fever a week after starting carbamazepine is the classic history.","D":"Urticaria is wheals; it does not cause epidermal detachment or a positive Nikolsky sign.","E":"A type I reaction is minutes to an hour and gives urticaria, angio-oedema or anaphylaxis — not a one-week delayed blistering illness. SJS/TEN is a delayed, T-cell mediated (type IV) reaction."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q22; BM30 SUM1 Q22; BM28 SUM1 Q19 (with Nikolsky's sign named) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|4-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"4-1","t":"Preclinical studies","s":"During the development of a biopharmaceutical, laboratory and animal studies are performed before any human trial, in order to identify potential risks. What is the main role of these non-clinical studies?","lec":"L4","sl":10,"c":"The pipeline as the examiners test it: <b>Discovery</b> (target, hit, lead optimisation, structure-activity relationship) → <b>Preclinical</b> (in vitro + in vivo toxicity, under GLP; ends with the <b>IND</b> submission) → <b>Phase I</b> healthy volunteers, safety and dose → <b>Phase II</b> patients, efficacy and optimal dose → <b>Phase III</b> large populations, confirmatory efficacy and rarer adverse effects (the most expensive phase) → <b>NDA/FDA approval</b> → <b>Phase IV</b> post-marketing surveillance.","o":{"A":"To support extrapolation of indications","B":"To assess therapeutic superiority over existing treatment","C":"To identify potential toxicities through in vitro and in vivo methods","D":"To confirm the mechanism of action in humans","E":"To provide safety data that make clinical trials unnecessary"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Extrapolation of indications is a <b>biosimilar</b> regulatory concept, argued from the totality of evidence — not a preclinical objective.","B":"Superiority against an active comparator is a <b>Phase III</b> question, and it needs patients.","C":"Correct. Preclinical work exists to answer one question — <b>is it safe enough to put into a human at all?</b> — using cell and animal systems to find toxicity, establish a starting dose and define the therapeutic index. It must be done under <b>GLP</b>.","D":"Anything 'in humans' is by definition not preclinical. Human mechanism is probed from Phase I onward.","E":"Preclinical data <b>permit</b> clinical trials (via the IND); they never replace them. This is the distractor that sounds most reasonable and is most wrong."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q40; BM29 SUM1 Q21-22 (GLP) — the preclinical purpose item recurs every cohort","img":"slides_p/L4_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|4-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"4-2","t":"Research ethics","s":"A doctor invites a patient to join a research study, explains everything about it, and leaves the decision entirely to the patient with no pressure. Which principle of patient rights does this reflect?","lec":"L4","sl":12,"c":"⚠️ <b>Scope note:</b> BM33's L4 is a complete rewrite of BM32's and <b>dropped</b> the ICH-GCP / informed-consent / ethics block. This item is kept because BM32's paper examined it, and because the four principles — <b>autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice</b> — are general medical knowledge you are expected to carry anyway. Weight it as lower priority than the bioequivalence and generic-vs-biosimilar material that BM33 <i>did</i> keep.","o":{"A":"Beneficence","B":"Autonomy","C":"Non-maleficence","D":"Justice","E":"Confidentiality"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"<b>Beneficence</b> is the duty to act in the patient's interest. Relevant to trial design, but the stem is about who makes the decision.","B":"Correct. <b>Autonomy</b> is the patient's right to self-determination, and <b>informed consent</b> is its operational form: full disclosure, understanding, and a <b>voluntary</b> decision free of coercion. The stem stresses all three.","C":"<b>Non-maleficence</b> is 'first, do no harm' — the duty to minimise risk, not the consent process.","D":"<b>Justice</b> concerns fair distribution of the burdens and benefits of research — for instance not recruiting only from a vulnerable group.","E":"Confidentiality matters in research, but nothing in the stem is about protecting data."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM32's L4 block also asked the ICH-GCP E6(R2) vs E6(R3) difference","img":"slides_p/L4_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|5-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"5-1","t":"H2 antagonists","s":"A patient with peptic ulcer is prescribed an H2 antagonist. A week later he is confused, disorientated and dizzy. Which drug is he most likely taking?","lec":"L5","sl":12,"c":"The H2 blockers, by their distinguishing feature: <b>cimetidine</b> — CNS confusion, CYP450 inhibition, gynaecomastia; <b>ranitidine</b> — withdrawn (NDMA); <b>famotidine</b> — the clean one that remains in use; <b>nizatidine</b>. Note also the lecturer's off-slide ranking: for acid suppression a <b>PPI beats an H2 blocker</b>.","o":{"A":"Olopatadine","B":"Famotidine","C":"Ranitidine","D":"Loratadine","E":"Cimetidine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Olopatadine is an H1 antihistamine/mast-cell stabiliser used topically in the eye and nose.","B":"<b>Famotidine is the survivor</b> of the class: more potent, no meaningful CYP inhibition, and far less CNS trouble. It is what is actually prescribed today.","C":"Ranitidine was <b>withdrawn worldwide</b> over NDMA contamination, so it should not be being prescribed at all — a point the lecturer made explicitly.","D":"Loratadine is a second-generation <b>H1</b> antihistamine for allergy. Wrong receptor, and it is specifically non-sedating.","E":"Correct. <b>Cimetidine</b> is the H2 blocker notorious for <b>CNS effects</b> — confusion, disorientation and agitation, especially in the elderly and in renal impairment. It is also a potent <b>CYP450 inhibitor</b> and causes gynaecomastia through anti-androgen activity."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 — the same cimetidine-confusion stem","img":"slides_p/L5_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|5-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"5-2","t":"Vertigo","s":"A woman presents with dizziness and vertigo. She specifically denies tinnitus. Which drug should be used to treat her?","lec":"L5","sl":8,"c":"Sort the dizziness drugs by the picture in the stem:<br>• <b>Vertigo / Ménière's</b> → <b>betahistine</b><br>• <b>Motion sickness</b> → a first-generation H1 antihistamine (dimenhydrinate, cinnarizine, promethazine, CPM), taken <b>30–60 minutes before</b> the journey per the slide<br>• <b>Chemotherapy-induced vomiting</b> → <b>ondansetron</b> (5-HT₃ antagonist)<br>⚠️ In a <b>Parkinson's</b> patient, avoid cinnarizine and promethazine — both are dopamine antagonists and worsen parkinsonism. CPM is the safe choice there.","o":{"A":"Dimetindene","B":"Cetirizine","C":"Betahistine","D":"Betazole","E":"Pheniramine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Dimetindene is a first-generation H1 antihistamine. First-generation agents can blunt motion sickness, but betahistine is the specific answer for vestibular vertigo.","B":"Cetirizine is a second-generation H1 antihistamine for allergic rhinitis and urticaria. It does not treat vertigo.","C":"Correct. <b>Betahistine</b> is the drug for vestibular vertigo and Ménière's disease. It is a <b>dual-action</b> agent — a weak H1 agonist and an H3 <i>antagonist</i> — which increases inner-ear microcirculation and reduces endolymphatic pressure.","D":"<b>Betazole</b> is the trap: it is an H2 <i>agonist</i> used only as a diagnostic test of gastric acid secretion. The name is one letter away from the right answer.","E":"Pheniramine is another first-generation H1 antihistamine, used for allergy."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q48 (Ménière's) — two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|5-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"5-3","t":"Ergot alkaloids","s":"A patient buys an over-the-counter medicine for migraine. He develops cold, blackened fingertips and toes. Which drug caused this?","lec":"L5","sl":14,"c":"The <b>migraine ladder</b>: paracetamol → NSAID → <b>triptan (first-line specific therapy)</b> → <b>ergotamine, rescue only and never for prophylaxis</b>. Two details the lecturer flagged: <b>caffeine is combined with ergotamine because ergotamine is poorly absorbed from the gut</b>, and ergot overuse causes ergotism. The commonest migraine item of all is the plain <i>one-sided headache + photophobia/aura + nausea → <b>sumatriptan</b></i> stem, asked in five cohorts.","o":{"A":"Naproxen","B":"Sumatriptan","C":"Ibuprofen","D":"Paracetamol","E":"Ergotamine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Naproxen is the NSAID with the <b>most reassuring</b> cardiovascular profile — the lecturer's off-slide drug of choice when a patient at cardiovascular risk needs an NSAID.","B":"Sumatriptan is a <b>selective 5-HT₁B/₁D</b> agonist, so its vasoconstriction is largely confined to cranial vessels. It is contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, but it does not cause digital gangrene, and it is prescription-only rather than OTC.","C":"Ibuprofen is a non-selective COX inhibitor. Its problems are GI ulceration, renal impairment and cardiovascular risk — not peripheral vasospasm.","D":"Paracetamol's danger is hepatotoxicity in overdose; it has no vascular action.","E":"Correct. <b>Ergotamine</b> is a non-selective agonist at 5-HT, α-adrenergic and dopamine receptors. Its α-mediated vasoconstriction is intense and long-lasting; with overuse it produces <b>ergotism</b> — peripheral ischaemia, cold, cyanotic and eventually gangrenous digits."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q48 lists ergotamine as a distractor against sumatriptan","img":"slides_p/L5_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-1","t":"Glucocorticoid antagonists","s":"A 50-year-old woman with refractory Cushing syndrome is treated with drug A, which <b>blocks glucocorticoid receptors</b>. What is drug A?","lec":"L7","sl":25,"c":"⚠️ <b>The word RECEPTOR is the entire question.</b> Sort the anti-steroid drugs into two piles:<br><b>Receptor blockers</b> — <b>mifepristone</b> (glucocorticoid receptor); spironolactone/eplerenone (mineralocorticoid receptor).<br><b>Synthesis inhibitors</b> — <b>ketoconazole</b> (17-α-hydroxylase), <b>metyrapone</b> (11-β-hydroxylase), <b>aminoglutethimide</b> (cholesterol → pregnenolone, so it blocks ALL steroid synthesis), etomidate.<br>If the stem says 'blocks the receptor', only mifepristone qualifies.","o":{"A":"Hydrocortisone","B":"Ketoconazole","C":"Metyrapone","D":"Prednisolone","E":"Mifepristone"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Hydrocortisone is cortisol itself — also an agonist.","B":"<b>Ketoconazole</b> inhibits adrenal and gonadal steroid <b>synthesis</b> at the hydroxylases (17-α-hydroxylase). It does treat Cushing's — but not by receptor blockade, which is what the stem specifies.","C":"<b>Metyrapone</b> inhibits <b>11-hydroxylation</b> — again synthesis, not the receptor.","D":"Prednisolone is a steroid <b>agonist</b>. Giving it would make Cushing's worse.","E":"Correct. <b>Mifepristone is the only glucocorticoid RECEPTOR antagonist</b> in the lecture — cortisol is still synthesised normally but cannot act. It is also a strong antiprogestin, which is why the same molecule is used as a contragestive."},"also":"⚠️ asked TWICE in the same BM32 SUM 2 paper — once as 'inhibits glucocorticoid receptors', once as 'refractory Cushing, blocks glucocorticoid receptor'","img":"slides_p/L7_25.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-2","t":"Glucocorticoid mechanism","s":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is treated with prednisolone. What is the mechanism of this drug?","lec":"L5","sl":19,"c":"The arachidonic acid cascade, which links L5 and L7 and is worth drawing once:<br>membrane phospholipid <b>—(phospholipase A2)→</b> arachidonic acid, then either <b>—(COX)→</b> prostaglandins + thromboxane, or <b>—(lipoxygenase)→</b> leukotrienes.<br><b>Steroids block phospholipase A2</b> → both branches fall. <b>NSAIDs block COX</b> → only prostaglandins fall. <b>Zileuton blocks lipoxygenase</b>; <b>montelukast blocks the LT receptor</b>. A stem that says 'reduces arachidonic acid, prostaglandins AND leukotrienes' can only be a steroid.","o":{"A":"11-beta hydroxylase inhibition","B":"COX inhibition","C":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","D":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition","E":"17-alpha hydroxylase inhibition"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"11-beta hydroxylase inhibition is <b>metyrapone</b> — an ANTI-steroid drug, the opposite of what is being given.","B":"COX inhibition is the <b>NSAID</b> mechanism. It blocks only the prostaglandin branch — which is exactly why an NSAID can shunt substrate into the leukotriene branch and precipitate AERD, and a steroid cannot.","C":"Lipoxygenase inhibition is <b>zileuton</b>, used in asthma (and hepatotoxic).","D":"Correct, and this is the <b>single most repeated L7 item — three cohorts</b>. Glucocorticoids induce lipocortin/annexin-1, which inhibits <b>phospholipase A2</b>. Membrane phospholipid is therefore not converted to arachidonic acid, and <b>both</b> prostaglandins and leukotrienes fail to form.","E":"17-alpha hydroxylase inhibition is <b>ketoconazole</b> — also an anti-steroid drug. Both D and E are real drugs from this same lecture, which is what makes the option list hard."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q3; BM30 SUM2 Q13; BM31 SUM1 Q39; BM29 SUM1 Q32; BM30 SUM1 Q50 — five cohorts across both papers, sometimes naming prednisone, prednisolone, or an unnamed herbal knee remedy","img":"slides_p/L5_19.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-3","t":"Inhaled and nasal steroids","s":"A patient with allergic rhinitis is prescribed a nasal corticosteroid. Which drug is most suitable for <b>local</b> anti-inflammatory action?","lec":"L7","sl":6,"c":"The inhaled/nasal list from slide 6 is worth learning as a set: <b>beclomethasone, budesonide, ciclesonide, flunisolide, fluticasone, mometasone, triamcinolone</b>. What they share is high topical potency plus heavy first-pass metabolism, which keeps systemic effect small. Contrast with the systemic ladder on the same slide — short-acting hydrocortisone (1/1) and cortisone (0.8/0.8); intermediate prednisone and prednisolone (4/0.8), methylprednisolone (5/0.5), triamcinolone (5/0); long-acting betamethasone (25/0) and <b>dexamethasone (30/0)</b>; and <b>fludrocortisone (10/125)</b>, the mineralocorticoid.","o":{"A":"Hydrocortisone","B":"Budesonide","C":"Prednisolone","D":"Dexamethasone","E":"Fludrocortisone"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Hydrocortisone is the short-acting <b>reference</b> steroid (potency 1/1), given systemically or topically on skin — not a nasal preparation.","B":"Correct. <b>Budesonide</b> is one of the topical/inhaled steroids — high local potency combined with extensive <b>first-pass metabolism</b> of whatever is swallowed, so systemic exposure stays low. That is precisely the property a nasal steroid needs.","C":"Prednisolone is an oral, intermediate-acting <b>systemic</b> steroid. Using it for rhinitis would mean systemic exposure for a local problem.","D":"Dexamethasone is the most potent glucocorticoid on the table (30/0) and long-acting — used systemically. Giving it nasally would maximise, not minimise, systemic effect.","E":"Fludrocortisone is the <b>mineralocorticoid</b> (10/125) used for Addison's disease. It has almost no role as an anti-inflammatory."},"img":"slides_p/L7_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-4","t":"Antimetabolites","s":"A 35-year-old woman with gout is being treated with a xanthine oxidase inhibitor. Her haematologist wants to start chemotherapy in the next few days. The dose of which chemotherapy drug must be reduced?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11 is the purine-antagonist pathway, and the whole item lives on one arrow: <b>6-mercaptopurine → (xanthine oxidase) → inactive metabolite</b>. Block the enzyme with <b>allopurinol</b> (or febuxostat) and 6-MP accumulates. Note the irony the examiners like: allopurinol is often being given <i>for</i> the tumour-lysis hyperuricaemia that chemotherapy itself causes.","o":{"A":"6-Mercaptopurine","B":"Bleomycin","C":"Mitomycin C","D":"Doxorubicin","E":"Fluorouracil"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>6-Mercaptopurine is inactivated by xanthine oxidase.</b> Allopurinol blocks that enzyme, so 6-MP is not broken down, its level rises steeply, and severe myelosuppression follows unless the dose is cut (classically to about a quarter). This is a <b>pharmacokinetic</b> interaction, exactly as the stem's wording hints.","B":"Bleomycin is inactivated by bleomycin hydrolase, not xanthine oxidase. Its signature toxicity is <b>pulmonary fibrosis</b>.","C":"Mitomycin C is metabolised in the liver; its signature toxicity is <b>nephrotoxicity</b>.","D":"Doxorubicin is metabolised hepatically and excreted in bile. Its signature toxicity is <b>cardiotoxicity</b>, prevented with dexrazoxane.","E":"Fluorouracil is degraded by dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPD) — the enzyme whose deficiency causes severe 5-FU toxicity. Nothing to do with xanthine oxidase."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q25; BM30 SUM2 Q22 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-5","t":"Vinca alkaloids","s":"A 50-year-old man with non-small-cell lung carcinoma is treated with a chemotherapy drug that is a <b>mitotic inhibitor</b>. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>Mitotic inhibitors = vinca alkaloids + taxanes</b>, and the exam repeatedly asks you to tell them apart by mechanism:<br>• <b>Vinca</b> (vincristine, vinblastine) — bind <b>β-tubulin</b>, <b>prevent polymerisation</b>. Vincristine's dose-limiting toxicity is <b>peripheral neuropathy</b>; vinblastine's is myelosuppression.<br>• <b>Taxanes</b> (paclitaxel, docetaxel) — bind <b>α,β-tubulin</b> and <b>prevent DEpolymerisation</b>, freezing the spindle assembled.<br>Mnemonic from the lecture: <b>vinCristine → Childhood tumours; vinBlastine → Breast carcinoma.</b>","o":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Vincristine","D":"Carmustine","E":"Gemcitabine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cisplatin is a <b>platinum alkylating-like</b> agent forming DNA cross-links. Cell-cycle non-specific.","B":"Methotrexate is an <b>antimetabolite</b> — a folate antagonist blocking dihydrofolate reductase, so it acts in S phase, not M.","C":"Correct. The <b>vinca alkaloids</b> (vincristine, vinblastine) bind β-tubulin and prevent microtubule <b>polymerisation</b>, arresting the cell in metaphase — the definition of a mitotic inhibitor. The other mitotic inhibitors are the taxanes.","D":"Carmustine is a <b>nitrosourea</b> alkylating agent, lipid-soluble enough to cross into the CNS.","E":"Gemcitabine is a pyrimidine <b>antimetabolite</b>."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q19; BM30 SUM2 Q19 — both used Hodgkin's disease rather than lung carcinoma","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-6","t":"Taxanes","s":"A woman being treated for breast cancer develops numbness and tingling in her hands and feet. Which drug most likely caused this adverse effect?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>Learn one signature toxicity per drug — the examiners build entire option lists out of them:</b> <b>cyclophosphamide</b> → haemorrhagic cystitis (mesna); <b>cisplatin</b> → nephrotoxicity + ototoxicity (amifostine); <b>doxorubicin</b> → cardiotoxicity (dexrazoxane); <b>bleomycin</b> → pulmonary fibrosis; <b>mitomycin C</b> → nephrotoxicity; <b>vincristine</b> → peripheral neuropathy; <b>taxanes</b> → peripheral neuropathy; <b>methotrexate</b> → mucositis and myelosuppression (folinic acid rescue).","o":{"A":"Trastuzumab","B":"Doxorubicin","C":"Cyclophosphamide","D":"Tamoxifen","E":"Docetaxel"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Trastuzumab is an anti-HER2 antibody, and its notable toxicity is <b>cardiomyopathy</b> — which is why it is not combined with an anthracycline.","B":"Doxorubicin's signature toxicity is <b>cardiotoxicity</b> (irreversible, cumulative-dose related, mitigated by dexrazoxane), not neuropathy.","C":"Cyclophosphamide's signature toxicity is <b>haemorrhagic cystitis</b> from acrolein, prevented by <b>mesna</b>.","D":"Tamoxifen is a SERM. Its notable risks are endometrial carcinoma and thromboembolism.","E":"Correct. <b>Docetaxel</b> is a taxane, and taxanes cause a dose-limiting, stocking-and-glove <b>peripheral sensory neuropathy</b> — numbness and tingling of hands and feet. Taxanes are front-line in advanced breast cancer, so the clinical setting fits."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q18; BM30 SUM2 Q18 — three cohorts, always breast cancer + numbness and tingling","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM32|S2-7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM32","n":"S2-7","t":"Taxane mechanism","s":"A drug forms a complex with <b>α,β-tubulin</b>, inhibiting microtubule <b>depolymerisation</b> and therefore mitosis. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"⚠️ <b>A handout/video conflict worth knowing:</b> the recorded lecture simplifies both vinca alkaloids and taxanes to 'bind β-tubulin', but the <b>handout</b> — and every past paper — say taxanes bind <b>α,β-tubulin</b>. <b>Answer with the handout.</b> The pairing to memorise: <b>vinca = β-tubulin, blocks POLYmerisation</b>; <b>taxane = α,β-tubulin, blocks DEpolymerisation</b>.","o":{"A":"Topotecan","B":"Vincristine","C":"Paclitaxel","D":"5-Fluorouracil","E":"6-Mercaptopurine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Topotecan is a camptothecin, a <b>topoisomerase I</b> inhibitor. Nothing to do with tubulin.","B":"Vincristine is the mirror image: it binds <b>β-tubulin</b> and blocks <b>polymerisation</b>, so the spindle never forms. Direction of the error matters — this is the intended trap.","C":"Correct. <b>Taxanes bind α,β-tubulin and stabilise the microtubule</b>, blocking the <b>depolymerisation</b> that mitosis requires. The spindle is frozen in the assembled state and the cell cannot complete division.","D":"5-FU is a pyrimidine antimetabolite that inhibits thymidylate synthase.","E":"6-MP is a purine antimetabolite."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q21; BM30 SUM2 Q21 — three cohorts, the wording is near-identical each time","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q1","t":"NLEM","s":"A patient cannot tolerate the adverse effects of a first-line antihypertensive that is listed in <b>บัญชี ก</b> of the Thai National List of Essential Medicines. Manidipine is used instead. Which NLEM sub-list does manidipine belong to?","lec":"L1","sl":5,"c":"The five NLEM sub-lists, in ascending order of restriction: <b>ก</b> basic and unrestricted · <b>ข</b> the substitute when ก cannot be used, with the reason documented · <b>ค</b> needs expertise, dangerous if misused · <b>ง</b> very expensive / high-risk / complex conditions · <b>จ</b> special category, specialist + protocol + audit + PTC approval, split into จ(1) special projects and จ(2) named patients. Every stem hands you the <i>reason</i> for the choice — match the reason to the list.","o":{"A":"บัญชี ค","B":"บัญชี ก","C":"บัญชี ข","D":"บัญชี ง","E":"บัญชี จ"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"<b>บัญชี ค</b> needs a specialist or particular expertise, because incorrect use is dangerous (the classic example being antibiotics whose misuse breeds resistance).","B":"<b>บัญชี ก</b> is the basic essential list: common diseases, proven effective, safe and cost-effective. Any doctor may prescribe these at any level of facility — which is what the patient was on and could not tolerate.","C":"Correct. <b>บัญชี ข</b> is defined as the list of drugs used <i>in place of</i> a บัญชี ก drug when there is a specific clinical reason — the patient is allergic to it, has had a serious adverse effect, or has failed to respond. The prescriber must <b>record the indication in the notes</b>. The stem describes that situation exactly.","D":"<b>บัญชี ง</b> is for very expensive drugs, or drugs with high adverse-effect risk or complex conditions of use, that are nonetheless necessary for certain diseases.","E":"<b>บัญชี จ</b> is the special category — จ(1) for government special projects, จ(2) for named individual patients, both requiring a specialist prescriber, a strict protocol and PTC approval."},"also":"Ax [59] — antibiotic categorised as ค because misuse breeds resistance","img":"slides_p/L1_5.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q2","t":"Prescription writing","s":"A drug is to be taken <b>four times a day on an empty stomach for 7 days</b>, one 500 mg capsule per dose. Which transcription is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"The dispensed quantity is arithmetic the examiners genuinely test: <b># = doses per day × number of days</b>. qid × 7 days = 28; bid × 14 days = 28 as well, which is why they like the number. Pair that with the two food abbreviations — <b>ac</b> before, <b>pc</b> after — and the bedtime marker <b>hs</b>.","o":{"A":"A (500 mg) 1 cap P.O. tid ac #28","B":"A (500 mg) 1 cap P.O. qid pc #28","C":"A (500 mg) 1 cap P.O. qid ac #7","D":"A (500 mg) 1 cap P.O. qid ac #28","E":"A (500 mg) 1 cap P.O. bid ac #7"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"<b>tid</b> is three times daily, not four. The quantity would then also be wrong (21, not 28).","B":"<b>pc</b> is <i>post cibum</i> — after food. That is the opposite of an empty stomach.","C":"Frequency and timing are right, but <b>#7</b> would dispense only 7 capsules — under two days' supply for a 7-day course.","D":"Correct on all three counts. <b>qid</b> = four times daily. <b>ac</b> (<i>ante cibum</i>) = before food, which is what 'on an empty stomach' requires. And <b># 28</b> is the quantity: 4 capsules/day × 7 days = <b>28</b>.","E":"<b>bid</b> is twice daily and #7 is under four days' supply. Both wrong."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM32 SUM1 L1; BM30 SUM1 Q4 — an abbreviation item in three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L1_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q4","t":"First-pass metabolism","s":"Which route of drug administration carries the highest chance of extensive first-pass metabolism?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11's bioavailability table is worth memorising as a ranking: <b>IV 100% (by definition)</b> · IM ≥ 75% · SC ≥ 75% · <b>oral 5–100% — first pass may be important</b> · rectal 30–100% (<i>less</i> first pass than oral) · inhalation 5–100%, often very rapid onset · transdermal 80–100%, very slow, used precisely to avoid first pass. The routes that dodge first pass are <b>IV, IM, SC, sublingual, inhaled, transdermal</b>, and <b>rectal partially</b>.","o":{"A":"Oral","B":"Sublingual","C":"Inhalation","D":"Intramuscular","E":"Intravenous"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A swallowed drug is absorbed from the gut into the <b>portal vein</b> and must traverse the liver before it ever reaches the systemic circulation. Gut wall and hepatic enzymes can remove a large fraction — which is why oral bioavailability ranges from 5% to 100%.","B":"Sublingual mucosa drains into the systemic veins, so it <b>bypasses</b> the portal circulation and escapes first pass.","C":"Inhaled drug is absorbed across the alveolar membrane straight into the pulmonary veins — no portal circulation involved, and onset is very rapid.","D":"IM injection drains into systemic veins. Bioavailability is ≥ 75%.","E":"IV is <b>100% bioavailable by definition</b> — the drug is placed directly into the systemic circulation, so first-pass metabolism cannot occur."},"also":"BM31 Q8 asks the same idea inverted — 'which bypasses first pass' → intravenous","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q8","t":"First-pass metabolism","s":"Which of the following bypasses first-pass metabolism entirely?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"Careful with the two different questions the examiners ask about oral dosage forms. <b>Speed of release</b> — effervescent (already a solution) &gt; solution &gt; suspension &gt; capsule &gt; uncoated tablet &gt; coated tablet. <b>First-pass exposure</b> — identical for all of them, because they all go down the same route. Changing the <i>form</i> changes the rate; only changing the <i>route</i> changes first pass.","o":{"A":"Capsule","B":"Tablet","C":"Oral solution","D":"Intravenous injection","E":"Suspension"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A capsule is also swallowed. Capsules act faster than coated tablets because they need no disintegration — but first pass is unavoidable.","B":"A tablet is swallowed — portal vein, then liver. First pass applies.","C":"An oral solution is absorbed <b>faster</b> than a tablet, because no disintegration or dissolution step is needed. But 'faster' is not 'bypasses': it still goes through the portal circulation.","D":"Correct. IV places the drug directly into the systemic circulation, so it never passes through the liver before reaching its target. Bioavailability is 100% by definition.","E":"A suspension is an oral liquid; same portal route."},"also":"BM31 Q4 — the same concept asked the other way round, in the same paper","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q5","t":"Drug metabolism","s":"Which enzyme complex is most commonly involved in Phase I drug metabolism?","lec":"L2","sl":19,"c":"<b>Phase I</b> — oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis; mostly <b>CYP450</b>; adds or unmasks a polar group; the product may be active, inactive or <b>toxic</b>. <b>Phase II</b> — conjugation with glucuronate, sulfate, acetate, glycine or glutathione; almost always inactivating; makes the molecule water-soluble for excretion. A Phase II step can occur without a Phase I step. The <b>CYP3A4/3A5</b> pair metabolises drugs from nearly every class and is the one most often named in interaction questions.","o":{"A":"Sulfotransferases","B":"Cytochrome P450","C":"N-acetyltransferases","D":"Glutathione S-transferases","E":"UDP-glucuronosyltransferases"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Sulfotransferases catalyse <b>sulfation</b> — a Phase II conjugation.","B":"Correct. <b>Phase I = oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis</b>, and the great majority of it is carried out by the <b>cytochrome P450</b> mono-oxygenase family in the hepatic smooth endoplasmic reticulum. Phase I introduces or exposes a polar group.","C":"N-acetyltransferases catalyse <b>acetylation</b> — Phase II, and the source of the fast/slow acetylator polymorphism.","D":"Glutathione S-transferases catalyse <b>glutathione conjugation</b> — Phase II, and the pathway that detoxifies NAPQI in paracetamol metabolism.","E":"UGTs catalyse <b>glucuronidation</b> — the commonest Phase II reaction of all, but still Phase II."},"also":"Ax [58] — 'which reaction is Phase I' → reduction","img":"slides_p/L2_19.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q6","t":"Half-life","s":"What does the term <b>half-life</b> refer to in pharmacokinetics?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"Get the vocabulary of the concentration-time curve straight (slide 10): <b>Cmax</b> peak level · <b>Tmax</b> time to that peak · <b>MEC</b> minimum effective concentration · <b>MTC</b> minimum toxic concentration · <b>onset</b> when the curve crosses MEC · <b>duration</b> the time above MEC · <b>therapeutic range/window</b> the gap between MEC and MTC · <b>AUC</b> total exposure. Half-life belongs to elimination, and drives both dosing interval and time to steady state.","o":{"A":"The time required for the drug's plasma concentration to fall by 50%","B":"The time required for complete elimination of the drug from the body","C":"The time required for the drug to reach its maximum concentration","D":"The time between administration and the onset of action","E":"The duration of the drug's therapeutic effect"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. t½ is the time for the plasma concentration to fall to <b>half</b> its value. In first-order kinetics it is a constant, independent of the starting concentration.","B":"Complete elimination is asymptotic — mathematically it never finishes. In practice about 5 half-lives (≈ 97%) is taken as effectively complete.","C":"That is <b>Tmax</b>, the time to peak concentration.","D":"That is <b>onset</b>, which depends on how quickly the level crosses the MEC.","E":"That is <b>duration of action</b> — the time spent above the MEC. It depends on dose as well as half-life."},"also":"BM31 Q14 asks the clinical significance of the same parameter","img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q7","t":"AUC","s":"What does <b>AUC</b> stand for in pharmacokinetics?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"<b>AUC</b> is the bridge between L2 and L4. In L2 it gives clearance and bioavailability; in L4 it is one of the three <b>bioequivalence</b> parameters alongside <b>Cmax</b> and <b>Tmax</b>, and a generic qualifies when the <b>90% confidence interval</b> for the AUC and Cmax ratios lies within <b>80–125%</b>. Those two numbers are the ones prof SN said were worth memorising.","o":{"A":"Average urinary concentration","B":"Apparent uptake constant","C":"Area under the curve","D":"Adjusted utilisation coefficient","E":"Augmented unbound clearance"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Not a pharmacokinetic term.","B":"Not a pharmacokinetic term.","C":"Correct. The <b>area under the plasma concentration-time curve</b> measures the body's total exposure to the drug. It is what bioavailability and bioequivalence are computed from: F = AUC(oral)/AUC(IV), and CL = Dose(IV)/AUC(IV).","D":"Not a pharmacokinetic term.","E":"Not a pharmacokinetic term — invented from real vocabulary."},"img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q10","t":"Loading dose","s":"Which pharmacokinetic parameter is most important in determining the <b>loading dose</b> of a drug?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"This pairing is examined every year, most often through the renal-failure vignette:<br><b>Loading dose ← Vd.</b> Renal failure does not change Vd, so the <b>LD is unchanged</b>.<br><b>Maintenance dose ← clearance.</b> Renal failure lowers clearance, so the <b>MD is decreased</b>.<br>The mirror-image case: <b>hypoalbuminaemia</b> raises the unbound fraction → Vd rises → <b>LD increased, MD unchanged</b>.","o":{"A":"Clearance","B":"Bioavailability","C":"Maximum concentration","D":"Half-life","E":"Volume of distribution"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Clearance governs the <b>maintenance</b> dose rate (MD rate = Css × CL), not the loading dose.","B":"Bioavailability enters only as a correction when the loading dose is given by a non-IV route (LD = Vd × Css / F). It is not the governing parameter.","C":"Cmax is an observed outcome, not a design parameter.","D":"Half-life determines how long steady state takes to arrive and helps set the dosing interval — it is irrelevant to the size of a loading dose. The whole point of a loading dose is to <i>skip</i> the half-life-determined wait.","E":"Correct. <b>LD = Vd × Css</b>. The loading dose exists to fill the volume the drug will occupy, so it is set by the <b>volume of distribution</b> and by nothing else in the steady state."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q11 (slow metaboliser: LD unchanged, MD decreased); Ax [55,56] — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q11","t":"Absorption","s":"Which factor most affects the absorption of an orally administered drug?","lec":"L2","sl":6,"c":"Keep the ADME steps and their governing properties separate, because option lists are built by mixing them: <b>Absorption</b> ← lipid solubility, molecular size, ionisation (pH/pKa), dosage form, route, first pass. <b>Distribution</b> ← blood flow, Vd, special barriers (BBB, placenta), plasma protein binding. <b>Metabolism</b> ← CYP450, inducers and inhibitors, genetics, age, liver disease. <b>Excretion</b> ← renal function, urine pH, biliary/enterohepatic recycling.","o":{"A":"The drug's lipid solubility","B":"The drug's volume of distribution","C":"The drug's plasma protein binding","D":"The drug's elimination half-life","E":"The drug's renal clearance"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Absorption across the gut epithelium is mostly <b>passive diffusion</b>, and only the <b>un-ionised, lipid-soluble</b> fraction can cross a lipid bilayer. Lipid:water partition coefficient and molecular size are the physicochemical properties slide 6 names.","B":"Vd describes where the drug goes <i>after</i> it has been absorbed and reached the circulation.","C":"Protein binding is a <b>distribution</b> property — it determines the free fraction in plasma.","D":"Half-life is an <b>elimination</b> property.","E":"Renal clearance is an <b>excretion</b> property."},"img":"slides_p/L2_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q13","t":"Clearance","s":"Which pharmacokinetic term describes the <b>elimination</b> of drug from the body?","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"Slide 28 gives clearance four ways, and it is worth being able to move between them: <b>CL = Dose(IV)/AUC(IV)</b> · <b>CL = F × Dose(PO)/AUC(PO)</b> · <b>CL = Ke × Vd</b> · <b>CL = 0.693 × Vd / t½</b>. The last rearranges to the relationship that catches people out: <b>half-life rises if Vd rises OR clearance falls</b> — it is not a pure measure of elimination.","o":{"A":"Volume of distribution","B":"Half-life","C":"Bioavailability","D":"Clearance","E":"Maximum concentration"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Vd is a <b>distribution</b> parameter.","B":"Half-life is a <b>derived</b> quantity — it depends on both clearance and volume (t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL). A change in half-life could come from either, so it is not the term for elimination itself.","C":"Bioavailability is an <b>absorption</b> parameter — the fraction of an administered dose that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged.","D":"Correct. <b>Clearance</b> is the volume of plasma <i>completely cleared</i> of drug per unit time. It is the direct measure of the body's capacity to eliminate, and it is the parameter that sets the maintenance dose.","E":"Cmax is an observed peak on the concentration-time curve."},"img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q14","t":"Half-life","s":"What is the clinical significance of a drug's elimination half-life?","lec":"L2","sl":29,"c":"The two things half-life buys you: <b>time to steady state ≈ 4–5 × t½</b> — a fact that is independent of the dose, and the reason a loading dose is needed when you cannot wait — and the <b>dosing interval</b>. Slide 29's table shows the accumulation explicitly: dosing every t½ builds to about 200% of a single dose, arriving within a few percent of that by the fifth interval.","o":{"A":"It sets the therapeutic index","B":"It affects the rate of absorption","C":"It determines bioavailability","D":"It alters the volume of distribution","E":"It determines the dosing interval and the time to reach steady state"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The therapeutic index is LD50/ED50 (or TD50/ED50), a toxicity ratio, independent of half-life.","B":"Absorption rate is governed by the route and formulation, not by elimination.","C":"Bioavailability is set by absorption and first-pass loss.","D":"The causal arrow runs the other way — Vd is one of the two determinants <i>of</i> half-life.","E":"Correct, and both halves matter. The <b>dosing interval</b> is chosen around the half-life so the level stays inside the therapeutic window, and <b>steady state takes about 4–5 half-lives</b> to arrive regardless of dose or interval."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q12; BM28 SUM1 Q12 — 'when is steady state reached' asked as a calculation","img":"slides_p/L2_29.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q15","t":"Dose-response curves","s":"A graded log dose-response plot shows three drugs acting at the same receptor. Drug A and drug C reach the <b>same maximum response</b>, but drug C's curve lies to the right of drug A's. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11 states the reading rule with the lecturer's own blanks filled in: for four curves of the same class, 'the most potent agent is <b>Drug A</b>', 'Drug D is more potent than <b>C and B</b>', 'D is a <b>full</b> agonist', 'A and B are <b>partial</b> agonists'. Translated: <b>horizontal = potency/affinity, vertical = efficacy</b>. Everything the figure items ask reduces to those two axes.","o":{"A":"Drug C is a partial agonist","B":"Drugs A and C have equal potency","C":"Drug C is an irreversible antagonist","D":"Drugs A and C have the same affinity","E":"Drugs A and C have equal efficacy"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"A partial agonist cannot reach the full agonist's maximum. Equal maxima excludes this.","B":"Potency is the horizontal position (EC50/ED50). A rightward shift means drug C is <b>less</b> potent, so the potencies are not equal.","C":"An irreversible antagonist depresses the ceiling. The ceiling here is unchanged.","D":"Affinity is what puts a curve left or right — a lower EC50 reflects higher affinity. Different positions therefore mean different affinities.","E":"Correct. <b>Efficacy is the height of the plateau</b> (Emax). Equal maxima means equal efficacy, whatever the horizontal positions are."},"f":["image"],"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q16; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM28 SUM1 Q13 — a figure item in every cohort","img":"slides_p/L3_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q16","t":"Second messengers","s":"Which mechanism is involved in glycogenolysis in response to noradrenaline?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"<b>Gs raises cAMP · Gi lowers cAMP · Gq raises IP₃/DAG and therefore calcium.</b> β1/β2/β3 and D1 are Gs; α2, M2 and D2 are Gi; α1, M1/M3, H1 and 5-HT₂ are Gq. Almost every second-messenger item in this course is answerable from that one table.","o":{"A":"Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase","B":"Alpha-2 receptor blockade","C":"Elevation of cAMP","D":"Elevation of cytosolic calcium","E":"Reduction of phospholipase C activity"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Inhibiting adenylyl cyclase lowers cAMP — the Gi pathway, the wrong direction.","B":"Receptor blockade is not a mechanism by which an agonist acts.","C":"Correct. β-adrenoceptor → <b>Gs</b> → adenylyl cyclase → <b>cAMP ↑</b> → protein kinase A → glycogen phosphorylase activated → glycogenolysis.","D":"Calcium rises via the α1/Gq arm and does contribute in hepatocytes, but the keyed answer across five cohorts is the cAMP pathway.","E":"Reducing PLC activity would suppress IP₃/DAG signalling — again the wrong direction."},"also":"BM32 SUM1; BM30 SUM1 Q15; BM28 SUM1 Q14 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q17","t":"Nuclear receptors","s":"Adapalene, a drug that acts via <b>intranuclear</b> receptors, must have which of the following characteristics?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>Five cohorts have asked this — it is the single most repeated pharmacodynamics item.</b> The trigger words are <i>intranuclear</i>, <i>nuclear</i> or <i>heterodimeric</i> receptor, and the drug named is always lipophilic (adapalene, dexamethasone, a retinoid or a steroid). The answer is always the transcription option; every distractor is a membrane mechanism.","o":{"A":"Interacts with a Gi-protein-coupled receptor","B":"Inhibits K⁺ channels","C":"Activates Ca²⁺ channels","D":"Activates phospholipase C","E":"Regulates mRNA"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Gi-coupled receptors are <b>membrane</b> receptors that lower cAMP.","B":"Ion-channel block is a membrane action operating in milliseconds.","C":"Also a membrane ion-channel action.","D":"Phospholipase C is the effector of Gq-coupled <b>membrane</b> receptors.","E":"Correct. Intranuclear receptors are <b>ligand-activated transcription factors</b>: the complex binds a response element on DNA and <b>regulates mRNA transcription</b>. Onset is slow because new protein must be synthesised."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L3; BM30 SUM1 Q17; BM29 SUM1 Q15; BM28 SUM1 Q15 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q18","t":"Antagonism","s":"Drug A lowers blood pressure. Drug B raises blood pressure. The two bind to <b>different</b> receptors. What kind of interaction occurs when they are given together?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ The professor flagged <b>functional vs pharmacological antagonism</b> as a National Licensing Exam favourite. The four kinds, separated by <i>where</i> they act:<br><b>Pharmacological</b> — same receptor, competitive or irreversible.<br><b>Physiological / functional</b> — different receptors, opposite effects on the same endpoint.<br><b>Chemical</b> — the two molecules react with each other; no receptor.<br><b>Physical/pharmacokinetic</b> — one alters the other's absorption or disposition.<br>Note that BM31's key says 'physiological' and BM28's says 'functional' — <b>they are the same answer</b>, so take whichever appears.","o":{"A":"Pharmacological antagonism","B":"Chemical antagonism","C":"Physiological antagonism","D":"Physiological potentiation","E":"Pharmacological synergism"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"<b>Pharmacological antagonism</b> is competition at the <b>same</b> receptor — agonist versus antagonist. The stem explicitly excludes it.","B":"<b>Chemical antagonism</b> is a direct chemical reaction between the two molecules, so that one is inactivated in solution — protamine neutralising heparin, or a chelator binding a metal. No receptor is involved at all.","C":"Correct. <b>Physiological (= functional) antagonism</b> is two drugs producing opposite effects on the same physiological variable while acting through <b>different receptors</b>. The phrase 'different receptors' in the stem is the whole decision.","D":"Potentiation is 1 + 0 &gt; 1: an inactive drug amplifies an active one. Both drugs here are active, and in opposite directions.","E":"Synergism is 1 + 1 &gt; 2 — effects in the <b>same</b> direction, reinforcing each other."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q16 (keyed 'functional'); BM29 SUM1 Q16; Ax [57] (epinephrine reversing histamine bronchoconstriction) — three cohorts plus the Ax bank","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q19","t":"Pharmacogenomics","s":"In which of the following should the <b>HLA-B*15:02</b> gene be tested?","lec":"L3B","sl":8,"c":"The two pairings to keep straight — the option lists are built by swapping them:<br><b>Carbamazepine → HLA-B*15:02</b> → SJS/TEN. Screen <b>before the first dose</b>.<br><b>Allopurinol → HLA-B*58:01</b> → SJS/TEN and DRESS.<br>(<b>Abacavir → HLA-B*57:01</b> → hypersensitivity syndrome, on the same table.)<br>The word <b>'first time'</b> in a stem is the marker that pharmacogenomic screening is the point.","o":{"A":"A patient with trigeminal neuralgia who is about to be treated with allopurinol","B":"A patient with hyperuricaemia during chemotherapy","C":"A gout patient who has tested positive for HLA-B*15:02 for 6 months","D":"A patient with neuropathic pain who is about to start carbamazepine for the first time","E":"A patient with epilepsy who is HLA-B*58:01 positive"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"This option deliberately crosses the wires: <b>allopurinol</b> pairs with <b>HLA-B*58:01</b>, not B*15:02, and trigeminal neuralgia is the carbamazepine indication. Two swaps in one option.","B":"Hyperuricaemia during chemotherapy is tumour lysis syndrome — managed with allopurinol and hydration. No HLA typing involved.","C":"Testing someone who has already been tested, and who is not about to receive carbamazepine, achieves nothing. The result does not change over time.","D":"Correct. <b>HLA-B*15:02 predicts carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN</b>, and the test is only useful <b>before the first exposure</b>. The allele is common in South-East Asian populations, which is why screening is recommended in Thailand.","E":"B*58:01 is the allopurinol allele. It says nothing about carbamazepine risk."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q19 — word-for-word the same item, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q20","t":"Receptor regulation","s":"Which of the following <b>up-regulates</b> postsynaptic α1-adrenergic receptors?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Receptors move in the direction opposite to their stimulation.</b><br><b>Chronic AGONIST → down-regulation</b> (and tolerance).<br><b>Chronic ANTAGONIST → up-regulation</b> — which is why abruptly stopping a long-term β-blocker causes rebound tachycardia and angina.<br><b>Hyperthyroidism → up-regulation</b> of adrenergic receptors (slide 18's cardiac hypertrophy figure).<br>BM29 and BM28 asked the same idea about <b>β1</b>: chronic use of a β1 <i>antagonist</i> up-regulates β1.","o":{"A":"Internalisation of the α1 receptor","B":"Chronic use of an α1 agonist","C":"A single dose of an α1 agonist","D":"Hyperthyroidism","E":"Chronic use of an α2 antagonist"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Internalisation is one of the <i>mechanisms</i> of down-regulation, so it lowers surface receptors.","B":"Chronic <b>agonist</b> exposure causes <b>DOWN</b>-regulation — the cell defends itself against persistent stimulation. The rule is that receptors move opposite to their stimulation.","C":"A single dose produces no lasting change in receptor number.","D":"Correct. <b>Hyperthyroidism up-regulates adrenergic receptors</b> — thyroid hormone increases their expression, which is why thyrotoxic patients are tachycardic, tremulous and heat-intolerant, and why a β-blocker gives such rapid symptomatic relief.","E":"An α2 antagonist increases noradrenaline release (α2 is the inhibitory autoreceptor), so the postsynaptic receptor sees <i>more</i> agonist — and therefore down-regulates."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q20 (α1, identical); BM29 SUM1 Q17 and BM28 SUM1 Q17 (β1 version) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q21","t":"Moringa oleifera","s":"A recent paper reported that <b>Moringa oleifera</b> leaf extract enhances the endothelial <b>H₂S</b> synthesis pathway, producing vasorelaxation. Which substance contributes to this effect?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"⚠️ <b>The professor examines his own published research</b> — Moringa items have now appeared in <b>four cohorts</b>. There are two versions and they have different answers:<br><b>H₂S version</b> (BM31, BM30) → the enzyme <b>cystathionine-γ-lyase</b>.<br><b>NO version</b> (BM29, BM28) → the second messenger <b>cyclic GMP</b>.<br>Slide 15 is his gasotransmitter slide: the three gaseous signalling molecules are <b>NO</b> (NOS → sGC → cGMP), <b>H₂S</b> (CSE) and <b>CO</b> (haem oxygenase). Identify the gas first, then pick from its own pathway.","o":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"L-arginine","C":"Cystathionine-γ-lyase","D":"Nitric oxide synthase","E":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase makes cAMP — the β-adrenergic/prostacyclin arm of vasodilation.","B":"L-arginine is the <b>substrate for nitric oxide</b> synthesis, not H₂S.","C":"Correct. <b>Cystathionine-γ-lyase (CSE)</b> is the enzyme that generates <b>hydrogen sulfide</b> in vascular endothelium. The stem says H₂S pathway, so CSE is the only option in that pathway.","D":"<b>Nitric oxide synthase</b> is the NO pathway. This is the deliberate trap: the same research group published a <b>NO</b> version of this study, and BM29 and BM28 asked <i>that</i> one, where the answer is <b>cyclic GMP</b>. Read which gas the stem names.","E":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase is NO's downstream effector, producing cGMP — again the NO pathway."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q21 (H₂S, identical); BM29 SUM1 Q18 and BM28 SUM1 Q18 (NO → cGMP) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q22","t":"Severe cutaneous drug reactions","s":"A man with trigeminal neuralgia was prescribed carbamazepine. After one week he developed a flu-like fever, inflammation of the skin, and detachment of 5% of his total skin surface. What type of reaction is this?","lec":"L3B","sl":6,"c":"The severe cutaneous adverse reaction family, with the features the exam uses: <b>SJS</b> &lt; 10% BSA · <b>SJS/TEN overlap</b> 10–30% · <b>TEN</b> &gt; 30%, mortality far higher · <b>DRESS</b> — rash with <b>eosinophilia</b>, fever and organ involvement, onset 2–6 weeks, the longest latency · <b>AGEP</b> — sterile pustules, onset within 1–2 days, resolves quickly. Match the latency and the morphology, not just the drug.","o":{"A":"A type A reaction","B":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)","C":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome","D":"Delayed urticaria","E":"An immediate hypersensitivity response"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Type A reactions are dose-related, predictable extensions of the drug's pharmacology. SJS is type B — bizarre, immune-mediated and not dose-related.","B":"TEN requires <b>&gt; 30%</b>; 10–30% is the overlap zone. The number in the stem is doing all the work.","C":"Correct — <b>5% detachment is below the 10% threshold, so this is SJS</b>.","D":"Urticaria produces wheals, never epidermal detachment.","E":"An immediate (type I) reaction occurs within minutes to an hour. A week's delay with blistering is a delayed, T-cell mediated reaction."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L3; BM30 SUM1 Q22; BM28 SUM1 Q19 — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q23","t":"CYP450 interactions","s":"Which of the following is a serious drug interaction in clinical practice?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"⚠️ Two defensible options here (A and C), and the papers key <b>A</b>. Learn the underlying rule rather than the list: a dangerous CYP interaction needs <b>a strong inhibitor/inducer + a narrow-margin substrate</b>.<br><b>Inhibitors</b> (substrate level ↑, toxicity): ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir, erythromycin/clarithromycin, cimetidine, fluconazole, grapefruit juice.<br><b>Inducers</b> (substrate level ↓, treatment failure): rifampicin, phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, St John's wort, chronic ethanol.<br><b>CYP3A4/3A5</b> handles drugs from almost every class and is the enzyme named most often.","o":{"A":"Ketoconazole and simvastatin","B":"Itraconazole and rifampicin","C":"Atorvastatin and ritonavir","D":"Itraconazole and ciclosporin","E":"Ciclosporin and tacrolimus"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Ketoconazole is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor</b> and <b>simvastatin is a CYP3A4 substrate</b> with a narrow safety margin for muscle toxicity. Inhibiting its metabolism raises simvastatin levels several-fold and precipitates <b>rhabdomyolysis</b> — the classic, and genuinely dangerous, statin interaction.","B":"Rifampicin is a strong CYP3A4 <b>inducer</b> and itraconazole an inhibitor; combining them mostly means the antifungal fails, which is a treatment problem rather than a toxicity emergency.","C":"Ritonavir is a very strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, and this pairing is genuinely risky — but <b>atorvastatin is less 3A4-dependent than simvastatin</b>, so of the two statin options offered the ketoconazole/simvastatin combination is the more serious.","D":"Itraconazole does raise ciclosporin levels and needs monitoring, but it is a managed interaction rather than the archetypal severe one.","E":"Both are calcineurin inhibitors and are not normally co-prescribed; this is a duplication of therapy rather than a metabolic interaction."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q23 (ibuprofen vs S-warfarin); Ax [56] — CYP3A4/3A5 as the most important isoenzyme","img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q24","t":"Tolerance","s":"A 60-year-old man has taken sleeping tablets for 2 months and the effect is now diminishing. What is this called?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"Slide 17's list, ordered by time course: <b>tachyphylaxis</b> (minutes-hours, rapid receptor desensitisation) → <b>tolerance</b> (days-weeks, receptor down-regulation, needs dose escalation) → <b>resistance</b> (the organism) → <b>intolerance</b> (low threshold). '2 months' has now been the stem's duration in three cohorts.","o":{"A":"Tachyphylaxis","B":"Drug tolerance","C":"Desensitisation","D":"Drug resistance","E":"Drug intolerance"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Tachyphylaxis is <b>rapid</b> — within minutes to hours after a few closely spaced doses.","B":"Correct. Gradual loss of effect over weeks to months of repeated dosing = <b>tolerance</b>.","C":"Desensitisation is the receptor-level <i>mechanism</i>, not the name of the clinical phenomenon.","D":"Resistance applies to organisms and tumours, not to a patient's response to a hypnotic.","E":"Intolerance is an exaggerated response at an unusually low dose — the opposite direction."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L2; BM30 SUM1 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q26","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q26","t":"Structure-activity relationship","s":"What is the role of the <b>structure-activity relationship (SAR)</b> in drug development?","lec":"L4","sl":9,"c":"The discovery stage in the order the slides give it: <b>target identification</b> → <b>hit finding</b> (screening) → <b>lead identification</b> → <b>lead optimisation</b>, in which <b>SAR</b> is the main tool and <b>druggability</b> — whether a target can be modulated by a drug-like molecule at all — is the question being answered. Only then does preclinical toxicology begin.","o":{"A":"It relates a drug's chemical structure to its biological effect, guiding lead optimisation","B":"It evaluates a drug's pharmacokinetics in humans","C":"It assesses the drug's marketing potential","D":"It determines the optimal route of administration","E":"It is the FDA's criterion for approval"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. SAR studies systematically vary the chemical structure and observe how activity changes. It belongs to the <b>discovery/lead-optimisation</b> stage, where the aim is to improve potency, selectivity and drug-like properties before any animal or human testing.","B":"Human pharmacokinetics starts at Phase I.","C":"Commercial assessment is not a scientific stage of development.","D":"Route selection follows from the compound's absorption and stability, and is settled far later.","E":"FDA approval rests on the whole clinical dossier, not on SAR."},"also":"BM31 Q32 (druggability) and Q33 (aim of lead optimisation) probe the same stage","img":"slides_p/L4_9.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q29","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q29","t":"Regulatory role","s":"Which best describes the role of the FDA in the drug approval process?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"Keep the three good-practice codes apart, because they map onto different stages: <b>GLP</b> — Good Laboratory Practice, governs <b>preclinical</b> safety studies. <b>GCP</b> — Good Clinical Practice, governs <b>clinical trials</b> (the ICH-GCP E6 guideline). <b>GMP</b> — Good Manufacturing Practice, governs <b>production</b>. Thailand's regulator is the Thai FDA, which is also the <b>National Pharmacovigilance Centre</b>.","o":{"A":"Ensuring the safety, efficacy and quality of drugs through regulatory review","B":"Conducting all phases of the clinical trials itself","C":"Manufacturing pharmaceutical products","D":"Marketing and distributing approved drugs","E":"Monitoring the stock-market performance of pharmaceutical companies"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A regulator <b>reviews evidence</b> submitted by the sponsor and decides whether safety, efficacy and quality have been demonstrated. It also runs post-marketing surveillance (pharmacovigilance) after approval.","B":"Trials are designed and run by the <b>sponsor</b>; the regulator authorises them (via the IND) and inspects them.","C":"Manufacturing is the company's job; the regulator inspects it against GMP.","D":"Marketing is the company's job; the regulator controls what claims may be made.","E":"Not a regulatory function in any sense."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q22 — 'what does the FDA require for preclinical laboratory studies' → GLP","img":"slides_p/L4_22.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q31","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"Which phase evaluates a drug's efficacy and monitors adverse effects in a <b>larger population</b>, before marketing?","lec":"L4","sl":17,"c":"The phases as a single table:<br><b>Phase I</b> — 20–100 healthy volunteers · <b>safety + dose</b><br><b>Phase II</b> — a few hundred patients · <b>efficacy + optimal dose</b><br><b>Phase III</b> — hundreds to thousands · <b>confirmatory efficacy + rarer adverse effects</b> · most expensive · leads to the NDA<br><b>Phase IV</b> — the whole market · <b>post-marketing surveillance</b>, rare and long-latency effects<br>⚠️ Phase III and Phase IV are the pair most often confused. The discriminator is always <b>before vs after approval</b>.","o":{"A":"Preclinical","B":"Phase I","C":"Phase II","D":"Phase IV","E":"Phase III"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Preclinical work is in vitro and in animals.","B":"<b>Phase I</b> is a small first-in-human study, typically 20–100 <b>healthy volunteers</b>, establishing <b>safety and dose</b>.","C":"<b>Phase II</b> is the first study in <b>patients</b> — a few hundred — looking for preliminary efficacy and the <b>optimal dose</b>.","D":"<b>Phase IV</b> is post-marketing surveillance, which is also 'a larger population' — but it happens <b>after</b> approval. The stem's 'before marketing' is what separates them.","E":"Correct. <b>Phase III</b> is the large, confirmatory, usually randomised and blinded trial in hundreds to thousands of patients. It confirms efficacy against placebo or an active comparator and picks up less common adverse effects. It is the <b>most expensive</b> phase, and it is what supports the NDA."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q43 and Q37-39; BM29 SUM1 Q24; BM30 Formative 1 Q4; Ax [55-58] — every single cohort","img":"slides_p/L4_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q36","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q36","t":"Triptans","s":"A woman has a one-sided headache preceded by a visual aura. Which pharmacological action relieves it?","lec":"L5","sl":14,"c":"<b>The most repeated L5 item — five cohorts.</b> It comes in two forms: named-drug (<i>one-sided headache + photophobia/aura + nausea → <b>sumatriptan</b></i>) and mechanism (<i>which action helps → <b>stimulate 5-HT receptors</b></i>). Both are the same fact. The rest of the ladder: paracetamol → NSAID → <b>triptan, first-line specific</b> → <b>ergotamine, rescue only</b> (and never prophylaxis; combined with caffeine because it is poorly absorbed from the gut).","o":{"A":"Inhibition of COX-1","B":"Inhibition of H2 receptors","C":"Stimulation of ACE activity","D":"Stimulation of 5-HT receptors","E":"Inhibition of leukotriene activity"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"COX-1 inhibition is what an NSAID does. NSAIDs do help migraine and sit on the ladder — but they are step two, and the aura-plus-unilateral picture is the triptan stem.","B":"H2 blockade reduces gastric acid — cimetidine, famotidine. Nothing to do with migraine.","C":"ACE is the angiotensin pathway. Stimulating it would raise blood pressure; ACE <i>inhibitors</i> treat hypertension.","D":"Correct. <b>Triptans are 5-HT₁B/₁D agonists</b>. Stimulating 5-HT₁B constricts the dilated cranial vessels and stimulating 5-HT₁D inhibits trigeminal release of vasoactive neuropeptides such as CGRP. This item is the mechanism-phrased version of the sumatriptan stem.","E":"Leukotriene inhibition (montelukast, zileuton) is asthma therapy."},"also":"BM31 Q48; BM32 SUM1 L2; BM30 SUM1 Q47; BM29 SUM1 Q31 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q37","t":"COX-2 inhibitors","s":"A 50-year-old man with rheumatoid arthritis is given a <b>selective COX-2 inhibitor</b>. Which drug is it?","lec":"L5","sl":20,"c":"<b>The suffix is the answer: -coxib = selective COX-2.</b> The trade-off runs both ways — COX-2 selectivity spares the stomach but <b>raises cardiovascular risk</b>, because COX-2-derived prostacyclin is antithrombotic while platelet COX-1-derived thromboxane is untouched. Off-slide from the lecturer: <b>naproxen is the safest NSAID for a patient at cardiovascular risk</b>, diclofenac and ibuprofen both carry thrombotic reports, and coxibs are contraindicated outright in established cardiovascular disease.","o":{"A":"Diclofenac","B":"Ibuprofen","C":"Aspirin","D":"Mefenamic acid","E":"Celecoxib"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Diclofenac is a non-selective acetic-acid NSAID (with some COX-2 preference), and carries appreciable cardiovascular risk.","B":"Ibuprofen is a non-selective propionic-acid NSAID.","C":"Aspirin is unique — it <b>irreversibly acetylates</b> COX, and at low dose is COX-1 selective in platelets, which is the basis of antiplatelet therapy.","D":"Mefenamic acid is a non-selective fenamate.","E":"Correct. The <b>-coxib</b> suffix marks the selective COX-2 inhibitors — <b>celecoxib</b>, etoricoxib, parecoxib. Sparing COX-1 means less gastric injury, which is the point of the class."},"also":"BM29 SUM2 Q38 and BM28 SUM1 Q34 ask the mirror image — the TIA patient on low-dose aspirin who needs an NSAID, where the answer is naproxen and the coxibs are wrong","img":"slides_p/L5_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q38","t":"5-HT3 antagonists","s":"A 70-year-old cancer patient has had chemotherapy. Which drug reduces the vomiting it causes?","lec":"L5","sl":16,"c":"Match the antiemetic to the <b>pathway</b> the stem describes: <b>chemotherapy / radiation → 5-HT₃ antagonist</b> (ondansetron, granisetron) · <b>motion sickness / vestibular → first-generation H1 antihistamine</b> (dimenhydrinate, cinnarizine, promethazine, CPM) or hyoscine · <b>gastroparesis → prokinetic</b> (metoclopramide, domperidone). The serotonin subtypes on the slides: <b>5-HT₁</b> triptans (migraine), <b>5-HT₁A</b> buspirone, <b>5-HT₃</b> ondansetron (emesis), <b>5-HT₄</b> cisapride/tegaserod (prokinetic).","o":{"A":"Ondansetron","B":"Cisapride","C":"Buspirone","D":"Cimetidine","E":"Cinnarizine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Ondansetron</b> (and granisetron) are <b>5-HT₃ antagonists</b>. Chemotherapy damages enterochromaffin cells, which release serotonin onto vagal 5-HT₃ receptors and onto the chemoreceptor trigger zone. Blocking 5-HT₃ is the specific treatment.","B":"Cisapride is a 5-HT₄ <b>agonist</b> and prokinetic — it speeds gastric emptying. It was withdrawn in many countries for QT prolongation.","C":"Buspirone is a 5-HT₁A partial agonist used as an anxiolytic.","D":"Cimetidine is an H2 blocker for acid suppression.","E":"Cinnarizine is a first-generation antihistamine for <b>motion sickness</b> and vertigo — a different vomiting pathway entirely (vestibular, not chemoreceptor)."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q33; BM30 Formative 1 Q5 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q39","t":"Steroid mechanism","s":"Where does <b>prednisolone</b> act?","lec":"L5","sl":19,"c":"Slides 18–19 draw the cascade explicitly, with the drug classes hung off it: <b>phospholipid —(phospholipase A2)→ arachidonic acid —(COX)→ PG/TXA₂</b> and <b>—(lipoxygenase)→ leukotrienes</b>. <b>Steroids block phospholipase A2 (both branches)</b>; <b>NSAIDs block COX (one branch)</b>; <b>zileuton blocks lipoxygenase</b>; <b>montelukast blocks the LT receptor</b>. A stem naming a fall in AA <i>and</i> PGs <i>and</i> LTs can only be a steroid.","o":{"A":"Phospholipase A2","B":"Cyclooxygenase","C":"Thromboxane A2","D":"Prostaglandin","E":"Angiotensin"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Glucocorticoids induce lipocortin, which inhibits <b>phospholipase A2</b>, so arachidonic acid is never liberated and <b>both</b> prostaglandins and leukotrienes fail to form.","B":"COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism — it blocks only the prostaglandin/thromboxane branch.","C":"Thromboxane A2 is a <b>product</b> of the COX pathway in platelets, not a drug target for steroids.","D":"Prostaglandins are products of the pathway, not the enzyme being inhibited.","E":"Angiotensin belongs to the peptide autacoids and the renin-angiotensin system."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q32 (drug X reduces AA, PGs and LTs → prednisolone); BM30 SUM1 Q50 (herbal knee remedy — adulterated with steroid); BM32 SUM2; BM31 SUM2 Q3; BM30 SUM2 Q13 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_19.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q40","t":"H1 antagonists","s":"A person becomes dizzy and vomits while travelling by car. A drug with which mechanism should be used?","lec":"L5","sl":10,"c":"<b>Only first-generation antihistamines work for motion sickness</b>, precisely because they are sedating and cross the BBB — second-generation agents (loratadine, cetirizine, fexofenadine) do not, which is what makes them non-sedating and also useless here. The slide says to take them <b>30–60 minutes before</b> the journey (⚠️ she said 15–30 min aloud; <b>answer with the slide</b>). Two cautions the lecturer added off-slide: <b>cinnarizine and flunarizine lower dopamine and can cause parkinsonism</b> — avoid in Parkinson's disease and in the elderly — and <b>chronic first-generation antihistamine use impairs memory</b> through its anticholinergic action.","o":{"A":"5-HT₃ antagonist","B":"H2 antagonist","C":"COX-1 inhibitor","D":"H1 antagonist","E":"Angiotensin II antagonist"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"5-HT₃ antagonists are for <b>chemotherapy</b>-induced vomiting. They are notably ineffective in motion sickness, because the pathway is different — this is the intended trap.","B":"H2 receptors are on gastric parietal cells; blocking them reduces acid, not motion sickness.","C":"COX-1 inhibition is analgesic and anti-inflammatory.","D":"Correct. Motion sickness is <b>vestibular</b>, and the vestibular nuclei signal through <b>histamine H1</b> and muscarinic receptors. A <b>first-generation</b> H1 antihistamine — one that crosses the blood-brain barrier — is the treatment.","E":"Angiotensin II antagonists are antihypertensives."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q45 (Parkinson's patient + motion sickness → CPM, because cinnarizine and promethazine are the traps)","img":"slides_p/L5_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|Q43","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"Q43","t":"Second-generation antihistamines","s":"A medical student on the way to an examination develops flushing of the arms and face, a runny nose and sneezing. Which drug should be given?","lec":"L5","sl":11,"c":"The lecturer said explicitly that <b>both generations must be known by name, with their advantages and disadvantages</b>.<br><b>First generation</b> — chlorpheniramine (CPM), dimenhydrinate, diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, pheniramine, cinnarizine, promethazine. Cross the BBB → <b>sedation</b> and anticholinergic effects; useful for motion sickness, vertigo and sleep.<br><b>Second generation</b> — loratadine, cetirizine, levocetirizine, fexofenadine, ketotifen, azelastine. Non-sedating, once daily, for allergic rhinitis and urticaria.<br>⚠️ <b>Terfenadine and astemizole were withdrawn</b> for QT prolongation and torsades.<br>Whenever a stem specifies a job, a journey, driving or an exam, it is asking for the <b>non-sedating</b> option.","o":{"A":"Diphenhydramine","B":"Chlorpheniramine (CPM)","C":"Loratadine","D":"Hydroxyzine","E":"Cinnarizine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Diphenhydramine is the most sedating of the first-generation agents — it is sold as a sleep aid.","B":"CPM is first-generation and sedating. Effective for the allergy, disastrous for the exam.","C":"Correct, and the context is the whole question. <b>Loratadine is second-generation</b> — it does not cross the blood-brain barrier appreciably, so it relieves the allergic symptoms <b>without sedating</b> a student who is about to sit an exam.","D":"Hydroxyzine is first-generation and markedly sedating; it is used as an anxiolytic and antipruritic.","E":"Cinnarizine is first-generation, sedating, and indicated for vertigo and motion sickness rather than allergic rhinitis."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q46 — the identical stem, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q1","t":"Glucocorticoid adverse effects","s":"What happens to a patient who uses steroids for a long time?","lec":"L7","sl":17,"c":"The long-term adverse-effect list, which is simply <b>Cushing syndrome produced iatrogenically</b>: moon facies, buffalo hump and truncal obesity; <b>hyperglycaemia</b> and glycosuria; <b>osteoporosis</b> and myopathy; sodium retention with oedema and <b>hypertension</b>; <b>hypokalaemia</b>; peptic ulcer; thin skin, striae and poor wound healing; <b>increased risk of infection</b>; cataract and glaucoma; growth retardation in children; and <b>HPA axis suppression</b>. The lecturer said the <b>pharmacological effects list is the spine</b> — indications and adverse effects are both derived from it.","o":{"A":"Osteoporosis","B":"Hypotension","C":"Mydriasis","D":"Pituitary tumour","E":"Lupus-like syndrome"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Osteoporosis</b> is one of the defining long-term glucocorticoid toxicities: steroids inhibit osteoblasts, increase bone resorption and reduce intestinal calcium absorption. Vertebral compression fracture is the classic consequence.","B":"Glucocorticoids cause <b>hypertension</b>, not hypotension — sodium and water retention through mineralocorticoid activity. Hypotension belongs to steroid <i>withdrawal</i>.","C":"Steroids cause <b>cataract and glaucoma</b> in the eye, not mydriasis (which is an antimuscarinic or α1-agonist effect).","D":"Exogenous steroid <b>suppresses</b> the HPA axis — the pituitary shrinks its ACTH output. A pituitary tumour is the cause of Cushing <i>disease</i>, not a consequence of steroid therapy.","E":"Drug-induced lupus is associated with hydralazine, procainamide and isoniazid. Steroids <b>treat</b> lupus."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q14 — the identical item, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L7_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q2","t":"Steroid withdrawal","s":"A patient stops a corticosteroid abruptly and develops adrenal insufficiency. Which symptom would be found?","lec":"L7","sl":16,"c":"⚠️ <b>Every decoy in this item is an EXCESS effect.</b> When a stem describes withdrawal or insufficiency, invert the whole Cushing list and pick the option that has been flipped. This is why steroids must be <b>tapered</b>, never stopped abruptly, after more than about two weeks of therapy, and why <b>alternate-day (วันเว้นวัน) dosing</b> is used to limit HPA suppression in the first place. Acute adrenal crisis is treated with IV <b>hydrocortisone</b> plus fluids.","o":{"A":"Hypertension","B":"Hyperglycaemia","C":"Dehydration","D":"Moon face","E":"Emotional disturbance"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Excess causes hypertension; deficiency causes <b>hypotension</b> — and in adrenal crisis, shock.","B":"Steroid <i>excess</i> causes hyperglycaemia; deficiency causes <b>hypoglycaemia</b>.","C":"Correct. Adrenal insufficiency is <b>deficiency</b>, so every finding inverts the excess picture. Lost mineralocorticoid activity means sodium and water are not retained → <b>dehydration, hypotension, hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia</b>, with weakness, nausea and hypoglycaemia.","D":"Moon face is a feature of steroid <b>excess</b>.","E":"Emotional disturbance occurs with excess (and can occur in withdrawal), but it is not the specific sign the deficiency picture is built on."},"img":"slides_p/L7_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q3","t":"Steroid mechanism","s":"A patient with rheumatoid arthritis is treated with a drug that inhibits <b>phospholipase A2</b>. Which drug is it?","lec":"L7","sl":9,"c":"Notice the shape of this option list: <b>one agonist among four antagonists</b>. Sorting drugs into agonist vs antagonist is examinable on its own — the lecturer asked the class to name a glucocorticoid antagonist and explicitly <b>rejected prednisolone, betamethasone and dexamethasone</b>. Learn the antagonist roster: mifepristone (receptor), metyrapone (11-β-hydroxylase), ketoconazole (17-α-hydroxylase), aminoglutethimide (cholesterol → pregnenolone), etomidate, and spironolactone/eplerenone for the mineralocorticoid receptor.","o":{"A":"Ketoconazole","B":"Mifepristone","C":"Metyrapone","D":"Spironolactone","E":"Prednisolone"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"<b>Ketoconazole</b> inhibits <b>17-α-hydroxylase</b> and other steroidogenic enzymes.","B":"<b>Mifepristone</b> blocks the glucocorticoid <b>receptor</b> — an antagonist.","C":"<b>Metyrapone</b> inhibits <b>11-β-hydroxylase</b>, blocking cortisol synthesis.","D":"<b>Spironolactone</b> blocks the <b>mineralocorticoid</b> (aldosterone) receptor.","E":"Correct. Of the five options, only <b>prednisolone</b> is a glucocorticoid <b>agonist</b>. Its anti-inflammatory action runs through lipocortin-mediated inhibition of phospholipase A2."},"also":"BM32 SUM2; BM30 SUM2 Q13 — three cohorts, the most repeated L7 item","img":"slides_p/L7_9.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q4","t":"Ketoconazole","s":"A 45-year-old woman with Cushing's syndrome is to be treated with <b>ketoconazole</b>. What is this drug's action?","lec":"L7","sl":21,"c":"The synthesis inhibitors, mapped onto the pathway (slide 19's steroidogenesis figure): <b>aminoglutethimide</b> — cholesterol → pregnenolone, the very first step, so ALL steroid synthesis falls; <b>ketoconazole</b> — 17-α-hydroxylase and other CYP steps, adrenal and gonadal; <b>metyrapone</b> — 11-β-hydroxylase, the last step to cortisol; <b>etomidate</b> — also 11-β-hydroxylase, which is why a single induction dose can cause adrenal suppression. Ketoconazole's own toxicity is <b>hepatotoxicity</b>.","o":{"A":"Stimulates the aldosterone receptor","B":"Blocks the glucocorticoid receptor","C":"Reduces 17-alpha hydroxylase activity","D":"Reduces aromatase enzyme activity","E":"Increases aldosterone synthase activity"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Stimulating the aldosterone receptor would cause sodium retention and hypertension — the opposite of a treatment. Spironolactone <i>blocks</i> that receptor.","B":"Receptor blockade is <b>mifepristone</b>. Ketoconazole never touches the receptor.","C":"Correct. At high dose ketoconazole inhibits several cytochrome-P450-dependent steroidogenic enzymes, <b>17-α-hydroxylase</b> among them, suppressing both adrenal and gonadal steroid synthesis. Its clinical use in Cushing's is limited by <b>hepatotoxicity</b> and by drug interactions.","D":"Aromatase inhibition (anastrozole, letrozole) is breast-cancer therapy. Ketoconazole does reduce androgen synthesis, but by the hydroxylases, not aromatase.","E":"Increasing aldosterone synthesis would worsen the mineralocorticoid effects."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q16 — adrenal tumour with Cushingoid features → ketoconazole","img":"slides_p/L7_21.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q17","t":"Haemorrhagic cystitis","s":"A cancer patient is receiving bleomycin, vincristine, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin and dacarbazine. He develops <b>haematuria and bladder pain</b>. Which drug prevents these symptoms?","lec":"L6","sl":5,"c":"⚠️ <b>Read the answer list before committing.</b> The drug normally taught for this is <b>MESNA</b> (mercaptoethane sulfonate) — but mesna is <i>not offered</i> in BM31's or BM30's option list, and the handout's cyclophosphamide slide names <b>N-acetylcysteine</b> alongside it as the other sulfhydryl donor. Both cohorts key N-acetylcysteine. If mesna appears as an option, mesna is the answer.<br>The <b>protectant → toxicity</b> map the lecturer states outright: <b>mesna → cyclophosphamide</b> (haemorrhagic cystitis) · <b>dexrazoxane → doxorubicin</b> (cardiotoxicity) · <b>amifostine → cisplatin</b> (nephrotoxicity) · <b>folinic acid → methotrexate</b>. He noted that mesna has appeared in the National Licensing exam.","o":{"A":"Methylene blue","B":"Amifostine","C":"Folinic acid","D":"Mannitol","E":"N-acetylcysteine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Methylene blue is the antidote for methaemoglobinaemia — and for ifosfamide-induced <b>encephalopathy</b>, not cystitis.","B":"<b>Amifostine</b> is a phosphatase-activated prodrug and free-radical scavenger, used to protect against <b>cisplatin</b> nephrotoxicity.","C":"<b>Folinic acid (leucovorin)</b> is the rescue for <b>methotrexate</b>, bypassing the DHFR block.","D":"Mannitol produces a forced diuresis and is used adjunctively with cisplatin, but it does not inactivate acrolein.","E":"Correct as keyed. The culprit is <b>cyclophosphamide</b>: its metabolite <b>acrolein</b> is excreted in urine and injures the bladder urothelium, causing <b>haemorrhagic cystitis</b>. A sulfhydryl donor in the urine inactivates acrolein — <b>N-acetylcysteine</b> is such a donor, and the handout lists it alongside mesna on the cyclophosphamide slide."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q17 — the identical item and identical option list, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_5.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q18","t":"Taxanes","s":"A woman on treatment for advanced breast cancer complains of numbness and tingling in her hands and feet. Which drug most likely caused this?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"Every distractor in this item is another drug's <b>signature toxicity</b> — that is how the examiners build option lists throughout L6. Learn one toxicity per drug and these items collapse into recall: cyclophosphamide/cystitis, cisplatin/nephro- and ototoxicity, doxorubicin/cardiac, bleomycin/pulmonary fibrosis, mitomycin C/nephro, vincristine/neuropathy, taxane/neuropathy, methotrexate/mucositis.","o":{"A":"Docetaxel","B":"Doxorubicin","C":"Cyclophosphamide","D":"Tamoxifen","E":"Trastuzumab"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Taxanes cause a dose-limiting <b>peripheral sensory neuropathy</b> in a stocking-and-glove distribution, and docetaxel is front-line in advanced breast cancer.","B":"Doxorubicin → <b>cardiotoxicity</b> (irreversible, cumulative).","C":"Cyclophosphamide → <b>haemorrhagic cystitis</b> from acrolein.","D":"Tamoxifen → endometrial carcinoma and thromboembolism.","E":"Trastuzumab → <b>cardiomyopathy</b>, which is why it is not given with an anthracycline."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q18; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts, word-for-word","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q19","t":"Vinca alkaloids","s":"A man is diagnosed with <b>Hodgkin's disease</b> and a mitotic inhibitor is used. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"The five mechanistic classes of L6, which every item is built from: <b>1. Alkylating agents</b> (nitrogen mustards incl. cyclophosphamide, nitrosoureas, platinum compounds) · <b>2. Cytotoxic antibiotics</b> (anthracyclines, bleomycin, mitomycin C) · <b>3. Antimetabolites</b> (methotrexate; 6-MP/6-TG; 5-FU, gemcitabine) · <b>4. Mitotic inhibitors</b> (vinca alkaloids, taxanes) and topoisomerase inhibitors (camptothecins = topo I; epipodophyllotoxins = topo II). Cell-cycle <b>specific</b> agents act on dividing cells; alkylating agents are non-specific.","o":{"A":"Methotrexate","B":"Vincristine","C":"Daunorubicin","D":"Gemcitabine","E":"Epipodophyllotoxin"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Methotrexate is a folate <b>antimetabolite</b> acting in S phase.","B":"Correct. <b>Vincristine</b> is a vinca alkaloid — it binds β-tubulin and prevents microtubule polymerisation, arresting mitosis. It is part of the classic Hodgkin's regimens.","C":"Daunorubicin is an <b>anthracycline antibiotic</b> — it intercalates DNA and inhibits topoisomerase II. Not a mitotic inhibitor.","D":"Gemcitabine is a pyrimidine <b>antimetabolite</b>.","E":"Epipodophyllotoxins (etoposide) inhibit <b>topoisomerase II</b>. They come from a plant, which makes them a tempting distractor, but their target is DNA topology, not the spindle."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q19; BM32 SUM2 (non-small-cell lung carcinoma instead of Hodgkin's) — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q21","t":"Taxane mechanism","s":"A drug forms a complex with <b>α,β-tubulin</b>, inhibiting the depolymerisation of the spindle fibre. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"Two words decide it: <b>polymerisation → vinca</b>, <b>DEpolymerisation → taxane</b>. The tubulin detail reinforces it — <b>vinca binds β-tubulin, taxanes bind α,β-tubulin</b>. ⚠️ The recorded lecture simplifies both to 'β-tubulin'; the handout and every past paper say α,β for taxanes, and <b>the handout is what to answer with</b>.","o":{"A":"Vincristine","B":"Topotecan","C":"Paclitaxel","D":"5-Fluorouracil","E":"6-Mercaptopurine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Vincristine binds β-tubulin and blocks <b>polymerisation</b> — the mirror image. The examiners rely on candidates missing the direction.","B":"Topotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor.","C":"Correct. <b>Taxanes bind α,β-tubulin and stabilise the microtubule</b>, preventing the depolymerisation that mitosis needs. The spindle is locked in the assembled state.","D":"5-FU inhibits thymidylate synthase.","E":"6-MP is a purine antimetabolite inactivated by xanthine oxidase."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q21; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q25","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q25","t":"Antimetabolites","s":"A gout patient is prescribed a <b>xanthine oxidase inhibitor</b>, which means the dose of one chemotherapy drug must be reduced. Which drug?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"A pharmacokinetic interaction with a memorable irony: allopurinol is frequently given <i>because of</i> the hyperuricaemia that tumour lysis produces, and it is precisely that drug which makes 6-MP dangerous. Slide 11 shows the arrow — <b>6-MP → (xanthine oxidase) → inactive</b> — and 6-thioguanine sits alongside it on the same pathway.","o":{"A":"Doxorubicin","B":"Bleomycin","C":"Mitomycin C","D":"6-Mercaptopurine","E":"Fluorouracil"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Doxorubicin is hepatically metabolised and biliary excreted; its toxicity is cardiac.","B":"Bleomycin is inactivated by bleomycin hydrolase; its toxicity is pulmonary fibrosis.","C":"Mitomycin C is hepatically metabolised; its toxicity is nephrotoxicity.","D":"Correct. <b>Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-mercaptopurine.</b> Allopurinol blocks that enzyme, so 6-MP accumulates and causes severe myelosuppression unless the dose is cut substantially.","E":"5-FU is degraded by dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q22; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM31|S2-Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM31","n":"S2-Q22","t":"Platinum compounds","s":"Which adverse effect may result from <b>cisplatin</b> therapy?","lec":"L6","sl":7,"c":"Note the construction: <b>every decoy is another L6 drug's signature toxicity</b>, so the item is really testing the whole map at once. Cisplatin's triad — <b>nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, severe emesis</b> — is worth holding together, and the emesis is what makes the ondansetron item in L5 clinically continuous with this one.","o":{"A":"Haemolytic anaemia","B":"Cardiac toxicity","C":"Pulmonary fibrosis","D":"Haemorrhagic cystitis","E":"Renal failure"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Haemolytic anaemia is not a characteristic cisplatin effect.","B":"Cardiac toxicity is <b>doxorubicin</b> (and trastuzumab).","C":"Pulmonary fibrosis is <b>bleomycin</b>.","D":"Haemorrhagic cystitis is <b>cyclophosphamide</b>, via acrolein.","E":"Correct. <b>Nephrotoxicity is cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicity</b> — it damages the proximal tubule. It is mitigated by aggressive hydration, forced diuresis and <b>amifostine</b>. Cisplatin is also severely emetogenic and <b>ototoxic</b>."},"also":"BM30 SUM2 Q20 — two cohorts. In BM31 the item appears among the questions whose number the compilers could not recall","img":"slides_p/L6_7.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q1","t":"Route of administration","s":"For the same therapeutic effect, which route of administration needs the <b>largest</b> dose to reach the same plasma concentration?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"One fact, two questions: <b>largest dose = oral</b> (lowest F), <b>smallest dose = IV</b> (F = 100%). Dose required is inversely proportional to bioavailability. Watch the direction word in the stem — 'largest'/'least' is the entire question, and BM30 asked it both ways in the same exam season.","o":{"A":"Intramuscular","B":"Intravenous","C":"Oral","D":"Subcutaneous","E":"Sublingual"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"IM bioavailability is ≥ 75%; it avoids first pass entirely.","B":"IV is <b>100% bioavailable by definition</b>, so it needs the <b>smallest</b> dose — this is the same fact asked from the other end, and BM30's Formative 1 did exactly that.","C":"Correct. Oral has the <b>lowest bioavailability</b> of these routes (5–100%) because of incomplete absorption and <b>first-pass metabolism</b>. Less of each dose survives to reach the plasma, so a larger dose is needed for the same level.","D":"SC bioavailability is also ≥ 75%.","E":"Sublingual absorption drains into systemic veins, bypassing first pass."},"also":"BM30 Formative 1 Q1 (least → IV); BM28 SUM1 Q1 (largest → oral) — three appearances","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q2","t":"Infusion-related reactions","s":"Drug X is given intravenously and the patient develops a red face and neck. What is drug X?","lec":"L1","sl":15,"c":"Slide 15 carries the box the lecturer drills: <b>ตัวอย่างยาที่ห้ามฉีดแบบ IV bolus</b> — drugs that must never be given by IV push, only by slow infusion (IV drip). <b>Vancomycin</b> (red man syndrome), <b>potassium chloride</b> (cardiac arrest), <b>phenytoin</b> (hypotension, arrhythmia) and concentrated electrolytes are the ones to be able to name. The <b>IV push vs IV drip</b> distinction is examined in its own right.","o":{"A":"Phenytoin","B":"Vancomycin","C":"Cloxacillin","D":"Simvastatin","E":"Potassium chloride"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Phenytoin given by rapid IV push causes <b>hypotension and arrhythmia</b> (largely from its propylene glycol vehicle), and extravasation causes purple glove syndrome — not facial flushing. It is one of the drugs on slide 15's 'never IV push' list.","B":"Correct. <b>Vancomycin</b> causes <b>red man syndrome</b> — flushing of the face, neck and upper torso from <b>direct, non-IgE-mediated mast-cell histamine release</b> when the infusion runs too fast. It is not a true allergy, and it is prevented by slowing the infusion and pre-treating with an antihistamine.","C":"Cloxacillin can cause a true IgE-mediated penicillin allergy, which would present with urticaria and anaphylaxis rather than isolated flushing.","D":"Simvastatin is an oral drug; there is no IV preparation.","E":"<b>KCl</b> is on the never-push list too, and for a far more serious reason — rapid IV potassium causes <b>fatal cardiac arrest</b>."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q3 — 'after IV bolus, which drug gives a red rash' — two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L1_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q5","t":"Membrane transport","s":"By which mechanism are most drugs transported into cells?","lec":"L2","sl":5,"c":"Slide 5 lists the four routes across a membrane — <b>paracellular, simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, active transport</b> — and slide 6 gives what governs the dominant one: <b>molecular size</b> and <b>lipid:water partition coefficient</b>. Consequences worth carrying: only the <b>un-ionised</b> form diffuses, so pH and pKa control absorption (weak acid in acid urine → un-ionised → reabsorbed → clearance falls; alkalinise the urine and a weak acid such as aspirin is trapped and excreted).","o":{"A":"Active transport","B":"Simple diffusion","C":"Facilitated diffusion","D":"Pinocytosis","E":"Paracellular transport"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Active transport needs a carrier and ATP and can move drug <i>against</i> a gradient. It matters for specific drugs (levodopa, methotrexate, renal tubular secretion, P-glycoprotein efflux) but is not the general mechanism.","B":"Correct. The great majority of drugs cross membranes by <b>passive simple diffusion</b> of the <b>un-ionised, lipid-soluble</b> fraction, down a concentration gradient. It needs no carrier and no energy, and it is not saturable.","C":"Facilitated diffusion needs a carrier but no energy and cannot work against a gradient. Again, specific rather than general.","D":"Pinocytosis is engulfment in vesicles, relevant to very large molecules and to some protein drugs.","E":"Paracellular transport is passage <i>between</i> cells through tight junctions — restricted to small water-soluble molecules and to leaky epithelia."},"also":"Ax [58] — the pKa 4.5 at duodenal pH 5.5 absorption calculation","img":"slides_p/L2_5.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q7","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q7","t":"Therapeutic index","s":"Which kind of drug requires the greatest caution in use?","lec":"L2","sl":10,"c":"The narrow-therapeutic-index drugs are worth knowing by name, because the exam also asks 'which drug needs frequent plasma monitoring': <b>digoxin, phenytoin, theophylline, warfarin, lithium, aminoglycosides, ciclosporin</b>. Related quantities: <b>therapeutic index = LD50/ED50</b> (or TD50/ED50) — <b>the higher the TI, the safer the drug</b>; and the general dose ordering <b>ED50 &lt; TD50 &lt; LD50</b>.","o":{"A":"A drug with a short onset","B":"A drug with a high MTC","C":"A drug with a narrow therapeutic range","D":"A drug with a long duration","E":"A drug whose Tmax occurs rapidly"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A short onset is a convenience property.","B":"A <b>high</b> MTC is reassuring, not dangerous — it means the toxic threshold is far above the effective one, so the window is wide.","C":"Correct. The <b>therapeutic range</b> is the gap between the MEC and the MTC. When it is narrow, a small change in dose, absorption or clearance moves the patient from ineffective to toxic — which is why these drugs need <b>therapeutic drug monitoring</b>.","D":"A long duration affects dosing frequency; it is not in itself hazardous.","E":"A rapid Tmax means the peak arrives early. Not a safety concern by itself."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q7; BM29 SUM1 Q6 ('which drug requires frequent measurement of blood concentration' → the narrow-therapeutic-window drug) — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q8","t":"CYP450","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> related to CYP450 activity?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"Slide 8's equation makes the distinction the item turns on: <b>F = Fa × Fg × Fh</b>, where <b>Fa</b> = fraction absorbed across the gut wall (physicochemical), <b>Fg</b> = fraction escaping <b>gut</b> metabolism, <b>Fh</b> = fraction escaping <b>hepatic</b> metabolism. Only Fg and Fh are CYP-dependent. Note that CYP3A4 is abundant in the intestinal wall as well as the liver — which is how grapefruit juice raises the levels of oral CYP3A4 substrates.","o":{"A":"Percentage absorption","B":"Cmax","C":"Bioavailability","D":"First-pass effect","E":"Phase I metabolism"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>How much of a dose crosses the gut wall</b> is governed by the drug's physicochemical properties, the dosage form and gut transit — not by a metabolising enzyme. Absorption happens before any hepatic enzyme sees the drug.","B":"Cmax <i>is</i> affected: heavier first-pass metabolism lowers the peak that reaches the systemic circulation.","C":"Bioavailability is the parameter first-pass metabolism most directly reduces — F = Fa × Fg × Fh, and CYP enzymes act at both Fg (gut wall) and Fh (liver).","D":"The first-pass effect <b>is</b> CYP-mediated metabolism, in gut wall and liver.","E":"Phase I metabolism is largely CYP450 by definition."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q6 — word-for-word the same item, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q9","t":"Distribution interactions","s":"A pregnant woman takes <b>sulfonamide</b> and paracetamol during pregnancy, and the baby is born with <b>kernicterus</b>. How did this happen?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ Two options can look right here. 'A pharmacokinetic interaction' is <b>true but generic</b>, while 'drug-endogenous' names the actual competing pair; slide 17 is headed <i>Binding competition between endogenous and drug</i> and uses this very example. Take the most specific option offered. The underlying mechanism to hold on to: <b>acidic drugs bind albumin, basic drugs bind α1-acid glycoprotein</b>; displacement raises the <b>free</b> fraction, which is the pharmacologically active one. Newborns are uniquely vulnerable because their blood-brain barrier is immature — the same reason slide 14 notes the BBB becomes permeable in inflammation.","o":{"A":"A drug-drug interaction at metabolism","B":"A pharmacodynamic interaction","C":"A drug-endogenous substance interaction","D":"A drug-drug interaction at absorption","E":"A receptor-level interaction"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Metabolism is not involved; nothing about sulfonamide's biotransformation causes this.","B":"It is not pharmacodynamic — the two substances are not acting on a common effector. It is a binding competition, which is a distribution (pharmacokinetic) event.","C":"Correct, and this is the most specific description. <b>Sulfonamide displaces bilirubin from albumin.</b> Bilirubin is <b>endogenous</b>, not another drug, so the competition is between a drug and an endogenous ligand — which is exactly what slide 17 is titled. The freed, unbound bilirubin crosses the neonate's immature blood-brain barrier and stains the basal ganglia: kernicterus.","D":"Absorption is not involved — the competition happens in the plasma, after absorption is complete.","E":"No receptor is involved at any point."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q8 — same vignette, keyed as 'the drug competes for plasma protein binding'","img":"slides_p/L2_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q11","t":"Dose adjustment","s":"How should the loading dose and maintenance dose be adjusted in a patient who is a <b>slow metaboliser</b>?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"Memorise the two vignettes as a matched pair, because both are asked:<br><b>Renal failure / slow metaboliser</b> — clearance ↓ → <b>LD unchanged, MD decreased</b>.<br><b>Hypoalbuminaemia</b> — free fraction ↑ → Vd ↑ → <b>LD increased, MD unchanged</b>.<br>Work it from the formulas rather than memory: <b>LD = Vd × Css</b>, <b>MD rate = Css × CL</b>. Ask which of Vd or CL the condition changes.","o":{"A":"LD unchanged, MD decreased","B":"LD increased, MD increased","C":"LD increased, MD unchanged","D":"LD decreased, MD unchanged","E":"LD unchanged, MD increased"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Slow metabolism means <b>reduced clearance</b>. The maintenance dose rate is Css × CL, so it must come <b>down</b>. The loading dose is Vd × Css, and metabolic capacity does not change the volume of distribution, so the <b>LD is unchanged</b>.","B":"Raising either dose in someone who cannot clear the drug would cause toxicity.","C":"The LD only rises when Vd rises — as in hypoalbuminaemia, where the free fraction increases.","D":"Reducing the LD would delay reaching the target concentration for no reason; the volume to be filled is the same.","E":"Raising the MD is the opposite of what reduced clearance requires."},"also":"Ax [55,56] — the renal-failure and hypoalbuminaemia versions, both keyed","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q12","t":"Loading dose","s":"A 35-year-old man weighing 70 kg comes to the emergency department with severe asthma. The doctor wants to give a single dose of <b>theophylline</b>. How much should he receive?<br><i>Css = 15 mg/L · CL = 2.8 L/h · Vd = 35 L · F = 1 · t½ = 5 h</i>","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"⚠️ The examiners deliberately supply <b>every</b> parameter so that both formulas look available. Decide which question is being asked <i>before</i> multiplying:<br>• <b>'a single dose', 'to reach a target level', 'immediately'</b> → <b>LD = Vd × Css</b><br>• <b>'infusion rate', 'to maintain', 'per hour'</b> → <b>Q = Css × CL</b><br>Here, D is the answer to the maintenance question and is placed as a decoy.","o":{"A":"320 mg","B":"525 mg","C":"36.8 mg","D":"42 mg","E":"210 mg every 5 h"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"320 mg does not follow from any of these numbers.","B":"Correct. A single dose to reach a target concentration is a <b>loading dose</b>: <b>LD = Vd × Css = 35 L × 15 mg/L = 525 mg</b>. F is 1, so no correction is needed.","C":"36.8 mg is far too small — roughly the size of an hourly maintenance amount, not a loading dose.","D":"42 mg is <b>Css × CL = 15 × 2.8</b> — that is the maintenance <b>infusion rate</b> in mg/h, which is the answer to a different question.","E":"'210 mg every 5 h' is a maintenance regimen. The stem says <b>only 1 dose</b>."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q10 — the identical theophylline numbers; BM32 SUM1 L2 (Vd 0.5 L/kg, 60 kg → 360 mg); BM29 SUM1 Q11 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q13","t":"Infusion rate","s":"A 50-year-old patient receives an IV infusion of drug X, and a plasma concentration of 100 mg/L is required. How fast must the infusion run?<br><i>Vd = 30 L · CL = 2.4 L/h · t½ = 6 h</i>","lec":"L2","sl":28,"c":"<b>Q = Css × CL</b> is on slide 28, stated in exactly that form. Two habits make these items reliable: check the <b>units</b> the options are in (mg/min versus mg/h is where marks are lost), and notice that the examiners always give more parameters than the formula needs. The companion formula for oral maintenance dosing is <b>MD = Q × T / F</b>.","o":{"A":"50 mg/min","B":"10 mg/min","C":"4 mg/min","D":"30 mg/h","E":"300 mg/h"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"50 mg/min is 3000 mg/h, wildly high.","B":"10 mg/min is 600 mg/h — two and a half times too fast.","C":"Correct. <b>Q = Css × CL = 100 mg/L × 2.4 L/h = 240 mg/h</b>. Convert: 240 ÷ 60 = <b>4 mg/min</b>. Note that Vd and t½ are decoys — an infusion rate depends on clearance alone.","D":"30 mg/h would come from using the volume (30 L) as if it were a rate.","E":"300 mg/h is close but wrong; it corresponds to a clearance of 3 L/h rather than 2.4."},"img":"slides_p/L2_28.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q14","t":"First-order elimination","s":"A man was given a drug. After 40 hours, 97% of it has been metabolised. What is the drug's half-life?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>5 half-lives ≈ 97%</b> — the same 5 half-lives is also the time to reach <b>steady state</b>, which is why the number is worth over-learning. Full ladder: 1 → 50%, 2 → 75%, 3 → 87.5%, 4 → 93.75%, 5 → 96.875%, 6 → 98.4%, 7 → 99.2%.","o":{"A":"8 hours","B":"4 hours","C":"5 hours","D":"10 hours","E":"15 hours"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. 97% eliminated = <b>5 half-lives</b> (50 → 75 → 87.5 → 93.75 → 96.875%). 40 ÷ 5 = <b>8 hours</b>.","B":"4 hours would mean 10 half-lives had passed — over 99.9% gone.","C":"5 hours is the answer to BM28's version of this item, where the elapsed time was 25 h. Check the number in front of you.","D":"10 hours gives 4 half-lives = 93.75%, not 97%.","E":"15 hours gives under 3 half-lives ≈ 85%."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L2 (40 h → 8 h); BM28 SUM1 Q9 (25 h → 5 h) — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q15","t":"Second messengers","s":"Which mechanism is involved in glycogenolysis in response to noradrenaline?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"Recurring at a rate of five cohorts, this item is really a test of the G-protein table: <b>Gs ↑cAMP · Gi ↓cAMP · Gq → PLC → IP₃/DAG → Ca²⁺</b>. Pair each with its receptors: β1/β2/β3 and D1 (Gs); α2, M2 and D2 (Gi); α1, M1/M3, H1 and 5-HT₂ (Gq).","o":{"A":"Alpha-2 receptor blockade","B":"Elevation of cAMP","C":"Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase","D":"Elevation of cytosolic calcium","E":"Reduction of phospholipase C activity"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Blockade cannot be an agonist's mechanism of action.","B":"Correct. β-adrenoceptor → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → <b>cAMP ↑</b> → PKA → glycogen phosphorylase → glycogenolysis.","C":"That lowers cAMP — the Gi arm, and the wrong direction.","D":"The α1/Gq arm does raise calcium in hepatocytes, but the keyed answer across five cohorts is cAMP.","E":"Reducing PLC activity suppresses IP₃/DAG signalling."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q16; BM32 SUM1; BM28 SUM1 Q14 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q16","t":"Dose-response curves","s":"A graded dose-response figure shows drugs A, B and C. Drug A's curve is left of the others and reaches the same plateau as C; B plateaus lower. Which statement does the data support?","lec":"L3","sl":11,"c":"Two axes, two properties — that is the whole of figure reading: <b>horizontal = potency</b> (EC50/ED50, and affinity), <b>vertical = efficacy</b> (Emax). Then three shapes: parallel rightward shift with the same ceiling = <b>competitive antagonist</b>; ceiling pushed down = <b>non-competitive/irreversible</b>; a plateau below the full agonist's = <b>partial agonist</b>. Prof DA's own slide 11 has the answers written into it.","o":{"A":"Drugs A and C have equal potency","B":"Drugs A and C have equal efficacy but A is more potent","C":"Drug A is an irreversible antagonist","D":"Drugs A and C have the same affinity","E":"Drugs A and B have the same mechanism"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Different horizontal positions mean different potencies.","B":"Correct. Same plateau = <b>equal efficacy</b>; curve further left = <b>greater potency</b> (lower EC50).","C":"An irreversible antagonist would <b>depress</b> the ceiling of the agonist it is tested against; A is producing the largest response at the lowest dose, which is agonist behaviour.","D":"Affinity tracks with the horizontal position — different positions, different affinities.","E":"A lower plateau for B suggests B is a <b>partial</b> agonist, and nothing in the figure establishes a shared mechanism."},"f":["image"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q15; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM28 SUM1 Q13; BM30 Formative 1 Q3 — every cohort","img":"slides_p/L3_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q17","t":"Nuclear receptors","s":"A drug which acts via <b>intranuclear receptors</b> must have which characteristic?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"<b>Five cohorts.</b> The stem names a lipophilic drug and the word nuclear/intranuclear/heterodimeric; the answer is the transcription option. Steroid receptors <b>homodimerise</b>; retinoid, thyroid and vitamin D receptors <b>heterodimerise with RXR</b> — but both regulate transcription, so the dimer type never changes the answer. It does matter in L7, where the lecturer stressed that a glucocorticoid-receptor complex which cannot form a <b>homodimer produces no effect at all</b>.","o":{"A":"Activates Ca²⁺ channels","B":"Inhibits K⁺ channels","C":"Regulates mRNA","D":"Activates phospholipase C","E":"Interacts with a Gi-protein-coupled receptor"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A membrane ion-channel action.","B":"A membrane ion-channel action.","C":"Correct. Intranuclear receptors are ligand-activated <b>transcription factors</b>: they bind DNA response elements and regulate <b>mRNA</b>, hence protein synthesis. Slow onset, long duration.","D":"The Gq membrane pathway.","E":"A membrane G-protein-coupled pathway."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q17; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM29 SUM1 Q15; BM28 SUM1 Q15 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q19","t":"Pharmacogenomics","s":"In which case should the <b>HLA-B*15:02</b> gene be considered?","lec":"L3B","sl":8,"c":"<b>Carbamazepine → HLA-B*15:02</b> · <b>allopurinol → HLA-B*58:01</b> · <b>abacavir → HLA-B*57:01</b>. The phrase '<b>for the first time</b>' is the examiners' marker that pre-emptive pharmacogenomic screening is the point — after an exposure has already happened, the test no longer prevents anything.","o":{"A":"A patient with trigeminal neuralgia about to be treated with allopurinol","B":"A patient with hyperuricaemia while receiving chemotherapy","C":"A gout patient who has tested positive for HLA-B*15:02 for 6 months","D":"A patient with neural pain about to be treated with carbamazepine for the first time","E":"A patient with epilepsy who is HLA-B*58:01 positive"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The wires are crossed twice: allopurinol pairs with <b>B*58:01</b>, and trigeminal neuralgia is the carbamazepine indication.","B":"That is tumour lysis syndrome — allopurinol and hydration, no HLA typing.","C":"The result is already known and no carbamazepine is planned, so testing changes nothing.","D":"Correct. HLA-B*15:02 screening is indicated <b>before the first dose of carbamazepine</b>, to predict SJS/TEN risk. The allele is common in South-East Asian populations.","E":"B*58:01 is the allopurinol allele, irrelevant to carbamazepine."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q19 — word-for-word, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q20","t":"Receptor regulation","s":"Which up-regulates postsynaptic α1-adrenergic receptors?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Receptors adapt opposite to their stimulation.</b> Chronic agonist → down-regulation and tolerance; chronic antagonist → up-regulation and rebound on withdrawal (the reason a long-term β-blocker must be tapered); hyperthyroidism → up-regulation. Slide 18 is prof DA's hyperthyroidism/cardiac hypertrophy figure.","o":{"A":"Internalisation of the α1 receptor","B":"Chronic use of an α1 agonist","C":"A single dose of an α1 agonist","D":"Hyperthyroidism","E":"Chronic use of an α2 antagonist"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Internalisation is a mechanism of down-regulation.","B":"Chronic agonist exposure causes <b>down</b>-regulation.","C":"A single dose changes nothing durable.","D":"Correct. Thyroid hormone increases adrenergic receptor expression — the basis of the adrenergic symptoms of thyrotoxicosis and of the rapid relief a β-blocker gives.","E":"An α2 antagonist increases noradrenaline release, so the postsynaptic receptor is more stimulated and down-regulates."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q20 (α1); BM29 SUM1 Q17 and BM28 SUM1 Q17 (β1 antagonist version) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q21","t":"Moringa oleifera","s":"A recent paper reported that <b>Moringa oleifera</b> leaf extract enhances the endothelial <b>H₂S</b> synthesis pathway, causing vasorelaxation. Which substance contributes to this effect?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"⚠️ <b>The recalled BM30 option list did not include cystathionine-γ-lyase</b> — it offered GABA in that slot, which cannot be right for an H₂S stem. The list is completed here with the correct enzyme so the item is answerable as intended. Slide 15 is the gasotransmitter slide: <b>NO</b> (NOS → sGC → cGMP), <b>H₂S</b> (CSE), <b>CO</b> (haem oxygenase). <b>Read which gas the stem names, then answer from that gas's own pathway.</b> Moringa items have appeared in four cohorts — this professor examines his own published work.","o":{"A":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase","B":"L-arginine","C":"Adenylyl cyclase","D":"Nitric oxide synthase","E":"Cystathionine-γ-lyase"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase is NO's effector, producing cGMP.","B":"L-arginine is the substrate for <b>nitric oxide</b> synthesis.","C":"Adenylyl cyclase generates cAMP — a different vasodilator pathway.","D":"NOS belongs to the <b>NO</b> pathway. The same group published an NO version of this study, which BM29 and BM28 examined, and there the answer is <b>cyclic GMP</b>.","E":"Correct. <b>Cystathionine-γ-lyase (CSE)</b> is the endothelial enzyme that produces <b>H₂S</b>."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q21 (H₂S → CSE); BM29 SUM1 Q18 and BM28 SUM1 Q18 (NO → cGMP) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q22","t":"Severe cutaneous drug reactions","s":"A man with trigeminal neuralgia was prescribed carbamazepine. One week later he had a flu-like fever, skin inflammation, and 5% of his skin detached. What type of reaction is this?","lec":"L3B","sl":6,"c":"<b>&lt; 10% SJS · 10–30% overlap · &gt; 30% TEN.</b> The partner facts: <b>Nikolsky's sign</b> positive in both; the culprit drugs are carbamazepine, allopurinol, sulfonamides, nevirapine and NSAIDs; and the pharmacogenomic screen for carbamazepine is <b>HLA-B*15:02</b>.","o":{"A":"Delayed urticaria","B":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)","C":"A type A reaction","D":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome","E":"An immediate response"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Urticaria does not detach epidermis.","B":"TEN requires <b>&gt; 30%</b>.","C":"Type A is dose-related and predictable; SJS is type B, immune-mediated.","D":"Correct — <b>5% is under the 10% threshold, so SJS</b>.","E":"An immediate reaction happens within an hour, not after a week."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q22; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM28 SUM1 Q19 — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q23","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q23","t":"NSAID interactions","s":"Which pair of drugs must <b>not</b> be taken together?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"The warfarin interactions are the archetype, and the reason <b>S</b>-warfarin is specified in the stem: warfarin is a racemate whose S-enantiomer is 3–5× more potent and is cleared by <b>CYP2C9</b>. Anything that inhibits CYP2C9 (amiodarone, fluconazole, metronidazole, some NSAIDs) or displaces warfarin from albumin raises the INR. The opposite direction — inducers such as rifampicin, phenobarbital and carbamazepine — causes loss of anticoagulation.","o":{"A":"Ibuprofen and S-warfarin","B":"Ciclosporin and tacrolimus","C":"Paracetamol and vitamin C","D":"Amoxicillin and clavulanate","E":"Metformin and lifestyle modification"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and it is dangerous by <b>two</b> independent routes. Ibuprofen <b>displaces warfarin from albumin</b>, raising the free (active) fraction; and it inhibits <b>CYP2C9</b>, which is the enzyme that clears the more potent <b>S</b>-warfarin enantiomer. On top of that, NSAIDs impair platelet function and damage gastric mucosa. The result is major bleeding risk.","B":"Both are calcineurin inhibitors, so co-prescription is duplication rather than a metabolic hazard.","C":"No clinically significant interaction.","D":"This is a deliberate, beneficial combination — clavulanate is a β-lactamase inhibitor.","E":"Lifestyle modification is recommended alongside metformin, not a contraindication."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q23 asks the same 'serious interaction' idea with a CYP3A4/statin pair","img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q24","t":"Enzyme induction","s":"Which drug <b>reduces the effectiveness</b> of losartan?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"⚠️ <b>Direction is everything.</b> 'Reduces the effect' = <b>INDUCER</b>; 'causes toxicity' = <b>INHIBITOR</b>. Note that every distractor in this item is an inhibitor — so identifying the one inducer answers it without knowing anything about losartan.<br><b>Inducers:</b> rifampicin, phenytoin, phenobarbital, carbamazepine, St John's wort, chronic alcohol, smoking (CYP1A2).<br><b>Inhibitors:</b> the -azoles, macrolides, ritonavir, cimetidine, fluoxetine, amiodarone, grapefruit juice, acute alcohol.<br><i>A wrinkle worth knowing but not needed here: losartan is a prodrug activated by CYP2C9, so an inhibitor would also reduce its effect. The exam wants the induction principle.</i>","o":{"A":"Phenobarbital","B":"Amiodarone","C":"Fluoxetine","D":"Fluconazole","E":"Cimetidine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Phenobarbital is a strong CYP450 inducer.</b> Induction accelerates metabolism, plasma levels fall and the drug stops working. That is the mechanism behind 'reduces the effect'.","B":"Amiodarone is an <b>inhibitor</b> of several CYPs — it would raise levels, not reduce effect.","C":"Fluoxetine is a potent <b>CYP2D6 inhibitor</b>.","D":"Fluconazole is a <b>CYP2C9/3A4 inhibitor</b>.","E":"Cimetidine is a well-known non-selective <b>CYP inhibitor</b> — one of the reasons it fell out of favour."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q24 — the same losartan item appears in both papers, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q37","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q37","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"Which phase of a clinical trial is the <b>first</b> trial in humans?","lec":"L4","sl":14,"c":"⚠️ Phase I uses <b>healthy volunteers</b>, with the notable exception of cytotoxic oncology drugs, where it would be unethical to expose healthy people. The case study on slide 19 is worth remembering as the reason Phase I is taken so seriously: <b>TGN1412</b>, where six healthy volunteers suffered a catastrophic cytokine storm.","o":{"A":"Phase II","B":"Phase I","C":"Phase III","D":"Phase IV","E":"Preclinical"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Phase II is the first study in <b>patients</b> with the disease.","B":"Correct. <b>Phase I</b> is first-in-human: a small study, typically 20–100 <b>healthy volunteers</b>, whose objectives are <b>safety, tolerability, and dose finding</b> (plus human pharmacokinetics). It follows IND authorisation.","C":"Phase III is the large confirmatory trial.","D":"Phase IV is after approval.","E":"Preclinical work is in vitro and in animals — by definition not in humans."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q24; Ax [55-58] — 'healthy normal human volunteer' → Phase I, four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L4_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q38","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"What does <b>Phase II</b> do?","lec":"L4","sl":15,"c":"The distinction candidates most often blur is <b>Phase I dose-finding vs Phase II dose optimisation</b>. Phase I asks 'what dose is <i>tolerated</i>?' in healthy volunteers; Phase II asks 'what dose <i>works best</i>?' in patients. Phase II is also where the highest attrition occurs — most candidates fail here for lack of efficacy.","o":{"A":"Assesses effectiveness and finds the optimal dose in patients","B":"Establishes safety and dose in healthy volunteers","C":"Confirms efficacy in a large population","D":"Detects rare adverse effects after marketing","E":"Tests toxicity in animals"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Phase II</b> is the first exposure of the <b>target patient population</b> — a few hundred people — and its job is preliminary <b>efficacy</b> plus the <b>optimal dose</b> to carry into Phase III.","B":"That is Phase I.","C":"That is Phase III.","D":"That is Phase IV.","E":"That is preclinical."},"also":"BM30 Q37, Q39, Q43 and Formative 1 Q4 — this cohort examined the phases four separate times","img":"slides_p/L4_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q39","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q39","t":"Regulatory pathway","s":"A team has completed preclinical testing and all three trial phases. What comes next?","lec":"L4","sl":22,"c":"The regulatory milestones in order: <b>IND</b> (Investigational New Drug — the gate between preclinical and Phase I) → Phases I, II, III → <b>NDA</b> (New Drug Application) → <b>approval</b> → <b>Phase IV / pharmacovigilance</b>. The two gates, IND and NDA, are what the examiners test — everything else in the pathway hangs off them.","o":{"A":"Lead optimisation","B":"Post-marketing surveillance","C":"Phase II approval","D":"Regulatory (FDA) approval","E":"Preclinical testing"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Lead optimisation belongs to the discovery stage, long before.","B":"Post-marketing surveillance (Phase IV) can only begin <b>after</b> approval — the drug has to be on the market to be surveilled. This is the trap in the item.","C":"There is no such step; phases are not individually 'approved' in that sense.","D":"Correct. After Phase III the sponsor files a <b>New Drug Application (NDA)</b>, and the regulator reviews the entire dossier before granting marketing authorisation.","E":"Preclinical testing was already completed, per the stem."},"img":"slides_p/L4_22.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q40","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q40","t":"Preclinical testing","s":"What is the purpose of a <b>preclinical</b> trial?","lec":"L4","sl":10,"c":"What preclinical toxicology actually measures, and the vocabulary that comes with it: <b>acute toxicity</b> (single dose, LD50) · <b>subacute/subchronic</b> (repeated dosing, 28 days to 90 days) · <b>chronic</b> (6 months to 2 years) · plus <b>carcinogenicity, mutagenicity (Ames test) and teratogenicity</b>. The duration-to-category mapping is asked directly in the Summative II toxicology block, and rests on the same slides.","o":{"A":"To discover drug-drug interactions in patients","B":"To obtain FDA approval","C":"To evaluate safety and the therapeutic effect in animal models before human exposure","D":"To establish the optimal dosage and route in humans","E":"To confirm efficacy in a large population"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Anything 'in patients' is clinical.","B":"Approval comes after the whole clinical programme.","C":"Correct. Preclinical work uses <b>in vitro and in vivo (animal)</b> systems to establish that the compound is active and, above all, that its toxicity profile is acceptable enough to justify a first human dose. It must be conducted under <b>GLP</b>, and it culminates in the IND application.","D":"Human dosing and route are settled from Phase I onward.","E":"Large-population confirmation is Phase III."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q21-22 — 'the most important step before submitting to FDA' and 'GLP'","img":"slides_p/L4_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q43","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q43","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"Which phase recruits a larger patient population to further evaluate efficacy and monitor side effects?","lec":"L4","sl":16,"c":"⚠️ <b>Phase III vs Phase IV is the trap of this whole topic</b>, because both involve large populations and both watch for adverse effects. Use two discriminators:<br>• <b>Before vs after marketing.</b> If the drug is described as already prescribed, on the market, or newly launched → Phase IV.<br>• <b>Is efficacy still being evaluated?</b> If yes → Phase III; Phase IV is about safety, rare events and real-world use.<br>BM30 asked both versions in the same season, so read the stem rather than pattern-matching.","o":{"A":"Phase IV","B":"Phase I","C":"Phase II","D":"Phase III","E":"Preclinical"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Phase IV is also a large population — but it is <b>after marketing</b>, and it does not 'evaluate efficacy' as its purpose. The presence of the word <b>efficacy</b> here is what selects Phase III.","B":"Phase I is small and in healthy volunteers.","C":"Phase II is a few hundred patients and is exploratory rather than confirmatory.","D":"Correct. <b>Phase III</b> takes the drug into hundreds to thousands of patients to confirm efficacy and to detect adverse effects too uncommon to show up in Phase II. This cohort's own Formative 1 asked the identical question in the identical words.","E":"Preclinical is animal work."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM30 Formative 1 Q4 — the same wording, same cohort; BM31 SUM1 Q31","img":"slides_p/L4_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q45","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q45","t":"Motion sickness in Parkinson's disease","s":"A 65-year-old man with <b>Parkinson's disease</b> is being driven to see a doctor. Which drug is appropriate to prevent motion sickness in this patient?","lec":"L5","sl":10,"c":"This item works only if you know both halves: the drug must be <b>first-generation</b> (to cross the BBB and act on the vestibular system) <b>and</b> free of <b>dopamine antagonism</b> (because the patient has Parkinson's). That leaves CPM and dimenhydrinate. The same dopamine-blocking property is why cinnarizine and flunarizine cause <b>drug-induced parkinsonism</b> in the elderly — which BM29's SUM 2 examined from the rational-drug-use angle instead.","o":{"A":"Chlorpheniramine (CPM)","B":"Cinnarizine","C":"Promethazine","D":"Cetirizine","E":"Loratadine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>CPM</b> is a first-generation H1 antihistamine, so it crosses the blood-brain barrier and works on the vestibular pathway — and unlike the two traps below it has <b>no significant dopamine antagonism</b>, so it will not worsen parkinsonism.","B":"<b>Cinnarizine</b> is the main trap. It is effective for motion sickness, but it and flunarizine <b>block dopamine receptors and can cause drug-induced parkinsonism</b> — the lecturer said explicitly to avoid them in Parkinson's disease and in the elderly.","C":"<b>Promethazine</b> is a phenothiazine, and phenothiazines are dopamine antagonists. Same problem.","D":"Cetirizine is second-generation — it barely crosses the BBB, so it does not help motion sickness.","E":"Loratadine is second-generation, for the same reason ineffective here."},"also":"BM29 SUM2 Q41-44 examine long-term cinnarizine/flunarizine harm as an RDU problem","img":"slides_p/L5_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q46","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q46","t":"Second-generation antihistamines","s":"A medical student on the way to an exam develops flushing of the arms and face, a runny nose and sneezing. Which drug should be given?","lec":"L5","sl":11,"c":"<b>The context word decides it.</b> Exam, driving, work, machinery, school → the <b>non-sedating (second-generation)</b> agent: loratadine, cetirizine, levocetirizine, fexofenadine. Motion sickness, vertigo, needing sedation → <b>first-generation</b>: CPM, dimenhydrinate, diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, promethazine, cinnarizine. ⚠️ <b>Terfenadine and astemizole were withdrawn</b> for QT prolongation.","o":{"A":"Diphenhydramine","B":"Chlorpheniramine (CPM)","C":"Cinnarizine","D":"Loratadine","E":"Hydroxyzine"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Diphenhydramine is the most sedating of the class — it is marketed as a sleep aid.","B":"CPM is first-generation and sedating.","C":"Cinnarizine is first-generation, sedating, and indicated for vertigo/motion sickness rather than allergy.","D":"Correct. <b>Loratadine is second-generation and non-sedating</b> — it treats the allergic rhinitis without impairing a student who is about to sit an examination.","E":"Hydroxyzine is markedly sedating."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q43 — identical stem, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q47","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q47","t":"Triptans","s":"A 25-year-old man has a headache on one side, sensitivity to light, nausea and vomiting. Which drug should be used?","lec":"L5","sl":14,"c":"<b>The single most repeated L5 item — five cohorts.</b> Triptans are contraindicated in <b>ischaemic heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension and previous stroke</b>, because 5-HT₁B constriction is not confined to cranial vessels. The mechanism-worded version of the same item (BM31 Q36) asks which action helps, and the answer there is <b>stimulate 5-HT receptors</b>.","o":{"A":"Cinnarizine","B":"Sumatriptan","C":"Betahistine","D":"Montelukast","E":"Cimetidine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Cinnarizine is for vertigo and motion sickness.","B":"Correct. Unilateral headache + photophobia + nausea is <b>migraine</b>, and <b>sumatriptan</b> — a selective <b>5-HT₁B/₁D agonist</b> — is the first-line specific treatment.","C":"Betahistine is for vestibular vertigo and Ménière's disease.","D":"Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist for asthma and allergic rhinitis. ⚠️ The lecturer stressed it is <b>not first-line for asthma</b> — an inhaled steroid is.","E":"Cimetidine is an H2 blocker for acid suppression."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q48 and Q36; BM32 SUM1 L2; BM29 SUM1 Q31 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q48","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q48","t":"Vertigo","s":"A patient has Ménière's disease with vertigo. Which drug should be used?","lec":"L5","sl":8,"c":"Slide 8 is the histamine agonist/analogue slide, and it is where the two trap names live: <b>betahistine</b> (dual-action, for vertigo — the answer) and <b>betazole</b> (H2 agonist, diagnostic only). Note that histamine itself has <b>no therapeutic value</b> and is used only in diagnostic tests. The four receptors: <b>H1</b> allergy, bronchoconstriction, itch, CNS wakefulness · <b>H2</b> gastric acid · <b>H3</b> presynaptic autoreceptor in brain (negative feedback) · <b>H4</b> immune cells — and the lecturer said H4 and the H3 lab tools are <b>not emphasised</b>.","o":{"A":"Betahistine","B":"Cetirizine","C":"Betazole","D":"Nizatidine","E":"Pheniramine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Betahistine</b> is the specific treatment for Ménière's disease and vestibular vertigo — a weak <b>H1 agonist</b> and <b>H3 antagonist</b> ('dual action' on slide 8) that improves inner-ear microcirculation and lowers endolymphatic pressure.","B":"Cetirizine is a second-generation H1 antagonist for allergy.","C":"<b>Betazole</b> is the near-homonym trap — an <b>H2 agonist</b> used only as a diagnostic test of gastric acid secretion.","D":"Nizatidine is an H2 <b>antagonist</b> for peptic ulcer.","E":"Pheniramine is a first-generation H1 antagonist for allergy."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L5 — the 'denied tinnitus' version, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|Q50","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"Q50","t":"Adulterated herbal remedies","s":"A 60-year-old man buys a herbal preparation online to treat pain in both knees. What does its active constituent most likely inhibit?","lec":"L5","sl":19,"c":"Both L5 and L7 return to this Thai clinical problem — steroids adulterated into <b>ยาลูกกลอน</b> and 'ยาผีบอก' herbal remedies, producing dramatic relief followed by <b>iatrogenic Cushing syndrome</b> and adrenal suppression when they are stopped. Pharmacologically, the item is the same phospholipase A2 fact that five cohorts have asked directly.","o":{"A":"Cyclooxygenase","B":"Phospholipase A2","C":"Lipoxygenase","D":"Prostaglandin","E":"Thromboxane A2"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"COX inhibition would be an NSAID. Possible in principle as an adulterant, but the classic adulterant, and the answer here, is a steroid.","B":"Correct, and the point of the item is the Thai public-health context. Herbal preparations sold for joint pain are frequently <b>adulterated with steroids</b> — that is why they work so impressively and why they cause iatrogenic Cushing's. A steroid inhibits <b>phospholipase A2</b>.","C":"Lipoxygenase inhibition is zileuton — not something found in adulterated herbals.","D":"Prostaglandins are products of the pathway, not an enzyme to inhibit.","E":"Thromboxane A2 is likewise a product."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q39; BM29 SUM1 Q32; BM32 SUM2; BM31 SUM2 Q3; BM30 SUM2 Q13 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_19.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|F1-Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"F1-Q1","t":"Route of administration","s":"Which route of administration uses the <b>least</b> amount of medicine to achieve the same plasma concentration?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"Slide 16's particle-size rule is worth carrying alongside the bioavailability table, because inhalation is the route whose delivery varies most: particles <b>&gt; 10 µm</b> deposit in the oropharynx, <b>2–10 µm</b> in the conducting airways, and <b>&lt; 2 µm</b> reach the alveoli. Device choice (nebuliser vs MDI) and technique therefore change bioavailability directly.","o":{"A":"Intravenous","B":"Oral","C":"Inhalation","D":"Subcutaneous","E":"Intramuscular"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. IV bioavailability is <b>100% by definition</b> — the whole dose reaches the systemic circulation — so the smallest dose achieves any given concentration.","B":"Oral needs the <b>largest</b> dose: incomplete absorption plus first-pass metabolism.","C":"Inhalation is fast, but bioavailability varies widely (5–100%) with particle size and technique.","D":"SC is ≥ 75%.","E":"IM is ≥ 75%."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q1 and BM28 SUM1 Q1 ask the same fact inverted — three appearances","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|F1-Q3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"F1-Q3","t":"Potency and efficacy","s":"Two new vasodilators (A and B) are compared with a standard agent (C) in an animal study. On the vasorelaxation dose-response plot, drug B's curve lies <b>left</b> of drug C's, and drug A reaches a <b>lower plateau</b> than drug C. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":8,"c":"Take these figures one word at a time. <b>'Potent' → look left–right.</b> <b>'Effective' / 'efficacy' → look up–down.</b> A drug can be more potent and less effective at once (that is exactly what a high-affinity partial agonist looks like), which is why the examiners' compound statements are answerable only by checking each half separately.","o":{"A":"Drug A is more potent than drug B and more effective than drug C","B":"Drug B is more potent than drug C, and more effective than drug A","C":"Drug B is the least potent","D":"Drug C is the most potent","E":"Drug A is the most effective"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"A is the <i>least</i> effective, since it plateaus lowest — and nothing puts A left of B.","B":"Correct, reading each axis in turn. B left of C = <b>B is more potent</b>. A's plateau below C's means A has the <b>lowest efficacy</b>, so B (which reaches at least C's ceiling) is more effective than A.","C":"B is furthest left, so B is the <b>most</b> potent, not the least.","D":"C is to the right of B, so C is less potent than B.","E":"A has the lowest plateau, so it is the least effective — the exact opposite."},"f":["image"],"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q16; BM31 SUM1 Q15; BM29 SUM1 Q7 ('which drug is most effective and most safe')","img":"slides_p/L3_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|F1-Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"F1-Q6","t":"Competitive antagonism","s":"Drug X is used to treat hypertensive episodes. Tested on isolated rat blood vessels together with phenylephrine, it shifts the phenylephrine dose-response curve to the right <b>in parallel, with the maximum unchanged</b>. What property does drug X have?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"The two antagonist signatures, which every figure item reduces to:<br><b>Competitive</b> — parallel rightward shift, <b>Emax unchanged</b>, surmountable, potency falls, affects <b>potency</b>.<br><b>Non-competitive / irreversible</b> — <b>Emax reduced</b>, insurmountable, affects <b>efficacy</b>.<br>Ax puts it as a one-liner: <i>'the competitive antagonist affects agonist POTENCY, the non-competitive affects agonist EFFICACY.'</i>","o":{"A":"It acts as a vasodilator","B":"It acts as a competitive antagonist","C":"It acts as a reversible agonist","D":"It acts as an uncompetitive antagonist","E":"It acts as a beta-1 receptor antagonist"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Direct vasodilators relax the vessel by themselves. Drug X is doing nothing on its own here — its effect is only visible in the presence of phenylephrine, which is antagonist behaviour.","B":"Correct. A <b>parallel rightward shift with an unchanged maximum</b> is the definition of <b>competitive (surmountable) antagonism</b>: more agonist can still reach the same peak, so only potency has changed.","C":"An agonist would produce a response by itself and would not shift another agonist's curve rightward.","D":"A non-competitive/irreversible antagonist <b>lowers the maximum</b> — insurmountable. The stem says the maximum is unchanged.","E":"Phenylephrine is an <b>α1</b> agonist, so a β1 blocker would not shift its curve at all."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q20 and BM28 SUM1 Q20 — the identical phenylephrine experiment, three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|F2-Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"F2-Q2","t":"Anthracyclines","s":"A 65-year-old man with Hodgkin lymphoma is on the <b>ABVD</b> regimen — doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine and dacarbazine. Which adverse effect may he experience <b>due to doxorubicin</b>?","lec":"L6","sl":9,"c":"<b>ABVD is a gift of a stem</b>, because it contains four drugs with four different signature toxicities and the examiners simply pick one: <b>A</b>driamycin (doxorubicin) → cardiomyopathy · <b>B</b>leomycin → pulmonary fibrosis · <b>V</b>inblastine → myelosuppression (vin<b>C</b>ristine would be neuropathy) · <b>D</b>acarbazine → nausea and myelosuppression. Mnemonic from the lecture: <b>vinCristine → Childhood tumours, vinBlastine → Breast carcinoma.</b>","o":{"A":"Secondary ovarian cancer","B":"Pneumonitis","C":"Hepatic fibrosis","D":"Cardiomyopathy","E":"Skin pigmentation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Secondary malignancy — classically <b>acute leukaemia</b> — follows alkylating agents; the handout flags secondary leukaemia specifically.","B":"Pneumonitis progressing to <b>pulmonary fibrosis</b> is <b>bleomycin</b> — also in this regimen, which is what makes the distractor good. Read which drug the stem names.","C":"Hepatic fibrosis is associated with long-term <b>methotrexate</b>.","D":"Correct. <b>Doxorubicin causes a dose-dependent, cumulative and irreversible cardiomyopathy</b>, mediated by free-radical generation and iron chelation in cardiac muscle. It is why a lifetime cumulative dose limit is observed and why <b>dexrazoxane</b> is used as a cardioprotectant.","E":"Doxorubicin does cause reversible red discolouration of urine, but skin pigmentation is not its characteristic toxicity."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q17 and BM30 SUM2 Q17 use a very similar regimen list for the cyclophosphamide item","img":"slides_p/L6_9.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|F2-Q3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"F2-Q3","t":"Glucocorticoid effects","s":"Which of the following is a pharmacological effect of <b>exogenous glucocorticoids</b>?","lec":"L7","sl":10,"c":"The lecturer's own emphasis — <b>'อันนี้สำคัญนะ ฤทธิ์อะไรได้บ้างนี่สำคัญ'</b> — is that the <b>pharmacological effects list is the spine of the topic</b>, and that both indications and adverse effects are derived from it. Run through it in order: <b>metabolic</b> (gluconeogenesis ↑, protein catabolism ↑, fat redistribution) · <b>anti-inflammatory</b> (phospholipase A2 inhibited, cytokines down) · <b>immunosuppressive</b> (lymphocytes down, neutrophils <i>up</i> through demargination) · <b>mineralocorticoid</b> (Na⁺ and water retained, K⁺ lost). Every distractor in this item is one of those effects stated backwards.","o":{"A":"Increased muscle mass","B":"Inhibition of leukotriene synthesis","C":"Improved wound healing","D":"Hypoglycaemia","E":"Increased excretion of sodium and water"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Glucocorticoids are <b>catabolic</b>: they break protein down, causing muscle wasting and proximal myopathy.","B":"Correct. By inducing lipocortin and inhibiting <b>phospholipase A2</b>, glucocorticoids stop arachidonic acid being liberated — so <b>both</b> prostaglandins and <b>leukotrienes</b> fail to be made. That both branches fall is precisely what distinguishes a steroid from an NSAID.","C":"They <b>impair</b> wound healing — reduced collagen synthesis and fibroblast activity, on top of thin skin and striae.","D":"They cause <b>hyperglycaemia</b> — gluconeogenesis up, peripheral glucose uptake down, which is why steroid-induced diabetes exists.","E":"Their mineralocorticoid activity <b>retains</b> sodium and water (and wastes potassium), producing oedema, hypertension and hypokalaemia."},"img":"slides_p/L7_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q13","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q13","t":"Steroid mechanism","s":"A patient is given <b>prednisone</b> to treat rheumatoid arthritis. What is the mechanism?","lec":"L7","sl":9,"c":"⚠️ <b>Prednisone is a PRODRUG</b> — it must be converted by hepatic 11-β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase to <b>prednisolone</b> to have any effect at all. The lecturer emphasised this: in severe liver disease, prednisolone is given directly. Note that both are intermediate-acting, 12–36 h, with a glucocorticoid:mineralocorticoid potency of <b>4 / 0.8</b> relative to hydrocortisone's 1 / 1.","o":{"A":"17-alpha hydroxylase inhibition","B":"COX inhibition","C":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","D":"11-beta hydroxylase inhibition","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"17-α-hydroxylase inhibition is <b>ketoconazole</b> — also an anti-steroid drug.","B":"COX inhibition is the NSAID mechanism, and blocks only one branch.","C":"Lipoxygenase inhibition is zileuton.","D":"11-β-hydroxylase inhibition is <b>metyrapone</b> — an anti-steroid drug.","E":"Correct — the most repeated L7 item, three cohorts. Steroids induce lipocortin, which inhibits <b>phospholipase A2</b>; arachidonic acid is not released, so neither prostaglandins nor leukotrienes are formed."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q3; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L7_9.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q14","t":"Glucocorticoid adverse effects","s":"A 45-year-old woman has been on long-term glucocorticoid therapy. Which effect would be expected?","lec":"L7","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ Distinguish the two directions carefully, because the examiners ask both in the same paper: <b>excess</b> (Cushingoid — moon face, buffalo hump, hyperglycaemia, hypertension, osteoporosis, thin skin, infection risk, cataract) versus <b>withdrawal/insufficiency</b> (dehydration, hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia, weakness). BM31's paper asked the withdrawal version immediately after this one.","o":{"A":"Lupus-like syndrome","B":"Dehydration","C":"Hypotension","D":"Pituitary tumour","E":"Osteoporosis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Drug-induced lupus belongs to hydralazine, procainamide and isoniazid; steroids treat lupus.","B":"Dehydration belongs to steroid <b>withdrawal</b> and adrenal insufficiency — the opposite state.","C":"Chronic steroids cause <b>hypertension</b> through sodium and water retention.","D":"Exogenous steroid <b>suppresses</b> the HPA axis. A pituitary adenoma causes Cushing <i>disease</i>.","E":"Correct. Long-term steroids inhibit osteoblasts, increase bone resorption and reduce calcium absorption → <b>osteoporosis</b>, with vertebral fracture as the classic complication."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q1 — identical, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L7_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q15","t":"Steroid antagonism","s":"Which combination <b>reduces</b> the effect of a glucocorticoid?","lec":"L7","sl":25,"c":"Two receptors, two blockers — keep them paired: <b>mifepristone blocks the GLUCOCORTICOID receptor</b>; <b>spironolactone and eplerenone block the MINERALOCORTICOID (aldosterone) receptor</b>. Fludrocortisone is in the stem because it has both activities (10 glucocorticoid / <b>125 mineralocorticoid</b>), so the question is really asking which arm is being blocked. The stem says 'glucocorticoid' — so mifepristone.","o":{"A":"Fludrocortisone + spironolactone","B":"Fludrocortisone + NSAIDs","C":"Fludrocortisone + salicylate","D":"Fludrocortisone + mifepristone","E":"Fludrocortisone + aldosterone"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Spironolactone blocks the <b>mineralocorticoid</b> receptor. It would oppose fludrocortisone's salt-retaining action, but not its glucocorticoid effect — a partial answer, and a good distractor.","B":"NSAIDs and steroids both reduce inflammation; combining them increases anti-inflammatory effect — and markedly increases GI ulcer risk, which is a real clinical warning but not a reduction in effect.","C":"Salicylate is an NSAID; same reasoning.","D":"Correct. <b>Mifepristone blocks the glucocorticoid receptor</b>, so it directly opposes any steroid given with it. That is pharmacological antagonism at the same receptor.","E":"Aldosterone is the endogenous mineralocorticoid; adding it reinforces rather than opposes."},"img":"slides_p/L7_25.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q16","t":"Cushing syndrome treatment","s":"A 50-year-old with an <b>adrenal tumour</b> has weight gain, a fat pad, a neck hump, fatigue, decreased muscle mass and thinning skin. Which drug treats this?","lec":"L7","sl":21,"c":"Notice that <b>four of the five options are agonists</b> — recognising which drugs oppose steroid action is the whole skill. Also distinguish the two Cushings: <b>Cushing DISEASE</b> = a <b>pituitary</b> ACTH-secreting adenoma; <b>Cushing SYNDROME</b> = the clinical picture from any cause, most commonly <b>exogenous steroids</b>, and here an adrenal tumour. If a stem named a drug that blocks the <b>receptor</b> instead of synthesis, the answer would be <b>mifepristone</b>.","o":{"A":"Fludrocortisone","B":"Cortisol","C":"Triamcinolone","D":"Ketoconazole","E":"Betamethasone"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Fludrocortisone is a potent <b>mineralocorticoid</b> (10 / 125) used in Addison's disease — the opposite clinical problem.","B":"Cortisol <b>is</b> the hormone in excess — giving more would worsen it.","C":"Triamcinolone is a synthetic glucocorticoid agonist (5 / 0).","D":"Correct. The picture is <b>Cushing syndrome</b> from an adrenal tumour producing excess cortisol. <b>Ketoconazole</b> inhibits adrenal steroid synthesis (17-α-hydroxylase and other CYP steps), lowering cortisol production. It is the only anti-steroid drug offered.","E":"Betamethasone is a long-acting glucocorticoid agonist (25 / 0)."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q4 — ketoconazole's mechanism asked directly, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L7_21.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q17","t":"Haemorrhagic cystitis","s":"A patient is on chemotherapy including <b>cyclophosphamide</b>, bleomycin and vincristine. He has severe bladder pain and bleeding from the bladder. Which drug prevents this?","lec":"L6","sl":5,"c":"⚠️ <b>MESNA is the drug normally taught for this</b> — but it is not offered in BM30's or BM31's option list, and both cohorts key <b>N-acetylcysteine</b>, which the handout does list beside mesna as the alternative sulfhydryl donor. <b>If mesna appears among the options, choose mesna.</b> The protectant map: <b>mesna → cyclophosphamide</b> · <b>dexrazoxane → doxorubicin</b> · <b>amifostine → cisplatin</b> · <b>folinic acid → methotrexate</b>. The lecturer noted that mesna has appeared in the National Licensing exam.","o":{"A":"Methylene blue","B":"Amifostine","C":"N-acetylcysteine","D":"Mannitol","E":"Sugammadex"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Methylene blue treats methaemoglobinaemia, and ifosfamide-induced <b>encephalopathy</b>.","B":"<b>Amifostine</b> protects against <b>cisplatin</b> nephrotoxicity — a phosphatase-activated prodrug and antioxidant, so it helps against any reactive-oxygen generator.","C":"Correct as keyed. Cyclophosphamide's metabolite <b>acrolein</b> is concentrated in urine and injures the bladder urothelium. A sulfhydryl donor inactivates it, and <b>N-acetylcysteine</b> is named alongside mesna on the handout's cyclophosphamide slide.","D":"Mannitol forces a diuresis; used with cisplatin, not for acrolein.","E":"Sugammadex reverses rocuronium — a neuromuscular blocker antidote from a different lecture entirely."},"f":["keydiff"],"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q17 — identical item and identical option list, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_5.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q18","t":"Taxanes","s":"A woman on treatment for advanced breast cancer complains of numbness and tingling in her hands and feet. Which drug most likely caused it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"Two chemotherapy drugs cause the same neuropathy by different routes, and both appear in breast and lymphoma stems: <b>taxanes</b> (paclitaxel, docetaxel) and <b>vincristine</b>. Both act on microtubules, which is exactly why axonal transport suffers.","o":{"A":"Tamoxifen","B":"Doxorubicin","C":"Cyclophosphamide","D":"Docetaxel","E":"Trastuzumab"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Tamoxifen → endometrial carcinoma, thromboembolism.","B":"Doxorubicin → cardiotoxicity.","C":"Cyclophosphamide → haemorrhagic cystitis.","D":"Correct. Taxane-induced <b>peripheral sensory neuropathy</b>, dose-limiting and in a stocking-and-glove distribution.","E":"Trastuzumab → cardiomyopathy."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q18; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q19","t":"Vinca alkaloids","s":"A man with <b>Hodgkin's disease</b> is treated with a mitotic inhibitor. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"<b>Mitotic inhibitor = spindle poison = vinca or taxane.</b> Anything that acts on DNA (alkylators, antimetabolites, topoisomerase inhibitors, anthracyclines) is not a mitotic inhibitor, however plant-derived it may be.","o":{"A":"Epipodophyllotoxin","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Daunorubicin","D":"Gemcitabine","E":"Vincristine"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Epipodophyllotoxins (etoposide) inhibit topoisomerase II — plant-derived, which makes them superficially tempting, but not spindle poisons.","B":"Methotrexate is an antimetabolite (S phase).","C":"Daunorubicin is an anthracycline that intercalates DNA and inhibits topoisomerase II.","D":"Gemcitabine is a pyrimidine antimetabolite.","E":"Correct. Vincristine is a vinca alkaloid — binds β-tubulin, prevents polymerisation, arrests mitosis."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q19; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q20","t":"Platinum compounds","s":"Which adverse effect may result from <b>cisplatin</b> therapy?","lec":"L6","sl":7,"c":"Cisplatin's triad: <b>nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, severe emesis</b>. Note the platinum compounds are the exception among alkylating agents — slide 4 says 'alkylating agents (<b>except</b> platinum-containing compounds)' — because they cross-link DNA without a true alkyl transfer. Carboplatin is less nephrotoxic but more myelosuppressive.","o":{"A":"Haemorrhagic cystitis","B":"Cardiac toxicity","C":"Pulmonary fibrosis","D":"Renal failure","E":"Haemolytic anaemia"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Cyclophosphamide.","B":"Doxorubicin.","C":"Bleomycin.","D":"Correct. <b>Nephrotoxicity is cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicity</b>, from proximal tubular damage. Prevented by hydration, forced diuresis and amifostine.","E":"Not characteristic of cisplatin."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 — two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_7.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q21","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q21","t":"Taxane mechanism","s":"A drug binds <b>α,β-tubulin</b> and inhibits the depolymerisation of the spindle fibre. Which drug is it?","lec":"L6","sl":13,"c":"Say it out loud until it sticks: <b>taxane STABILISES</b> (blocks DEpolymerisation, binds <b>α,β</b>-tubulin); <b>vinca DESTABILISES</b> (blocks polymerisation, binds <b>β</b>-tubulin). ⚠️ The video simplifies both to β-tubulin — <b>answer with the handout</b>.","o":{"A":"Vinblastine","B":"Paclitaxel","C":"Topotecan","D":"Etoposide","E":"Cytarabine"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Vinblastine is a vinca alkaloid — it blocks <b>polymerisation</b>, the mirror image.","B":"Correct. Taxanes bind α,β-tubulin and <b>stabilise</b> the microtubule, blocking depolymerisation.","C":"Topotecan inhibits topoisomerase I.","D":"Etoposide inhibits topoisomerase II.","E":"Cytarabine is a pyrimidine antimetabolite."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q21; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM30|S2-Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM30","n":"S2-Q22","t":"Antimetabolites","s":"A gout patient is prescribed a <b>xanthine oxidase inhibitor</b>, which means the dose of one chemotherapy drug must be reduced. Which one?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"Three cohorts have asked this. The pathway on slide 11: <b>6-mercaptopurine and 6-thioguanine</b> are purine antagonist <b>prodrugs</b> activated by HGPRT and inactivated by <b>xanthine oxidase</b>. Blocking xanthine oxidase therefore raises active drug — a pharmacokinetic interaction with a wide safety margin at stake.","o":{"A":"Bleomycin","B":"6-Mercaptopurine","C":"Mitomycin C","D":"Doxorubicin","E":"Fluorouracil"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Inactivated by bleomycin hydrolase.","B":"Correct. Xanthine oxidase inactivates 6-MP; inhibiting it with allopurinol causes 6-MP to accumulate and produces severe myelosuppression unless the dose is cut.","C":"Hepatically metabolised.","D":"Hepatically metabolised, biliary excretion.","E":"Degraded by dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase."},"also":"BM31 SUM2 Q25; BM32 SUM2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L6_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q1","t":"Dosage forms","s":"A patient is <b>unconscious</b>. Which dosage form is the <b>least</b> appropriate?","lec":"L1","sl":12,"c":"Match the route to the patient's <b>state</b>, not only to the drug. Unconscious, vomiting, or nil-by-mouth → <b>rectal, parenteral, transdermal, inhaled</b>. Slide 12 makes the point about suppositories directly: bullet-shaped solids that melt at body temperature, useful when the oral route is unavailable, with <b>less first-pass effect than oral</b> (rectal bioavailability 30–100%).","o":{"A":"Pill","B":"Transdermal patch","C":"Spray","D":"Sublingual tablet","E":"Suppository"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Swallowing a pill requires an <b>intact, protected airway and a conscious patient</b>. Giving one to an unconscious person risks aspiration and simply will not be swallowed.","B":"A patch is applied to skin and needs no cooperation at all — one of the best options here, though slow.","C":"A nasal or inhaled spray can be administered without swallowing.","D":"A sublingual tablet dissolves under the tongue and is absorbed by the mucosa; it is placed rather than swallowed, so it is usable — though care is still needed.","E":"A <b>suppository</b> is the classic choice for a patient who cannot take anything by mouth: no swallowing, no cooperation, and it partly avoids first-pass metabolism."},"img":"slides_p/L1_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q2","t":"Onset of action","s":"Which method of administration has the <b>longest</b> onset?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"Onset ranking, fastest to slowest: <b>IV → inhaled → sublingual/buccal → IM → SC → oral → rectal → transdermal</b>. Note that <b>transdermal</b> is deliberately the slowest of all — slide 11 describes it as 'usually very slow absorption, used for lack of first-pass effect, prolonged duration of action', which is exactly why a nitroglycerin patch is prophylaxis and a sublingual tablet is rescue.","o":{"A":"Intravenous","B":"Oral","C":"Intramuscular","D":"Sublingual","E":"Buccal"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"IV has the <b>most rapid onset</b> of all — the drug is already in the circulation.","B":"Correct. An oral dose must disintegrate, dissolve, be absorbed across the gut wall and survive first pass before any of it reaches the target — the longest sequence of steps of the routes offered, so the slowest onset.","C":"IM is fast: a well-perfused muscle absorbs quickly.","D":"Sublingual mucosa is thin and richly vascular; onset is within minutes.","E":"Buccal absorption is likewise rapid and avoids first pass."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L1 — the nitroglycerin item is the clinical version of this ranking","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q5","t":"Volume of distribution","s":"What should the Vd of an anticoagulant that acts in the plasma be?","lec":"L2","sl":13,"c":"Anchor the scale to body-fluid anatomy: <b>plasma ≈ 4.8 L (8%)</b>, <b>interstitial ≈ 15 L (25%)</b>, <b>intracellular ≈ 39.6 L (66%)</b>, total body water ≈ 42 L (60% of body weight). Then Vd ≤ 5 L = plasma only; 6–15 L = extracellular; &gt; 15 L = mostly tissue. Large molecular size and high plasma protein binding are what keep a drug in the plasma.","o":{"A":"3 L","B":"10 L","C":"50 L","D":"100 L","E":"500 L"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Plasma volume is about <b>4.8 L</b>, so a drug confined to plasma must have a Vd of that order. Heparin is the worked example on slide 13: Vd 0.06 L/kg ≈ 4 L in a 70 kg adult, because it is a large, highly protein-bound molecule.","B":"10 L is the extracellular-fluid range — plasma plus interstitial fluid (ibuprofen, 0.15 L/kg).","C":"50 L exceeds total body water; the drug is distributing into tissue.","D":"100 L means extensive tissue sequestration.","E":"500 L is a very lipophilic, tissue-bound drug (fluoxetine reaches ~2450 L)."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L2 — the same item, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q6","t":"Therapeutic monitoring","s":"Which drug requires frequent measurement of its concentration in the blood?","lec":"L2","sl":10,"c":"Learn the monitored drugs by name — <b>digoxin, phenytoin, theophylline, warfarin, lithium, aminoglycosides, ciclosporin</b> — and notice how many recur elsewhere in this course: phenytoin is also the zero-order example, theophylline the loading-dose calculation, warfarin the interaction item. That is not a coincidence; narrow-window drugs are where pharmacokinetics has clinical teeth.","o":{"A":"Drug C, which has a high first-pass effect","B":"Drug A, which has a rapid onset","C":"Drug B, which has high bioavailability","D":"Drug D, which has a narrow therapeutic window","E":"Drug E, which has a low MEC"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A high first-pass effect lowers bioavailability, which is compensated by dose selection; it does not by itself demand monitoring.","B":"Rapid onset is convenient, not hazardous.","C":"High bioavailability makes dosing more predictable, if anything.","D":"Correct. A <b>narrow therapeutic window</b> means the gap between the minimum effective and minimum toxic concentrations is small, so a modest change in dose, absorption or clearance can make the drug either useless or dangerous. That is precisely what therapeutic drug monitoring exists for.","E":"A low MEC means only a little drug is needed for effect. If the MTC is far above it, the window is <b>wide</b> — the safest situation."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q7; BM28 SUM1 Q7 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q8","t":"Distribution interactions","s":"What causes <b>kernicterus</b> in a newborn?","lec":"L2","sl":17,"c":"Slide 17 is titled <b>Binding competition between endogenous and drug</b> and uses this exact example. Slide 16 gives the arithmetic that makes it dangerous: a drug that is <b>97% bound</b> has only 3% free; displace a little and the free fraction can <b>double</b>, doubling the active concentration. That is why displacement matters most for highly bound, narrow-window drugs — warfarin, phenytoin, sulfonylureas.","o":{"A":"Two drugs taken together interact pharmacodynamically","B":"The baby's absorption of nutrients is abnormal","C":"A drug taken by the mother spreads into the baby's brain","D":"A drug taken by the mother competes with bilirubin for plasma protein binding","E":"Two drugs taken together interact at metabolism"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"No receptor or common effector is involved; the event is a binding competition in plasma.","B":"Nothing to do with nutrient absorption.","C":"It is the <b>bilirubin</b>, not the drug, that enters the brain. The drug's role is to free it up. This is the near-miss distractor.","D":"Correct. <b>Sulfonamides displace bilirubin from albumin.</b> Free, unbound bilirubin then crosses the neonate's immature blood-brain barrier and deposits in the basal ganglia — kernicterus.","E":"Metabolism is not the site — the competition is at the albumin binding site."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q9 — the same vignette, keyed as a drug-endogenous interaction","img":"slides_p/L2_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q11","t":"Loading dose","s":"A 25-year-old woman weighing 45 kg is given a drug by IV push every 8 hours, aiming for a plasma concentration of 25 mg/L. The drug's Vd is 0.5 L/kg. How much should the first dose be?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"⚠️ The recalled stem gave 'Vd = 60' with no units, which is not consistent with any of the offered answers; the value that reproduces the keyed 562.5 mg is <b>0.5 L/kg</b>, so the stem is reconstructed with that figure. The method is unchanged and is the one to carry into the exam: <b>convert weight-based Vd to litres first</b>, then multiply by the target concentration. Getting the units right at that first step is where most marks in these items are won or lost.","o":{"A":"250.5 mg","B":"100 mg","C":"562.5 mg","D":"800 mg","E":"1000 mg"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"250.5 mg is roughly half the correct value — it would follow from using 0.22 L/kg.","B":"100 mg has no derivation from these numbers.","C":"Correct. Vd = 0.5 L/kg × 45 kg = <b>22.5 L</b>; <b>LD = Vd × Css = 22.5 × 25 = 562.5 mg</b>.","D":"800 mg would need a Vd of 32 L.","E":"1000 mg would need a Vd of 40 L — approximately total body water, not this drug's."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q12; BM28 SUM1 Q10; BM32 SUM1 L2 — a loading-dose calculation in four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q12","t":"Steady state","s":"A 50-year-old man is given 5 mg of a drug intravenously every <b>12 hours</b> for 5 days. The drug's half-life equals the dosing interval. When is steady state reached?","lec":"L2","sl":29,"c":"<b>Time to steady state = 4–5 half-lives, and it does not depend on the dose or the interval.</b> Raising the dose raises the plateau, it does not arrive sooner — which is the entire justification for a <b>loading dose</b> when you cannot wait. Slide 29's accumulation table shows drug building toward about 200% of a single dose, reaching 96.9% of that by the fifth interval.","o":{"A":"108 hours","B":"36 hours","C":"90 hours","D":"60 hours","E":"120 hours"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"108 hours is 9 half-lives.","B":"36 hours is only 3 half-lives — about 87.5% of the way to steady state.","C":"90 hours is 7.5 half-lives; steady state has already been reached well before this.","D":"Correct. Steady state takes about <b>5 half-lives</b>. Here t½ = 12 h, so 5 × 12 = <b>60 hours</b>.","E":"120 hours is the full 5 days of therapy, not the time to steady state."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q12 — 50 mg IV every 6 h, so 5 × 6 h = 30 h = 1 day 6 hours; two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_29.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q15","t":"Nuclear receptors","s":"<b>Dexamethasone</b>, which acts via intranuclear receptors, must have which characteristic?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"The L7 lecture adds a detail worth carrying back here: the glucocorticoid receptor is <b>cytoplasmic, not membrane-bound</b>, and the complex <b>must become a homodimer</b> to enter the nucleus. A complex that cannot dimerise produces <b>no effect at all</b> — the lecturer examined precisely that. Compare with retinoid/thyroid/vitamin-D receptors, which are already nuclear and <b>heterodimerise with RXR</b>.","o":{"A":"Regulates mRNA","B":"Inhibits K⁺ channels","C":"Activates Ca²⁺ channels","D":"Interacts with phospholipase C","E":"Interacts with a Gi-protein-coupled receptor"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The glucocorticoid receptor is <b>cytoplasmic</b>; on binding dexamethasone the complex sheds hsp90, <b>homodimerises</b>, translocates to the nucleus and binds the <b>glucocorticoid response element (GRE)</b>, regulating <b>mRNA</b> transcription.","B":"A membrane ion-channel action.","C":"A membrane ion-channel action.","D":"The Gq membrane pathway.","E":"A membrane G-protein pathway."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q17; BM30 SUM1 Q17; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM28 SUM1 Q15 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q16","t":"Antagonism","s":"Two drugs produce opposite effects on blood pressure while acting at <b>different</b> receptors. What kind of antagonism is this?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ The stem was recorded only as 'จำโจทย์ไม่ได้'; the surviving option list is unmistakably the antagonism-type list, and the concept it belongs to is the one this course examines in three cohorts, so the stem is reconstructed in its standard form.<br><b>Functional and physiological are the same answer</b> — BM31's key says 'physiological', BM28's says 'functional'. The professor flagged this pair as a National Licensing Exam favourite. The Ax bank's version is worth memorising as the archetype: <b>epinephrine reversing histamine-induced bronchoconstriction is physiological antagonism</b>, because the two act at completely different receptors.","o":{"A":"Chemical antagonism","B":"Functional antagonism","C":"Allosteric antagonism","D":"Pharmacokinetic antagonism","E":"Pharmacological antagonism"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Chemical antagonism is a direct reaction between the two molecules — protamine and heparin, or a chelator and a metal. No receptor involved.","B":"Correct. <b>Functional (= physiological) antagonism</b>: opposite effects on the same physiological endpoint, produced through <b>different</b> receptors.","C":"Allosteric antagonism is binding at a <b>different site on the same receptor</b>, changing the agonist's affinity or efficacy.","D":"Pharmacokinetic antagonism is one drug reducing the other's concentration — an inducer, or impaired absorption.","E":"Pharmacological antagonism is competition at the <b>same</b> receptor."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q18; BM28 SUM1 Q16; Ax [57] — three cohorts plus the Ax bank","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q17","t":"Receptor regulation","s":"Which up-regulates the expression of postsynaptic <b>β1</b>-adrenergic receptors?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"<b>Receptors adapt in the direction opposite to their stimulation</b> — agonist down, antagonist up. Three clinical consequences worth carrying: <b>β-blocker withdrawal rebound</b> (taper it); <b>tolerance</b> to chronic agonists such as nitrates or opioids; and <b>hyperthyroidism</b>, which up-regulates adrenergic receptors independently of any drug.","o":{"A":"A single dose of a β1 agonist","B":"Chronic use of a β1 receptor agonist","C":"Chronic use of a β1 receptor antagonist","D":"Loss of GTPase activity","E":"Inhibiting the degradation of a β1 agonist"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A single dose produces no durable change in receptor number.","B":"Chronic agonist exposure causes <b>down</b>-regulation and tolerance.","C":"Correct. Chronic <b>antagonist</b> exposure leaves the receptor understimulated, and the cell compensates by <b>up-regulating</b>. This is why abruptly stopping a long-term β-blocker causes rebound tachycardia, hypertension and angina — and why β-blockers must be tapered.","D":"Loss of GTPase would prolong G-protein signalling — more stimulation, not less.","E":"Preventing an agonist's breakdown increases its concentration, so the receptor sees more agonist and down-regulates."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q17 (β1, same); BM31 SUM1 Q20 and BM30 SUM1 Q20 (α1 version) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q18","t":"Moringa oleifera","s":"A paper reports that <b>Moringa oleifera</b> leaf extract enhances the endothelial <b>nitric oxide</b> synthesis pathway, causing vasorelaxation. What is the likely <b>second messenger</b>?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"⚠️ <b>Two traps in one item.</b> First, which gas: the <b>NO</b> version (BM29, BM28) answers <b>cGMP</b>; the <b>H₂S</b> version (BM31, BM30) answers <b>cystathionine-γ-lyase</b>. Second, <b>enzyme vs messenger</b>: guanylyl cyclase is the enzyme, cGMP is the messenger, and both are offered. Read the noun the question asks for.<br>The full NO pathway: shear stress/ACh → <b>eNOS</b> converts <b>L-arginine</b> → <b>NO</b> → <b>sGC</b> → <b>cGMP</b> → PKG → vasodilation. (Sildenafil works by blocking PDE5, which degrades cGMP.)","o":{"A":"Diacylglycerol","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Cyclic GMP","D":"K⁺ channel","E":"Guanylyl cyclase"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"DAG is the Gq pathway's second messenger, alongside IP₃.","B":"Adenylyl cyclase is an <b>enzyme</b>, and it belongs to the cAMP pathway.","C":"Correct. NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates <b>soluble guanylyl cyclase</b>, which converts GTP to <b>cyclic GMP</b>. cGMP activates protein kinase G → myosin light-chain dephosphorylation → relaxation.","D":"A potassium channel is an <b>effector</b>, not a second messenger.","E":"Guanylyl cyclase is the <b>enzyme that makes</b> cGMP — read the question, which asks for the <b>messenger</b>, not the enzyme. This is the deliberate near-miss."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q18 (NO → cGMP); BM31 SUM1 Q21 and BM30 SUM1 Q21 (H₂S → CSE) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q20","t":"Competitive antagonism","s":"Drug X is used to treat hypertensive episodes. Tested on isolated rat blood vessels together with <b>phenylephrine</b>, it shifts the dose-response curve to the right without lowering the maximum. What property does drug X have?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"Clinically, drug X is <b>phentolamine</b> — a reversible, competitive α-blocker used in hypertensive episodes such as phaeochromocytoma. Contrast it with <b>phenoxybenzamine</b>, which is <b>irreversible</b> and would lower phenylephrine's maximum instead. That pair is the cleanest illustration in the course of competitive vs non-competitive antagonism in a real drug.","o":{"A":"It acts as a β1 receptor antagonist","B":"It acts as a vasodilator","C":"It acts as a reversible agonist","D":"It acts as an uncompetitive antagonist","E":"It acts as a competitive antagonist"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Phenylephrine is an <b>α1</b> agonist — a β1 blocker would not shift its curve.","B":"A direct vasodilator would relax the vessel on its own. Drug X only shows itself in the presence of phenylephrine.","C":"An agonist produces a response by itself and would not shift another agonist's curve right.","D":"Non-competitive/uncompetitive antagonism <b>depresses the maximum</b>. The maximum is unchanged.","E":"Correct. <b>Parallel rightward shift + unchanged maximum = competitive antagonism</b>. The antagonist can be out-competed by more agonist, so the ceiling is still reachable — 'surmountable'."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q20; BM30 Formative 1 Q6 — the identical experiment, three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q22","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q22","t":"Good Laboratory Practice","s":"What does the FDA require researchers to use for <b>preclinical laboratory studies</b>?","lec":"L4","sl":10,"c":"Map each code to its stage: <b>GLP → preclinical laboratory and animal studies</b> · <b>GCP → clinical trials</b> · <b>GMP → manufacturing</b>. The regulatory gates that sit between them are the <b>IND</b> (preclinical → Phase I) and the <b>NDA</b> (Phase III → approval). BM32's paper additionally asked the <b>ICH-GCP E6(R2) vs E6(R3)</b> difference — content BM33 dropped, so treat it as lower priority.","o":{"A":"GCP","B":"GLP","C":"GMP","D":"GPP","E":"GTO"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"<b>Good Clinical Practice</b> governs trials in humans (the ICH-GCP E6 guideline).","B":"Correct. <b>Good Laboratory Practice</b> governs non-clinical safety studies — how they are planned, performed, recorded and reported — so that the toxicity data supporting a first-in-human dose can be trusted.","C":"<b>Good Manufacturing Practice</b> governs production quality.","D":"Good Pharmacy Practice concerns pharmacy services, not research.","E":"Not a recognised standard in this context."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q29 — the FDA's role in approval, the same regulatory block","img":"slides_p/L4_10.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q24","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q24","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"What does <b>Phase 1</b> do?","lec":"L4","sl":14,"c":"A two-word summary per phase makes these items automatic: <b>I = safety + dose</b> (healthy volunteers) · <b>II = efficacy + optimal dose</b> (patients) · <b>III = confirmation + rarer adverse effects</b> (large, expensive) · <b>IV = surveillance</b> (after marketing). This cohort's paper asked the phases at least twice, and every other cohort's did too.","o":{"A":"Establishes efficacy and side effects","B":"Establishes safety and efficacy","C":"Establishes safety and dosage","D":"Establishes efficacy and monitoring","E":"Determines whether the drug is harmful to animals"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Efficacy plus adverse effects in numbers is <b>Phase III</b>.","B":"Efficacy first appears in <b>Phase II</b>.","C":"Correct. <b>Phase I = safety + dose</b>, in 20–100 <b>healthy volunteers</b>, together with the first human pharmacokinetics. Efficacy is not the question at this stage.","D":"Ongoing monitoring after marketing is <b>Phase IV</b>.","E":"Animal toxicity is <b>preclinical</b>."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q37; Ax [55-58] — 'healthy normal human volunteer' → Phase I, four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L4_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q31","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q31","t":"Triptans","s":"A patient has a headache on one side, sees flashes of light, and has nausea and vomiting. Which drug is used?","lec":"L5","sl":14,"c":"<b>Five cohorts — the most repeated L5 item in the bank.</b> The ladder: paracetamol → NSAID → <b>triptan (first-line specific)</b> → <b>ergotamine (rescue only)</b>. Triptans are contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease and uncontrolled hypertension. Ergotamine is combined with <b>caffeine</b> because it is poorly absorbed from the gut.","o":{"A":"Ibuprofen","B":"Ergotamine","C":"Sumatriptan","D":"Cetirizine","E":"Promethazine"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Ibuprofen sits on the ladder as step two and can help a mild attack, but the aura-plus-vomiting picture is the triptan stem.","B":"<b>Ergotamine</b> is the trap and it is offered in almost every version of this item. It is <b>rescue only</b> — non-selective, causing intense and prolonged vasoconstriction, and with overuse, ergotism (cold, blackened digits). Never for prophylaxis.","C":"Correct. Unilateral headache with visual aura, nausea and vomiting is <b>migraine with aura</b>, and the <b>triptans are first-line specific therapy</b> — selective 5-HT₁B/₁D agonists.","D":"Cetirizine is a second-generation antihistamine for allergy.","E":"Promethazine is a first-generation antihistamine with antiemetic action — it might help the vomiting, but it does not treat the migraine."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q48 and Q36; BM30 SUM1 Q47; BM32 SUM1 L2 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q32","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q32","t":"Steroid mechanism","s":"Drug X reduces inflammation. It reduces <b>arachidonic acid, prostaglandins and leukotrienes</b>. What is drug X?","lec":"L5","sl":19,"c":"<b>Count the branches in the stem and the answer follows.</b> Prostaglandins <i>only</i> → NSAID (COX). Leukotrienes <i>only</i> → zileuton (lipoxygenase) or montelukast (LT receptor). <b>Both, plus arachidonic acid itself → steroid</b> (phospholipase A2). <b>AERD</b> — the lecturer said 'นักศึกษาโจทย์ไป' about it — is the clinical proof of the shunt: aspirin triggers asthma precisely because blocking COX pushes substrate down the leukotriene pathway.","o":{"A":"Prednisolone","B":"Aspirin","C":"Naproxen","D":"Celecoxib","E":"Paracetamol"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct, and the giveaway is that <b>all three</b> fall. Only a drug acting <b>above</b> arachidonic acid — at <b>phospholipase A2</b> — can reduce AA itself and therefore both downstream branches. That is a glucocorticoid.","B":"Aspirin inhibits COX <b>below</b> arachidonic acid, so AA is unchanged and leukotrienes are <b>unaffected or even increased</b> as substrate is shunted into the lipoxygenase branch. That shunt is the mechanism of <b>aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD)</b>.","C":"Naproxen is a non-selective COX inhibitor — same argument.","D":"Celecoxib is COX-2 selective; even narrower.","E":"Paracetamol is a weak, largely central COX inhibitor (the COX-3 story the lecturer mentioned) with very little peripheral anti-inflammatory effect."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q39; BM30 SUM1 Q50; BM32 SUM2; BM31 SUM2 Q3; BM30 SUM2 Q13 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_19.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q33","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q33","t":"5-HT3 antagonists","s":"A 70-year-old cancer patient has had chemotherapy. Which drug reduces the resulting vomiting?","lec":"L5","sl":16,"c":"The serotonin receptor map the lecturer emphasised: <b>5-HT₁</b> → triptans, migraine · <b>5-HT₁A</b> → buspirone, anxiety · <b>5-HT₂</b> → ketanserin, and the antipsychotics · <b>5-HT₃</b> → <b>ondansetron, granisetron</b>, emesis · <b>5-HT₄</b> → cisapride, tegaserod, prokinetic. Note that <b>fluoxetine</b> (an SSRI) also comes from this lecture, and that the <b>LSD/serotonin-euphoria digression is explicitly NOT examined</b> — 'ไม่มีข้อสอบนะคะ เล่าเฉยๆ'.","o":{"A":"Ondansetron","B":"Cisapride","C":"Buspirone","D":"Cimetidine","E":"Cinnarizine"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Ondansetron</b> is a <b>5-HT₃ antagonist</b>. Chemotherapy damages gut enterochromaffin cells, releasing serotonin onto vagal afferents and the chemoreceptor trigger zone; blocking 5-HT₃ interrupts that.","B":"Cisapride is a 5-HT₄ <b>agonist</b> and prokinetic, withdrawn in many countries for QT prolongation.","C":"Buspirone is a 5-HT₁A partial agonist used as an anxiolytic.","D":"Cimetidine is an H2 blocker.","E":"Cinnarizine is a first-generation antihistamine for <b>vestibular</b> nausea — the wrong pathway."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q38; BM30 Formative 1 Q5 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q34","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q34","t":"Prostaglandin analogues","s":"Which drug can be used to <b>induce labour</b>?","lec":"L5","sl":21,"c":"Prostaglandins in obstetrics, held as opposites: <b>PGE₁ analogue (misoprostol) → contracts the uterus, induces labour</b>; <b>NSAID (indomethacin) → inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, suppresses labour and closes the ductus</b>. And the ductus pair: <b>alprostadil keeps it open, indomethacin closes it</b>. The lecturer specifically named misoprostol and alprostadil as emphasised drugs.","o":{"A":"Celecoxib","B":"Alprostadil","C":"Indomethacin","D":"Misoprostol","E":"Ondansetron"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Celecoxib is a COX-2 inhibitor; NSAIDs are avoided in late pregnancy.","B":"<b>Alprostadil</b> is also PGE₁, but its clinical uses are <b>maintaining a patent ductus arteriosus</b> in duct-dependent congenital heart disease, and erectile dysfunction. Both drugs are emphasised in the lecture, so learn them as a contrasting pair.","C":"Indomethacin is an NSAID — it inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, so it <b>closes</b> a patent ductus and is used as a <b>tocolytic</b> to suppress labour. The exact opposite.","D":"Correct. <b>Misoprostol</b> is a <b>PGE₁ analogue</b>. It stimulates uterine contraction and ripens the cervix, so it induces labour — and it is for exactly this reason <b>contraindicated in pregnancy</b> when used for its other indication, preventing NSAID-induced gastric ulcers.","E":"Ondansetron is an antiemetic."},"img":"slides_p/L5_21.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|Q35","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"Q35","t":"Angiotensin","s":"A woman is examined and the doctor prescribes <b>enalapril</b>. What disease does she have?","lec":"L5","sl":24,"c":"<b>Angiotensin</b> is one of the peptide autacoids the lecturer flagged as emphasised. The cascade: angiotensinogen (an α2-globulin) → <b>renin</b> → angiotensin I → <b>ACE</b> → <b>angiotensin II</b> → AT₁ receptor → vasoconstriction + aldosterone release. <b>ACE inhibitors (-pril) cause a dry cough</b>, because ACE also degrades <b>bradykinin</b> — which is why the ARBs (<b>-sartan</b>) exist as an alternative. Note that BM29's own SUM 2 asked the mirror question: a 60-year-old with borderline readings and no history should be <b>followed up without medication</b>, not started on enalapril.","o":{"A":"Migraine","B":"Vertigo","C":"Asthma","D":"Hypertension","E":"Inflammation"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Migraine → triptan.","B":"Vertigo → betahistine.","C":"Asthma → inhaled corticosteroid, β2 agonist, montelukast.","D":"Correct. <b>Enalapril is an ACE inhibitor</b>, used for hypertension (and heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy). Blocking ACE reduces angiotensin II — less vasoconstriction, less aldosterone — so blood pressure falls.","E":"Inflammation → NSAID or steroid."},"also":"BM29 SUM2 Q33 — the RDU version, where prescribing enalapril is the WRONG answer","img":"slides_p/L5_24.webp"},{"id":"P|BM29|S2-Q38","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM29","n":"S2-Q38","t":"NSAID choice","s":"A man with a history of <b>transient ischaemic attack</b> takes low-dose aspirin. He now has bilateral knee pain from osteoarthritis and needs an NSAID. Which is most appropriate?","lec":"L5","sl":20,"c":"Two separate reasons make this item's answer robust, and BM28 asked it as a short-answer question requiring both: (1) <b>coxibs and diclofenac raise thrombotic risk</b> — wrong for a patient who has already had a TIA; (2) <b>ibuprofen interferes with aspirin's antiplatelet action</b> by competing for the same site on platelet COX-1. Naproxen avoids both problems. If ibuprofen must be used, it should be taken at least 2 hours <i>after</i> the aspirin.","o":{"A":"Naproxen","B":"Celecoxib","C":"Diclofenac","D":"Etoricoxib","E":"Ibuprofen"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Naproxen carries the most reassuring cardiovascular profile</b> of the NSAIDs — the lecturer said so explicitly off-slide, calling it the drug of choice for a patient at cardiovascular risk and in the elderly.","B":"<b>Celecoxib</b> is COX-2 selective, and COX-2 selectivity <b>increases</b> thrombotic risk: it suppresses antithrombotic prostacyclin while leaving platelet thromboxane intact. Contraindicated here.","C":"<b>Diclofenac</b> now carries thrombotic reports of its own and is best avoided in this patient.","D":"<b>Etoricoxib</b> is another coxib — same objection as celecoxib.","E":"<b>Ibuprofen</b> has a second, specific problem: it <b>competes with aspirin</b> for the platelet COX-1 channel and can <b>block aspirin's irreversible antiplatelet effect</b>, undoing the very protection the TIA patient depends on."},"also":"BM28 SUM1 Q34 (short answer: why can ibuprofen, diclofenac, celecoxib and etoricoxib not be used?); BM32 SUM 2 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L5_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q1","t":"Route of administration","s":"Which route needs the <b>largest</b> amount of drug to achieve a given plasma concentration?","lec":"L2","sl":11,"c":"<b>Dose required ∝ 1/bioavailability.</b> Largest → oral; smallest → IV. Three cohorts have asked this, and BM30 asked it in both directions in the same exam season, so read whether the stem says 'largest' or 'least'.","o":{"A":"Intravenous","B":"Inhalation","C":"Intramuscular","D":"Oral","E":"Sublingual"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"IV is 100% by definition and therefore needs the <b>smallest</b> dose.","B":"Inhaled drug reaches the pulmonary veins directly, with no first pass.","C":"IM bioavailability is ≥ 75%.","D":"Correct. Oral bioavailability is the lowest of these — incomplete absorption plus <b>first-pass metabolism</b> — so the largest dose is needed.","E":"Sublingual bypasses first pass through systemic venous drainage."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q1 (largest → oral); BM30 Formative 1 Q1 (least → IV) — three appearances","img":"slides_p/L2_11.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q2","t":"Dosage forms","s":"Which statement about <b>solid</b> dosage forms is <b>false</b>?","lec":"L1","sl":9,"c":"Slide 9's two-column comparison: <b>liquid</b> — better for patients who have trouble swallowing, <b>faster absorption</b>, easier dose adjustment; but less stable and less accurate to measure. <b>Solid</b> — high stability, accurate dose, easy to carry; but slower and harder to swallow. The examiners like negative stems here, so read for the word <b>false</b> or <b>not</b>.","o":{"A":"They allow accurate dosing","B":"They are rapidly absorbed","C":"They are stable","D":"They are bulky to carry in large numbers","E":"They are the most common dosage form"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"True. Accurate, reproducible dosing is a genuine advantage of solids.","B":"Correct — this is the false statement. A solid must <b>disintegrate and dissolve</b> before any drug can be absorbed, which makes it <b>slower</b> than a liquid. Slide 9 lists 'faster absorption' among the advantages of <b>liquid</b> dosage forms, not solid ones.","C":"True. High stability is the reason solids dominate.","D":"True — a stated disadvantage.","E":"True. Slide 11 calls solids 'the most common form due to high stability, ease of administration and accurate dosing'."},"f":["completed"],"img":"slides_p/L1_9.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q3","t":"Infusion reactions","s":"After an IV bolus of a drug, the patient develops a red rash over the face and neck. Which drug?","lec":"L1","sl":15,"c":"Note the distinction the item rests on: red man syndrome is <b>not an allergy</b> — it is direct histamine release, rate-dependent, and it does not contraindicate the drug. The 'never IV push' list on slide 15 is worth naming: <b>vancomycin, potassium chloride, phenytoin</b> and concentrated electrolytes. <b>IV push vs IV drip</b> is examined in its own right.","o":{"A":"Cloxacillin","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Vancomycin","D":"Phenytoin","E":"Furosemide"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Cloxacillin can cause true penicillin allergy, presenting with urticaria and anaphylaxis rather than isolated flushing.","B":"Rapid IV KCl causes <b>fatal arrhythmia</b>, not a rash.","C":"Correct — <b>red man syndrome</b>. Vancomycin infused too quickly triggers direct, non-IgE-mediated <b>mast-cell histamine release</b>, producing flushing of the face, neck and upper torso. Slow the infusion and pre-treat with an antihistamine.","D":"Rapid IV phenytoin causes hypotension and arrhythmia.","E":"Rapid IV furosemide causes ototoxicity."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q2 — two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L1_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q4","t":"Routes of administration","s":"A <b>suppository</b> is administered by which route?","lec":"L1","sl":13,"c":"The route taxonomy on slide 13: <b>Enteral</b> — oral, sublingual, buccal, <b>rectal</b>. <b>Parenteral</b> — IV, IM, SC, ID, IP, IA, intrathecal. <b>Other</b> — inhalation, transdermal, topical. The catch is that <b>sublingual and rectal are enteral yet still escape first pass</b>, so 'enteral' and 'subject to first-pass metabolism' are not synonyms.","o":{"A":"Inhalational","B":"Parenteral","C":"Enteral","D":"Topical","E":"Transdermal"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Inhalation delivers to the lungs.","B":"Parenteral means <i>beside</i> the gut — injection routes: IV, IM, SC, ID, IP, IA, intrathecal.","C":"Correct. <b>Enteral</b> means via the gastrointestinal tract — which includes the <b>rectum</b>. Slide 13 defines enteral as 'feeding by the digestion process of the gastrointestinal tract', and rectal delivery is listed under it.","D":"Topical is applied to a surface for <b>local</b> effect; a suppository can act locally but is classified by its route, which is enteral.","E":"Transdermal is through intact skin for systemic effect."},"img":"slides_p/L1_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q5","t":"Pharmacokinetics vs pharmacodynamics","s":"Which of the following describes a <b>pharmacodynamic</b> rather than a pharmacokinetic event?","lec":"L2","sl":3,"c":"Slide 3 states it in one line: <b>Pharmacokinetics = what the BODY does to the DRUG</b> (ADME); <b>Pharmacodynamics = what the DRUG does to the BODY</b> (receptors, effects, dose-response). ⚠️ The Ax bank records students getting the direction backwards on exactly this item, so rehearse the sentence rather than the abbreviation. Four of the five options here are ADME steps — spotting the one that is an <i>effect</i> is the whole task.","o":{"A":"A drug is absorbed from the GI tract into the blood","B":"A drug increases the expression of a protein","C":"A drug is conjugated with sulfate and becomes more water-soluble","D":"A water-insoluble drug is excreted in the faeces","E":"A drug is distributed to the site where it will act"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Absorption — the body acting on the drug. Pharmacokinetics.","B":"Correct. Changing protein expression is the drug <b>acting on the body</b> — an effect. That is pharmacodynamics.","C":"Phase II conjugation — metabolism. Pharmacokinetics.","D":"Excretion. Pharmacokinetics.","E":"Distribution. Pharmacokinetics."},"f":["completed"],"also":"Ax [58] — 'pharmacokinetics is the effect of ___, pharmacodynamics is the effect of ___'","img":"slides_p/L2_3.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q6","t":"CYP450","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> related to CYP450 activity?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"<b>F = Fa × Fg × Fh</b> — memorise the three terms and this item answers itself: <b>Fa</b> fraction absorbed across the gut wall (physicochemical, <i>not</i> CYP), <b>Fg</b> fraction escaping <b>gut wall</b> CYP metabolism, <b>Fh</b> fraction escaping <b>hepatic</b> metabolism (= 1 − EH). Intestinal CYP3A4 is why grapefruit juice raises oral levels of 3A4 substrates.","o":{"A":"Bioavailability","B":"Cmax","C":"Percentage absorption","D":"First-pass effect","E":"Duration of action"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Bioavailability is directly reduced by CYP-mediated gut and hepatic metabolism.","B":"Cmax falls when first-pass metabolism is heavier.","C":"Correct. The <b>fraction of the dose that crosses the gut wall</b> is determined by the drug's physicochemical properties, the dosage form and gut transit — all of which happen before any metabolising enzyme is involved. In the bioavailability equation F = Fa × Fg × Fh, absorption is <b>Fa</b>, the only term that is not enzyme-dependent.","D":"The first-pass effect <b>is</b> CYP metabolism.","E":"Duration of action depends on how fast the drug is eliminated, and for most drugs that means CYP-mediated metabolism."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q8 — the same item with 'Phase 1 metabolism' in the last slot, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_8.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q8","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q8","t":"Blood-brain barrier","s":"A drug normally cannot cross the blood-brain barrier. In which patient might it do so?","lec":"L2","sl":14,"c":"Slide 14 covers the special barriers. The BBB excludes drugs by <b>tight junctions plus efflux transporters (P-glycoprotein)</b> and lets through only small, lipophilic, un-ionised molecules. Two consequences the exam uses: <b>inflammation opens it</b> (which is why penicillin works in meningitis), and the <b>neonatal BBB is immature</b> (which is why displaced bilirubin causes kernicterus).","o":{"A":"A patient with diabetes","B":"A pregnant woman","C":"A patient with hypertension","D":"A patient with inflammatory encephalopathy","E":"A patient with heart failure"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Chronic diabetes has subtle effects on the BBB but does not make it permeable to excluded drugs.","B":"Pregnancy concerns the <b>placental</b> barrier, a different structure. The mother's BBB is intact.","C":"Hypertension does not open the BBB, except in the extreme of hypertensive encephalopathy.","D":"Correct. The BBB depends on <b>tight junctions</b> between brain capillary endothelial cells. <b>Inflammation (meningitis, encephalitis) disrupts those junctions</b>, so drugs that are normally excluded — penicillins are the classic example — reach therapeutic concentrations in CSF.","E":"Heart failure alters perfusion and clearance, not barrier integrity."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q9 and BM29 SUM1 Q8 — the kernicterus items depend on the same immature-barrier fact","img":"slides_p/L2_14.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q9","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q9","t":"First-order elimination","s":"A woman takes 10 times the usual dose of a drug. <b>25 hours</b> later, 97% has been excreted. What is the half-life?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"⚠️ Note the deliberate distraction: the stem says <b>10 times the usual dose</b>, which changes nothing at all under first-order kinetics — the same <i>fraction</i> is removed per unit time whatever the level. (It would matter for a <b>zero-order</b> drug such as phenytoin, aspirin or ethanol, where a saturated enzyme means a small dose rise causes a disproportionate level rise.) 97% → <b>5 half-lives</b>, always.","o":{"A":"10 hours","B":"2 hours","C":"8 hours","D":"5 hours","E":"15 hours"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"10 hours gives only 2.5 half-lives ≈ 82%.","B":"2 hours would be 12.5 half-lives.","C":"8 hours is the answer to the <b>40-hour</b> version of this item, which BM32 and BM30 asked. Read the number.","D":"Correct. 97% eliminated = <b>5 half-lives</b>; 25 ÷ 5 = <b>5 hours</b>. Note that the overdose is irrelevant — in first-order kinetics the half-life is independent of the starting concentration.","E":"15 hours is under 2 half-lives."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L2 and BM30 SUM1 Q14 (40 h → 8 h) — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q10","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q10","t":"Loading dose","s":"A 35-year-old man weighing 70 kg comes to the emergency department with severe asthma. The doctor wants to give one dose of <b>theophylline</b>. How much?<br><i>Css = 15 mg/L · CL = 2.8 L/h · Vd = 35 L · F = 1 · t½ = 5 h</i>","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"The identical numbers appear in BM30's paper two cohorts later, which tells you something about how these papers are assembled. The habit that protects you: read for <b>'one dose' / 'a single dose' / 'immediately'</b> (→ loading dose, uses <b>Vd</b>) versus <b>'infusion rate' / 'to maintain' / 'per hour'</b> (→ maintenance, uses <b>CL</b>), and then ignore every parameter the chosen formula does not need.","o":{"A":"525 mg","B":"320 mg","C":"36.8 mg","D":"42 mg","E":"210 mg every 5 h"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. One dose to reach a target level is a loading dose: <b>LD = Vd × Css = 35 × 15 = 525 mg</b>.","B":"Does not follow from these numbers.","C":"Far too small for a loading dose.","D":"42 mg is <b>Css × CL</b> — the maintenance infusion rate per hour, the answer to a different question.","E":"A maintenance regimen; the stem asks for a single dose."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q12 — the same numbers, two cohorts; BM32 SUM1 L2; BM29 SUM1 Q11","img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q11","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q11","t":"Paracetamol overdose","s":"Which of the following is the antidote for <b>paracetamol</b>?","lec":"L2","sl":20,"c":"Slide 20 draws paracetamol metabolism as the course's worked example of <b>biotransformation producing toxicity</b>: at therapeutic dose, conjugation with <b>glucuronate and sulfate</b> dominates; in overdose those saturate, <b>CYP2E1</b> generates <b>NAPQI</b>, glutathione is exhausted, and hepatic necrosis follows. Slide 21's safe-use rules are examinable too: <b>10–15 mg/kg per dose</b>, maximum <b>1,000 mg per dose and 5,000 mg per day</b>. Chronic alcohol induces CYP2E1 and depletes glutathione, which is why alcoholics are at risk at lower doses.","o":{"A":"Mercapturic acid","B":"Atropine","C":"Glutathione","D":"N-acetylcysteine","E":"Sulfonamide"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"<b>Mercapturic acid</b> is the harmless end-product of the glutathione conjugation pathway — a downstream metabolite, not a treatment.","B":"Atropine is the antidote for organophosphate/muscarinic poisoning.","C":"<b>Glutathione</b> is the substance that must be restored — but it is not given as a drug, because it is poorly absorbed and does not enter hepatocytes. NAC is given as its <b>precursor</b>. This is the intended near-miss.","D":"Correct. In overdose the glucuronidation and sulfation pathways saturate, so more paracetamol is shunted through <b>CYP2E1</b> to the toxic metabolite <b>NAPQI</b>. Hepatic glutathione is consumed detoxifying it and, once depleted, NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins and causes necrosis. <b>N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione</b>, and is most effective within 8 hours.","E":"Sulfonamide is unrelated (and is the drug in the kernicterus item)."},"img":"slides_p/L2_20.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q12","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q12","t":"Steady state","s":"A 50-year-old patient is given 50 mg of drug X intravenously every <b>6 hours</b> for 5 days. Its half-life equals the dosing interval. When does the plasma concentration reach steady state?","lec":"L2","sl":29,"c":"<b>4–5 half-lives to steady state, and neither the dose nor the interval changes that.</b> The most common error in these items is arithmetic rather than pharmacology, so convert to hours first, multiply by 5, then convert back to days and hours. The same reasoning applies in reverse: after stopping a drug, it takes 4–5 half-lives to wash out.","o":{"A":"15 hours","B":"2 days","C":"2 days and 3 hours","D":"3 days","E":"1 day and 6 hours"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"15 hours is only 2.5 half-lives — about 82% of the way there.","B":"2 days is 48 h — 8 half-lives, comfortably past steady state.","C":"2 days 3 hours is 51 h.","D":"3 days is 72 h, or the whole course rather than the time to plateau.","E":"Correct. Steady state ≈ <b>5 half-lives</b>. t½ = 6 h, so 5 × 6 = <b>30 hours = 1 day 6 hours</b>."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q12 — 12-hourly dosing, so 60 h; two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_29.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q14","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q14","t":"Second messengers","s":"Which is involved in hepatic glycogenolysis?","lec":"L3","sl":13,"c":"This is the same item as the 'elevation of cAMP' version, worded one step upstream — <b>activation of adenylyl cyclase</b> is what <i>causes</i> the cAMP rise. The examiners move freely along the chain (receptor → G protein → enzyme → second messenger → kinase → effect), so know the whole sequence rather than one link: <b>β receptor → Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA → glycogen phosphorylase</b>.","o":{"A":"Activation of phospholipase C","B":"Activation of adenylyl cyclase","C":"Production of cGMP","D":"Inhibition of protein kinase A","E":"Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"PLC is the Gq pathway (IP₃/DAG/Ca²⁺). It contributes through α1 receptors in hepatocytes, but the keyed pathway is cAMP.","B":"Correct. β-adrenoceptor → <b>Gs</b> → <b>adenylyl cyclase activated</b> → cAMP ↑ → protein kinase A → glycogen phosphorylase → glycogen broken down.","C":"cGMP is the <b>nitric oxide</b> pathway, producing vasodilation.","D":"PKA is the <b>effector</b> of the cAMP pathway — inhibiting it would block glycogenolysis.","E":"Inhibiting adenylyl cyclase is the Gi pathway, the wrong direction."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q16; BM30 SUM1 Q15; BM32 SUM1; BM29 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_13.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q15","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q15","t":"Nuclear receptors","s":"Which characteristic must a drug acting via a <b>nuclear</b> receptor have?","lec":"L3","sl":16,"c":"The four receptor superfamilies, ordered by speed — this is the frame every one of these items sits in:<br><b>1. Ligand-gated ion channels</b> — milliseconds (nicotinic ACh, GABA-A)<br><b>2. G-protein-coupled receptors</b> — seconds (adrenergic, muscarinic, histamine) — <i>the largest drug-target class</i><br><b>3. Enzyme-linked receptors</b> — minutes to hours (insulin, growth factors)<br><b>4. Nuclear receptors</b> — <b>hours</b> (steroids, thyroid hormone, retinoids, vitamin D)<br>If a stem mentions transcription, mRNA or a slow onset, it is family 4.","o":{"A":"It interacts with a G protein","B":"It regulates mRNA","C":"It interacts with phospholipase C","D":"It opens a ligand-gated ion channel","E":"It acts within milliseconds"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"G proteins serve <b>membrane</b> receptors.","B":"Correct. Nuclear receptors are ligand-activated <b>transcription factors</b>: they bind DNA and regulate <b>mRNA</b> transcription.","C":"Phospholipase C is the Gq membrane effector.","D":"Ligand-gated ion channels are membrane receptors acting in milliseconds.","E":"Speed is the giveaway in the other direction: nuclear receptors act over <b>hours</b>, because new protein must be synthesised. Ion channels are the millisecond receptors."},"f":["completed"],"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q17; BM30 SUM1 Q17; BM32 SUM1 L3; BM29 SUM1 Q15 — five cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_16.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q16","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q16","t":"Antagonism","s":"Drug A lowers blood pressure, drug B raises it. What kind of interaction is this?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ <b>BM28's key says 'functional' and BM31's says 'physiological' — they are the same thing.</b> The professor flagged this concept as a National Licensing Exam favourite. Slide 17's combined-effect arithmetic is worth having ready: <b>addition 1+1=2</b> · <b>synergism 1+1&gt;2</b> · <b>potentiation 1+0&gt;1</b> · <b>antagonism 1+1&lt;2</b>.","o":{"A":"Potentiation","B":"Chemical antagonism","C":"Pharmacological antagonism","D":"Synergism","E":"Functional antagonism"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Potentiation is 1 + 0 &gt; 1 — an inactive drug amplifying an active one.","B":"Chemical antagonism is a direct reaction between the molecules — no receptor at all.","C":"Pharmacological antagonism is competition at the <b>same</b> receptor.","D":"Synergism is 1 + 1 &gt; 2 — same direction, reinforcing.","E":"Correct. Opposite effects on the same physiological variable, produced through different receptors, is <b>functional (physiological) antagonism</b>."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q18 (keyed 'physiological'); BM29 SUM1 Q16; Ax [57] — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q17","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q17","t":"Receptor regulation","s":"Which up-regulates the <b>β1</b> receptor?","lec":"L3","sl":18,"c":"Two clinical pay-offs for this one rule. <b>Chronic heart failure</b>: sustained sympathetic drive down-regulates β1, which is part of why β-blockers, counter-intuitively, improve outcomes — they let the receptors recover. <b>β-blocker withdrawal</b>: up-regulated receptors suddenly exposed to normal catecholamine levels produce rebound tachycardia and angina, so the drug must be tapered.","o":{"A":"Loss of GTPase activity","B":"Daily intake of a β1 agonist","C":"Excessive activation by noradrenaline","D":"Daily intake of a β1 antagonist","E":"Inhibiting the degradation of a β1 agonist"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Loss of GTPase prolongs G-protein activity — more signalling.","B":"Chronic agonist → down-regulation.","C":"Excessive noradrenaline is excessive agonist stimulation → down-regulation. This is why chronic heart failure, with its high sympathetic drive, features β1 down-regulation.","D":"Correct. Chronic <b>antagonist</b> exposure under-stimulates the receptor and the cell compensates by making more — <b>up-regulation</b>. This is the mechanism of β-blocker rebound.","E":"Blocking an agonist's breakdown raises its concentration → more stimulation → down-regulation."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q17 (β1); BM31 SUM1 Q20 and BM30 SUM1 Q20 (α1) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_18.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q18","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q18","t":"Moringa oleifera","s":"A paper reports that <b>Moringa oleifera</b> leaf extract enhances endothelial <b>nitric oxide</b> production, relaxing resistance arteries and lowering arterial blood pressure. What is the possible second messenger?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"⚠️ <b>Four cohorts, and the professor is examining his own published work.</b> Two variants with two different answers: <b>NO version → cGMP</b> (BM29, BM28); <b>H₂S version → cystathionine-γ-lyase</b> (BM31, BM30). BM28 also asked about the study's design — that <b>papaverine</b> was the positive control and <b>sodium nitroprusside</b> could replace it (both are direct, endothelium-independent vasodilators), and that the extract's blood-pressure effect suggests an action on adrenergic receptors.","o":{"A":"K⁺ channel","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Cyclic GMP","E":"Guanylyl cyclase"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"An effector, not a messenger.","B":"An enzyme, and from the cAMP pathway.","C":"DAG belongs to the Gq/PLC pathway.","D":"Correct. NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → <b>cGMP</b> → protein kinase G → smooth-muscle relaxation.","E":"The <b>enzyme</b> that makes cGMP. The question asks for the messenger — read the noun."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q18 (NO → cGMP); BM31 SUM1 Q21 and BM30 SUM1 Q21 (H₂S → CSE) — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_15.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q19","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q19","t":"Severe cutaneous drug reactions","s":"A man with trigeminal neuralgia was prescribed carbamazepine. After one week he had a flu-like fever, skin inflammation and 5% skin detachment. <b>Nikolsky's sign was positive.</b> What reaction is this?","lec":"L3B","sl":6,"c":"This is the most complete version of the item, because every distractor is a real severe cutaneous adverse reaction. Sort them by <b>latency and morphology</b>:<br><b>AGEP</b> — 1–2 days, sterile pustules, resolves fast.<br><b>SJS/TEN</b> — 1–3 weeks, blistering and <b>detachment</b>, Nikolsky positive (&lt;10% / 10–30% / &gt;30%).<br><b>DRESS</b> — <b>2–6 weeks</b>, the longest latency, <b>eosinophilia</b> + organ involvement.<br><b>Anaphylaxis</b> — minutes, type I.<br>Carbamazepine can cause all of them, so the drug does not decide the answer — the timing and the skin findings do.","o":{"A":"Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP)","B":"Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)","C":"A type A reaction","D":"Anaphylaxis","E":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"<b>AGEP</b> produces sterile pustules on erythematous skin within <b>1–2 days</b> and resolves quickly. Wrong morphology and wrong latency.","B":"<b>DRESS</b> is defined by <b>eosinophilia</b> with fever, a widespread rash and organ involvement (hepatitis, nephritis), and a long latency of <b>2–6 weeks</b>. It does not cause epidermal detachment.","C":"Type A reactions are dose-related and predictable — an extension of the drug's own pharmacology. SJS is <b>type B</b>.","D":"Anaphylaxis is minutes, with urticaria, angio-oedema, bronchospasm and hypotension — a type I reaction.","E":"Correct. <b>5% detachment is under the 10% cut-off → SJS.</b> A positive <b>Nikolsky's sign</b> confirms that the epidermis is separating."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q22; BM30 SUM1 Q22; BM32 SUM1 L3 — four cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3B_6.webp"},{"id":"P|BM28|Q20","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"BM28","n":"Q20","t":"Competitive antagonism","s":"An experiment on isolated mouse arterioles compares phenylephrine alone with phenylephrine plus drug X. The curve shifts right with no change in maximum. What is drug X?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"The Ax bank compresses this whole topic into two sentences worth memorising verbatim: <b>'The competitive antagonist affects agonist POTENCY; the non-competitive antagonist affects agonist EFFICACY.'</b> and <b>'Competitive antagonist: reversible binding, same site as the agonist, same maximum effect, shifts the graph right.'</b> Note the nuance it adds: non-competitive binding at the <b>active site</b> is irreversible only, whereas binding at an <b>allosteric site</b> can be reversible or irreversible.","o":{"A":"A reversible agonist","B":"A competitive antagonist","C":"A vasodilator","D":"A non-competitive antagonist","E":"A β1 antagonist"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"An agonist would produce its own response.","B":"Correct. <b>Parallel rightward shift, unchanged Emax = competitive antagonism</b> — surmountable, and it reduces the agonist's <b>potency</b> only.","C":"A direct vasodilator acts alone; drug X only reveals itself against phenylephrine.","D":"A non-competitive antagonist <b>lowers the maximum</b>.","E":"Phenylephrine acts at <b>α1</b>, so a β1 blocker would have no effect on its curve."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q20; BM30 Formative 1 Q6 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L21-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L21-1","t":"Drug nomenclature","s":"In clinical and medical usage, drugs should be referred to by their…","lec":"L1","sl":17,"c":"Slide 17 lays out the three names for paracetamol: <b>chemical</b> <i>N-(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetamide</i> → <b>generic</b> paracetamol (or acetaminophen; with a pharmacopoeia suffix it becomes the official name, <i>paracetamol BP</i>) → <b>trade</b> Tylenol®, Sara®. Rational drug use and the NLEM both rest on generic prescribing.","o":{"A":"Chemical name","B":"Generic name","C":"Brand name","D":"Botanical name","E":"Clinical name"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The <b>chemical name</b> describes the molecule (paracetamol is <i>N-(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetamide</i>) and is unusable in practice.","B":"Correct. The <b>generic name</b> is the official, non-proprietary name — unique worldwide, unambiguous, and independent of manufacturer. Prescribing generically avoids confusion between the many brands of the same molecule.","C":"The <b>brand/trade name</b> is proprietary — Tylenol®, Sara® and Panadol® are all the same drug. Using it invites duplication and error.","D":"A botanical name applies to a plant, not a medicine.","E":"There is no such category."},"also":"AX [58] also asked the reverse — 'which of these IS the generic name' → paracetamol","img":"slides_p/L1_17.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56,58] L21-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56,58] L21-2","t":"Specificity and selectivity","s":"Which drug acts through a <b>non-specific</b> mechanism rather than at a receptor?","lec":"L3","sl":5,"c":"Slide 5 divides drug targets into those acting <b>on receptors</b> and those acting <b>by physical or chemical properties</b> — antacids, osmotic agents, chelators, general anaesthetics, ethanol. Slide 6 then separates two words that are often conflated: <b>specificity</b> = the drug produces one kind of effect; <b>selectivity</b> = it prefers one receptor class. The bank's atropine item makes the point — ophthalmic atropine is <b>site-selective and specific</b>: applied locally to the eye, acting at one receptor class.","o":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Epinephrine","C":"Ethanol","D":"Atropine","E":"Propranolol"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Histamine acts at H1-H4 receptors.","B":"Epinephrine is an agonist at defined adrenergic receptors.","C":"Correct. <b>Ethanol</b> acts largely by its <b>physical and chemical properties</b> — perturbing membrane lipids and interacting broadly — rather than by binding a specific receptor. General anaesthetics, osmotic diuretics, antacids and chelators belong to the same non-receptor category.","D":"Atropine is a muscarinic antagonist.","E":"Propranolol is a β-adrenoceptor antagonist."},"img":"slides_p/L3_5.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[57] L23-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57] L23-1","t":"Potency","s":"Drugs A and B are full agonists at the same receptor, with <b>pD2</b> values of 10 and 8 respectively. Which statement is correct?","lec":"L3","sl":7,"c":"The '-p' notation is negative-log throughout, so <b>bigger p = more potent / more active</b>:<br><b>pD2</b> = −log EC50 → measures <b>agonist potency</b><br><b>pA2</b> = −log of the antagonist concentration requiring a doubled agonist dose → measures <b>antagonist potency</b>; equal pA2 implies the same receptor<br><b>Kd</b> = the ligand concentration occupying <b>50%</b> of receptors → lower Kd = higher affinity<br>The bank also asks what <b>cannot</b> determine agonist potency: <b>Emax</b> (that is efficacy) and <b>pA2</b> (that is an antagonist measure).","o":{"A":"A is more potent than B","B":"B is more potent than A","C":"A has greater efficacy than B","D":"B has greater efficacy than A","E":"One of them must be a partial agonist"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>pD2 = −log EC50</b>, so a <b>higher pD2 means a lower EC50 and greater potency</b>. pD2 10 versus 8 is a hundred-fold difference in potency.","B":"The scale is logarithmic and inverted — a higher pD2 is more potent, not less.","C":"Both are stated to be <b>full</b> agonists, so both reach the same maximum: efficacy is equal.","D":"Same reasoning.","E":"Both are given as full agonists; a difference in potency does not make either partial."},"also":"AX [56] — 'what measures potency' → pD2; 'what measures an antagonist' → pA2","img":"slides_p/L3_7.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[57] L23-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57] L23-2","t":"Antagonism","s":"The <b>competitive</b> antagonist affects agonist ______; the <b>non-competitive</b> antagonist affects agonist ______.","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"Everything about antagonism follows from this pair:<br><b>Competitive</b> — same site, <b>reversible</b>, <b>surmountable</b>, parallel <b>rightward shift</b>, <b>Emax unchanged</b>, ED50 increases.<br><b>Non-competitive</b> — <b>insurmountable</b>, <b>Emax reduced</b>.<br>⚠️ The bank corrects a common oversimplification: <b>non-competitive is not always irreversible</b>. Binding at the <b>active site</b> is irreversible only; binding at an <b>allosteric site</b> can be reversible or irreversible. And under a competitive antagonist the quantity that does <b>not</b> change is the <b>maximal effect</b>.","o":{"A":"Efficacy; potency","B":"Potency; efficacy","C":"Efficacy; efficacy","D":"Duration; speed","E":"Speed; duration"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Exactly backwards.","B":"Correct, and this single sentence answers most of the figure items in this course. A <b>competitive</b> antagonist can be out-competed, so the maximum is still reachable — only more agonist is needed: <b>potency falls, efficacy is preserved</b>. A <b>non-competitive</b> antagonist removes receptors from play, so the maximum can no longer be reached: <b>efficacy falls</b>.","C":"Only the non-competitive antagonist reduces efficacy.","D":"Neither is about time course.","E":"Neither is about time course."},"also":"BM30 Formative 1 Q6; BM29 SUM1 Q20; BM28 SUM1 Q20 — the phenylephrine figure item, three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[57] L23-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57] L23-3","t":"Physiological antagonism","s":"A drug that reverses histamine-induced bronchoconstriction by acting at a completely different receptor — epinephrine at adrenoceptors — is called…","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"This example is worth memorising because it is also the <b>clinical</b> reason adrenaline is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis: it reverses histamine's effects <b>functionally</b>, faster and more completely than any H1 antagonist could. The professor flagged functional vs pharmacological antagonism as a National Licensing Exam favourite, and BM31/BM29/BM28 all examined it with the two-blood-pressure-drugs stem.","o":{"A":"A partial agonist","B":"A chemical antagonist","C":"A physiological antagonist","D":"A pharmacological antagonist","E":"A non-competitive antagonist"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Epinephrine is a full agonist at its own receptors.","B":"Chemical antagonism requires the two molecules to react with each other directly, as protamine does with heparin.","C":"Correct, and this is the cleanest illustration of the concept in the whole bank. Histamine bronchoconstricts through <b>H1</b> receptors; epinephrine bronchodilates through <b>β2</b> receptors. Opposite effects on the same endpoint, entirely different receptors — <b>physiological (functional) antagonism</b>.","D":"Pharmacological antagonism means competing at the <b>same</b> receptor — an H1 antihistamine would be that.","E":"Non-competitive antagonism also implies acting at the same receptor's system, lowering Emax."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q18; BM29 SUM1 Q16; BM28 SUM1 Q16 — three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[55,58] L23-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[55,58] L23-4","t":"Partial agonists","s":"Drug X and drug Y bind the same receptor. X is a positive inotrope. Pre-treatment with Y reduces X's <b>maximum efficacy</b>. What could Y be?","lec":"L3","sl":10,"c":"Slide 10's three agonist types, in terms of intrinsic activity: <b>full agonist</b> — maximal response · <b>partial agonist</b> — submaximal response even at full occupancy, so it <b>antagonises a full agonist</b> while <b>agonising an empty system</b> · <b>inverse agonist</b> — produces the <i>opposite</i> of the constitutive baseline effect. The bank's related item describes the allosteric picture: a partial agonist <b>binds both Ra and Ri but prefers Ra</b>, which is exactly why its response is intermediate.","o":{"A":"A full agonist","B":"A competitive antagonist only","C":"An inverse agonist only","D":"A partial agonist or a non-competitive antagonist","E":"A chemical antagonist"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A second full agonist would add to the effect, not reduce it.","B":"A <b>competitive</b> antagonist shifts the curve right but leaves the maximum intact — it cannot be the answer when Emax has fallen.","C":"An inverse agonist stabilises the inactive receptor conformation and produces an effect below baseline; it is not the standard answer to a fall in a full agonist's Emax.","D":"Correct. Reducing the <b>maximum</b> is the key. A <b>non-competitive antagonist</b> does that by removing receptors from play. A <b>partial agonist</b> does it too: occupying receptors that a full agonist could have used, and producing only a submaximal response from them, so in the presence of a full agonist it behaves as an antagonist and lowers the ceiling.","E":"Chemical antagonism involves no receptor."},"img":"slides_p/L3_10.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L24-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L24-1","t":"Therapeutic index","s":"Generally, which is the correct order as dosage increases?","lec":"L3","sl":9,"c":"From this ordering come the safety measures: <b>therapeutic index = LD50/ED50</b> (or TD50/ED50), and <b>the higher the TI, the safer the drug</b>. The <b>therapeutic window</b> is the same idea expressed in concentrations — the gap between MEC and MTC. Slide 9 lists the narrow-window drugs explicitly, and the bank adds that to judge whether a drug is usable in a population you look at <b>TI</b>, not at pD2 or pA2 in an isolated tissue.","o":{"A":"ED50 < LD50 < TD50","B":"ED50 < TD50 < LD50","C":"LD50 < TD50 < ED50","D":"LD50 < ED50 < TD50","E":"TD50 < LD50 < ED50"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"This would mean the lethal dose comes before the toxic dose — impossible.","B":"Correct, and it is common sense once stated: as the dose rises you get the <b>effect</b> first (ED50), then <b>toxicity</b> (TD50), and only at the highest doses <b>death</b> (LD50).","C":"Reversed entirely.","D":"Death before effect.","E":"Reversed."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q7; BM29 SUM1 Q6; BM28 SUM1 Q7 — 'which drug needs the most caution', three cohorts","img":"slides_p/L3_9.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[57] L24-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57] L24-2","t":"Quantal dose-response","s":"On a quantal dose-response curve, plotting log dose against <b>% of population responding</b> gives which shape?","lec":"L3","sl":9,"c":"<b>Graded vs quantal is a distinction worth being able to state.</b> <b>Graded</b> — one preparation or individual, the response varies <i>continuously</i> with dose (isolated trachea dilating, blood vessel constricting, heart rate rising); gives EC50 and Emax. <b>Quantal</b> — a <i>population</i>, and the response is all-or-none (asleep / not asleep); gives ED50, TD50, LD50 and therefore the <b>therapeutic index</b>. The bank's worked example: 'drug A induces sleep in 50% of the population at 25 mg/kg' is a quantal statement.","o":{"A":"An exponential curve","B":"A linear plot","C":"A Gaussian (normal) distribution","D":"A hyperbola","E":"A step function"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Exponential describes first-order elimination against time, not population response.","B":"The relationship is not linear on either scale.","C":"Correct. Individual sensitivity is <b>normally distributed</b> in a population, so the frequency of responders at each log dose is <b>Gaussian</b>. Its cumulative form is the familiar sigmoid, from which ED50, TD50 and LD50 are read.","D":"A hyperbola is the shape of a <b>graded</b> curve on a linear (not log) dose axis.","E":"A step function would imply every individual responded at exactly the same dose."},"img":"slides_p/L3_9.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L26-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L26-1","t":"Ion trapping","s":"Which drug's clearance increases most if the urine is made <b>acidic</b>?","lec":"L2","sl":25,"c":"<b>Ion trapping — one rule, and it is worth over-learning because it is asked in several guises:</b> only the <b>un-ionised</b> form crosses membranes and is reabsorbed; the <b>ionised</b> form is trapped and excreted. <b>Acidify the urine → excrete BASES</b> (amphetamine, given NH₄Cl). <b>Alkalinise the urine → excrete ACIDS</b> (aspirin/phenobarbital overdose, given NaHCO₃ — 'alkaline diuresis', which is the keyed answer in the toxicology block of BM30's and BM31's Summative II). Related: <b>fe</b> = fraction excreted unchanged in urine, so <b>fe = 1 means the drug needs the greatest dose reduction in renal failure</b>.","o":{"A":"Drug C, pKa 4.5","B":"Drug B, pKa 10","C":"Drug A, pKa 12","D":"Drug D, pKa 2.4","E":"Drug E, pKa 8.0"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"pKa 4.5 is a weak <b>acid</b>. In acidic urine it stays <b>un-ionised</b> and is reabsorbed — clearance <i>falls</i>.","B":"pKa 10 is also basic and will show the same effect, but less strongly than pKa 12.","C":"Correct. A drug with a high pKa is a <b>weak base</b>. In <b>acidic</b> urine it becomes <b>ionised</b>, and an ionised molecule cannot diffuse back across the tubular membrane — it is <b>trapped in the urine and excreted</b>. The higher the pKa, the more completely this happens, so pKa 12 shows the largest increase in clearance.","D":"pKa 2.4 is a stronger acid; acidifying the urine reduces its excretion further.","E":"pKa 8.0 is weakly basic and would show an intermediate effect."},"also":"AX [55] — NaHCO₃ raises urine pH and increases a weak acid's excretion; [57] — NH₄Cl for a weak base","img":"slides_p/L2_25.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56] L26-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56] L26-2","t":"Plasma protein binding","s":"A patient's serum shows <b>low albumin</b> and <b>high α1-acid glycoprotein</b>. What follows?","lec":"L2","sl":15,"c":"Slide 15 states the pairing: <b>acidic drugs → albumin</b>, <b>basic drugs → α1-acid glycoprotein</b>. Two clinical consequences the exam uses: <b>hypoalbuminaemia</b> (nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, malnutrition, the elderly) raises the free fraction of acidic drugs → Vd ↑ → <b>loading dose increased, maintenance dose unchanged</b>; and <b>displacement</b> matters most for highly bound narrow-window drugs, because a fall from 97% to 94% bound <b>doubles</b> the free concentration (slide 16's arithmetic).","o":{"A":"Neither is affected by plasma protein levels","B":"Free acidic drug is low and free basic drug is high","C":"Both free acidic and free basic drug are high","D":"Both free acidic and free basic drug are low","E":"Free acidic drug is high and free basic drug is low"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Only the <b>free</b> fraction is pharmacologically active, so protein levels matter a great deal.","B":"Exactly the wrong way round for both.","C":"The two proteins move in opposite directions, so the two free fractions must too.","D":"Same objection.","E":"Correct. <b>Albumin binds ACIDIC drugs; α1-acid glycoprotein binds BASIC drugs.</b> Less albumin means fewer binding sites for acids, so the <b>free acidic</b> fraction rises. More α1-acid glycoprotein means more binding of bases, so the <b>free basic</b> fraction falls."},"also":"AX [56] — hypoalbuminaemia: LD increased, MD unchanged; contrast with renal failure","img":"slides_p/L2_15.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L26-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L26-3","t":"Non-linear kinetics","s":"A plot of Css against dose <b>bends downward</b> as the dose rises. Which factor has become saturated?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"<b>Two kinds of non-linear pharmacokinetics, and they curve in opposite directions:</b><br><b>Saturated METABOLISM → curve bends UP</b> — zero-order elimination, disproportionate rise in level. Phenytoin, aspirin, ethanol.<br><b>Saturated PROTEIN BINDING → curve bends DOWN</b> — free fraction rises, apparent clearance rises.<br>This is the deepest pharmacokinetics in any of the sources, and it is why the Ax bank is worth working through even though it is an old curriculum.","o":{"A":"Drug metabolism","B":"Plasma protein binding","C":"Renal filtration","D":"Gastric emptying","E":"Biliary excretion"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Saturated <b>metabolism</b> gives the opposite shape: elimination cannot keep pace, so Css rises more than proportionally and the curve <b>bends UP</b>. Phenytoin is the classic example.","B":"Correct. Once <b>protein binding saturates</b>, additional drug can only stay free. The free fraction rises, and free drug is what gets cleared — so clearance effectively increases and the measured total Css rises <b>less</b> than proportionally. The curve <b>bends down</b>.","C":"Glomerular filtration is not a saturable carrier process.","D":"Gastric emptying affects the rate of absorption, not steady-state level.","E":"Biliary transport can saturate, but it is not the mechanism this classic item is about."},"img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[55] L26-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[55] L26-4","t":"Time to steady state","s":"How long does a drug take to reach steady state?","lec":"L2","sl":29,"c":"<b>Time to steady state depends ONLY on half-life</b> — not on dose, not on interval, not on route. Corollaries worth carrying: the same 4–5 half-lives are needed to <b>wash out</b> after stopping; a <b>loading dose</b> is the only way to reach the target concentration sooner; and a change in dose starts the 4–5 half-life clock again toward the new plateau.","o":{"A":"Two half-lives","B":"One half-life","C":"About 4-5 half-lives","D":"It depends on the dose given","E":"It depends on the dosing interval"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Two half-lives gets you 75%.","B":"One half-life gets you only 50% of the way there.","C":"Correct. Steady state — where the rate in equals the rate out — is approached asymptotically and is taken as reached after about <b>4–5 half-lives</b> (93.75% and 96.875% respectively).","D":"A larger dose gives a <b>higher</b> plateau, but it arrives at exactly the same <b>time</b>. This is the single most useful thing to know about steady state, and the reason loading doses exist.","E":"The interval affects the size of peak-to-trough fluctuation, not the time to plateau."},"also":"BM29 SUM1 Q12; BM28 SUM1 Q12 — the calculation version, two cohorts","img":"slides_p/L2_29.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56] L26-5","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56] L26-5","t":"Enterohepatic circulation","s":"What is the effect of <b>enterohepatic circulation</b>?","lec":"L2","sl":26,"c":"Slide 26 covers biliary and faecal excretion. The clinically examinable consequences of enterohepatic recycling: prolonged half-life and a <b>secondary peak</b>; the antibiotic/oral-contraceptive interaction; and the use of repeated <b>activated charcoal</b> in poisoning to interrupt the cycle. Note the contrast with drugs that are <b>not</b> reabsorbed and simply leave in the faeces.","o":{"A":"It reduces oral bioavailability","B":"It shortens the drug's half-life","C":"It prolongs the drug's half-life","D":"It increases renal clearance","E":"It has no effect on drug disposition"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"It increases the effective exposure, not reduces it.","B":"Recycling delays elimination rather than hastening it.","C":"Correct. Drug conjugated in the liver is excreted in bile, then <b>hydrolysed by gut bacteria</b> and reabsorbed as the parent drug. That recycling keeps returning drug to the circulation, so the <b>half-life is prolonged</b> and the plasma curve often shows a secondary peak.","D":"Renal clearance is unaffected.","E":"It has a substantial effect — clinically, broad-spectrum antibiotics that kill gut flora can interrupt the recycling of <b>oral contraceptives</b> and cause contraceptive failure."},"img":"slides_p/L2_26.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L26-6","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L26-6","t":"Phase I metabolism","s":"Which reaction belongs to <b>Phase I</b> biotransformation?","lec":"L2","sl":19,"c":"Slide 19 draws it as two boxes: <b>Phase I</b> — oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis; enzyme CYP450; adds a polar group; product may be <b>active, inactive or toxic</b>. <b>Phase II</b> — conjugation with glucuronate, sulfate, acetate, glycine or glutathione; almost always inactivating; makes the molecule water-soluble for excretion. Two points that get examined: a drug can go <b>straight to Phase II</b> without Phase I, and a <b>prodrug</b> is <i>activated</i> by metabolism (cyclophosphamide, prednisone, 6-MP, enalapril).","o":{"A":"Glutathione conjugation","B":"Glucuronidation","C":"Acetylation","D":"Sulfation","E":"Reduction"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Glutathione conjugation is Phase II — the pathway that detoxifies paracetamol's NAPQI.","B":"Glucuronidation is the commonest <b>Phase II</b> conjugation.","C":"Acetylation is Phase II — and the source of the fast/slow acetylator polymorphism (isoniazid).","D":"Sulfation is Phase II.","E":"Correct. <b>Phase I = oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis</b>, mostly by CYP450. These reactions add or unmask a polar functional group."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q5 — 'which enzyme complex is most commonly involved in Phase I' → CYP450","img":"slides_p/L2_19.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L-DI-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L-DI-1","t":"Drug interactions","s":"Drug A has no effect of its own, but given with drug B it increases B's effect so that the total is greater than B alone. What is this called?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"Slide 17's arithmetic, which makes all four instantly separable: <b>1+1=2 addition</b> · <b>1+1&gt;2 synergism</b> · <b>1+0&gt;1 potentiation</b> · <b>1+1&lt;2 antagonism</b>. The Ax bank adds the definition-level detail that synergism means <b>different receptors or processes but the same effect</b>, which is what makes it distinct from simple addition at one receptor.","o":{"A":"Tachyphylaxis","B":"Synergism","C":"Additive effect","D":"Antagonism","E":"Potentiation"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Tachyphylaxis is rapid loss of response on repeated dosing — not a two-drug interaction.","B":"<b>Synergism is 1 + 1 &gt; 2</b> — both drugs are active and the combination exceeds their sum.","C":"<b>Addition is 1 + 1 = 2</b>.","D":"<b>Antagonism is 1 + 1 &lt; 2</b>.","E":"Correct. <b>Potentiation is 1 + 0 &gt; 1</b> — an inactive drug amplifies an active one. The bank's own note puts it as '2 + 0 = 5'. The classic clinical example is <b>probenecid + penicillin</b>: probenecid has no antibacterial effect but blocks penicillin's renal tubular secretion, raising and prolonging its level."},"img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L-DI-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L-DI-2","t":"Enzyme induction","s":"A tranquilliser is given with warfarin, and the warfarin dose has to be increased. When the tranquilliser is stopped, the patient <b>bleeds</b>. What was the tranquilliser doing?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"⚠️ <b>Induction and inhibition also differ in TIME COURSE, and that is often the point of the vignette.</b> <b>Inhibition is immediate</b> — the enzyme is blocked as soon as the drug is present. <b>Induction is slow</b>, taking days to weeks, because new enzyme protein must be synthesised — and it takes just as long to wear off. That delay is exactly what makes stopping an inducer dangerous for a narrow-window drug like warfarin.<br><b>Inducers:</b> rifampicin, phenytoin, phenobarbital, <b>carbamazepine</b>, St John's wort, chronic alcohol, smoking.<br><b>Inhibitors:</b> -azoles, macrolides, ritonavir, cimetidine, fluoxetine, amiodarone, grapefruit juice, acute alcohol.","o":{"A":"Increasing warfarin's renal excretion","B":"Inhibiting CYP450","C":"Increasing warfarin's protein binding","D":"Inducing CYP450","E":"Antagonising warfarin at its receptor"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Warfarin is cleared by metabolism, not renal excretion.","B":"An inhibitor would have <i>reduced</i> the dose requirement, and stopping it would cause loss of anticoagulation, not bleeding.","C":"Increased binding would lower the free fraction — again the opposite pattern on withdrawal.","D":"Correct, and the logic is worth walking through because this shape of question recurs. While the tranquilliser was present the warfarin dose had to be <b>raised</b> → warfarin was being cleared faster → the tranquilliser was an <b>inducer</b>. Stopping it removed the induction; enzyme levels fell back over days; the now-excessive warfarin dose caused <b>bleeding</b>.","E":"Warfarin acts on vitamin K epoxide reductase; a tranquilliser does not antagonise it."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q24 (phenobarbital reduces losartan's effect); AX [58] — carbamazepine is a CYP inducer","img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56] L-DI-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56] L-DI-3","t":"CYP isoenzymes","s":"Which CYP450 enzyme is the most important, because it metabolises drugs from almost every class?","lec":"L2","sl":9,"c":"Slide 9 shows the CYP family. Learn one or two substrates each, because the interaction items are all built on them: <b>3A4/3A5</b> — the big one, statins, ciclosporin, many others; <b>2C9</b> — S-warfarin, phenytoin; <b>2C19</b> — omeprazole, clopidogrel; <b>2D6</b> — codeine, metoprolol, antidepressants, highly polymorphic; <b>1A2</b> — caffeine, theophylline, induced by smoking; <b>2E1</b> — <b>paracetamol → NAPQI</b>, induced by chronic alcohol.","o":{"A":"CYP2C9","B":"CYP1A2","C":"CYP3A4/3A5","D":"CYP2C19","E":"CYP2D6"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"CYP2C9 clears <b>S-warfarin</b> and phenytoin — small in share, large in consequence.","B":"CYP1A2 metabolises caffeine and theophylline; it is <b>induced by smoking</b>.","C":"Correct. <b>CYP3A4/3A5</b> handles roughly half of all drugs metabolised by CYP enzymes, and is abundant in <b>both</b> liver and intestinal wall — which is why grapefruit juice, an intestinal 3A4 inhibitor, raises the levels of oral 3A4 substrates such as simvastatin and ciclosporin.","D":"CYP2C19 clears omeprazole and clopidogrel; its polymorphism explains clopidogrel non-response.","E":"CYP2D6 clears codeine, many antidepressants and metoprolol, and is the most polymorphic — ultra-rapid metabolisers convert codeine to morphine dangerously fast."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q23 — the ketoconazole/simvastatin interaction is a 3A4 story","img":"slides_p/L2_9.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L29-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L29-1","t":"Modified-release forms","s":"An elderly patient cannot swallow tablets, so the tablets must be crushed. Which form must <b>not</b> be given?","lec":"L1","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11's forms and what each is for: <b>uncoated/film-coated</b> — plain, crushable · <b>enteric-coated (EC)</b> — acid-resistant, dissolves in the intestine, <b>never crush</b> · <b>extended-release (ER/XR/SR)</b> — slow release for once-daily dosing, <b>never crush</b> · <b>sugar-coated</b> — taste masking. Related bank items: a <b>transdermal patch</b> gives the flattest controlled-release profile, and <b>capsules act faster than coated tablets because they need no disintegration step</b>.","o":{"A":"Sugar-coated tablet","B":"Hard gelatin capsule","C":"Effervescent tablet","D":"Film-coated tablet","E":"Delayed-release tablet"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"A sugar coat masks taste; crushing makes it bitter but is not dangerous.","B":"A hard capsule can usually be opened and its contents given, unless the contents are themselves modified-release beads.","C":"An effervescent tablet is <b>designed</b> to be dissolved in water — an excellent choice for someone who cannot swallow solids.","D":"A film coat is cosmetic and protective only; crushing is acceptable.","E":"Correct. <b>Do not chew or crush enteric-coated, sustained-release or controlled-release tablets.</b> Crushing destroys the release mechanism, so the entire dose is dumped at once — risking toxicity — and, for an enteric coat, exposes an acid-labile drug to gastric acid or lets a gastric irritant loose on the stomach."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L1 (enteric-coated for acid avoidance); AX [55,57] — 'which starts release at the intestine' → enteric-coated","img":"slides_p/L1_11.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L29-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L29-2","t":"Sublingual route","s":"Which route gives the <b>fastest onset without first-pass metabolism</b> among oral preparations?","lec":"L1","sl":14,"c":"Two questions that look the same and are not: <b>'fastest release'</b> among swallowed forms → effervescent &gt; solution &gt; suspension &gt; capsule &gt; tablet &gt; coated tablet. <b>'fastest onset AND no first pass'</b> → <b>sublingual</b> (or buccal). Changing the <i>form</i> changes the rate; only changing the <i>route</i> escapes first pass.","o":{"A":"Sustained-release tablet","B":"Effervescent tablet","C":"Syrup","D":"Enteric-coated tablet","E":"Sublingual"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Sustained-release is deliberately the slowest.","B":"An effervescent tablet is already in solution and so is the fastest of the <b>swallowed</b> forms — but it is swallowed, so it still undergoes first pass. This is the deliberate near-miss.","C":"A syrup is also a solution and also swallowed.","D":"An enteric coat deliberately <b>delays</b> release until the intestine.","E":"Correct. Sublingual mucosa is thin and richly vascular, and it drains into <b>systemic</b> veins, so the drug appears in the circulation within a minute or two and never passes through the liver first."},"also":"BM32 SUM1 L1 — the sublingual nitroglycerin item is the clinical version","img":"slides_p/L1_14.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[55] L29-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[55] L29-3","t":"Sterile preparations","s":"Which property must an <b>injection</b> have?","lec":"L1","sl":15,"c":"The bank asks this from several angles, so hold the whole set: <b>injections, eye drops, eye ointments and parenteral nutrition must be sterile</b>; <b>eye drops and injections share sterility and isotonicity</b>; and <b>nasal spray does NOT need to be sterile</b>, because the nasal mucosa is not a sterile site. <b>Pyrogens</b> are bacterial endotoxins that survive sterilisation and cause fever — which is why 'sterile' and 'pyrogen-free' are separate requirements.","o":{"A":"Sterile only","B":"Sterile, pyrogen-limited and isotonic","C":"Isotonic only","D":"Pyrogen-limited only","E":"None of these are required"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Sterility alone is not enough — dead bacteria still leave pyrogens behind.","B":"Correct. An injection bypasses every natural barrier, so it must be <b>sterile</b> (no viable organisms), <b>pyrogen-free/limited</b> (no bacterial endotoxin, which causes fever even after sterilisation) and, ideally, <b>isotonic</b> and pH-adjusted to avoid haemolysis and pain.","C":"Isotonicity alone would be dangerous without sterility.","D":"Pyrogen limitation alone likewise.","E":"All three matter."},"img":"slides_p/L1_15.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[55-58] L30-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[55-58] L30-1","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"In which phase of drug development are <b>healthy normal volunteers</b> used?","lec":"L4","sl":14,"c":"⚠️ <b>The exception worth naming:</b> cytotoxic <b>oncology</b> drugs run Phase I in patients, not healthy volunteers, because deliberately exposing a healthy person to a cytotoxic agent would be unethical. Otherwise the rule holds. This item has appeared in <b>four separate years</b> of the Ax bank alone, on top of BM30 and BM29 — it is one of the most reliable facts in the whole course.","o":{"A":"Phase IV","B":"Preclinical","C":"Phase II","D":"Phase III","E":"Phase I"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Phase IV is the general treated population after marketing.","B":"Preclinical is in vitro and in animals.","C":"Phase II is in <b>patients</b> with the target disease.","D":"Phase III is in a large number of patients.","E":"Correct. <b>Phase I</b> is first-in-human, in 20–100 healthy volunteers, and its purpose is <b>safety, tolerability and dose</b> plus first human pharmacokinetics."},"also":"BM30 SUM1 Q37; BM29 SUM1 Q24 — asked in every cohort","img":"slides_p/L4_14.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[58] L30-2","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[58] L30-2","t":"Clinical trial phases","s":"In which phase is <b>further</b> efficacy and safety tested in <b>many</b> patients?","lec":"L4","sl":16,"c":"Keyword-spotting is genuinely the skill here, because every phase involves 'safety':<br><b>'healthy volunteers', 'first in human', 'dose finding'</b> → Phase I<br><b>'first in patients', 'optimal dose'</b> → Phase II<br><b>'further', 'confirm', 'large', 'double-blind', 'placebo-controlled', 'thousands', 'most expensive'</b> → Phase III<br><b>'after marketing', 'already prescribed', 'rare adverse effects', 'surveillance'</b> → Phase IV","o":{"A":"Phase I","B":"Preclinical","C":"Phase III","D":"Phase II","E":"Phase IV"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Small, healthy volunteers, safety and dose.","B":"Animal work.","C":"Correct — and the bank's own note says the keyword is <b>'further'</b>. Phase II establishes efficacy preliminarily; Phase III <b>confirms</b> it in a much larger, usually randomised and double-blind population, and picks up less common adverse effects.","D":"A few hundred patients, first efficacy signal and dose optimisation — not 'further'.","E":"After marketing."},"also":"BM31 SUM1 Q31; BM30 SUM1 Q43 and Formative 1 Q4 — every cohort","img":"slides_p/L4_16.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56,58] L30-3","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56,58] L30-3","t":"Teratogenicity","s":"Which teratogen is associated with underdevelopment of the limbs (<b>phocomelia</b>)?","lec":"L4","sl":3,"c":"Thalidomide is the historical spine of the L4 lecture. The regulatory lesson it teaches: preclinical <b>reproductive and developmental toxicity</b> testing became mandatory, and drugs acquired <b>pregnancy risk categories</b> — <b>Category X</b> being the highest risk to mother and fetus (contraindicated outright), <b>Category D</b> meaning there is evidence of fetal risk but the benefit may justify use. BM33's L4 keeps the case-study approach, using <b>TGN1412</b> (Phase I failure), <b>Torcetrapib</b> (Phase III failure) and <b>Mibefradil</b> (post-marketing withdrawal) on slides 19-21.","o":{"A":"Warfarin","B":"Thalidomide","C":"Isotretinoin","D":"Valproate","E":"Tetracycline"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Warfarin causes fetal warfarin syndrome — nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses.","B":"Correct. <b>Thalidomide</b>, marketed as a sedative and antiemetic for morning sickness in the late 1950s, caused <b>phocomelia</b> in thousands of infants. The disaster is the reason modern drug regulation, mandatory reproductive toxicology and the pregnancy risk categories exist at all.","C":"Isotretinoin causes craniofacial, cardiac and CNS defects — hence mandatory pregnancy prevention programmes.","D":"Valproate causes neural tube defects.","E":"Tetracycline discolours teeth and affects bone growth."},"also":"AX [55,56,58] — the discovery of penicillin as an example of serendipity, from the same section","img":"slides_p/L4_3.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[56,58] L30-4","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[56,58] L30-4","t":"Placebo","s":"What is a <b>placebo</b>?","lec":"L4","sl":16,"c":"What placebo control buys a trial, and why it matters: it separates the drug's effect from the <b>placebo response</b>, from natural history, and from <b>regression to the mean</b>. Related concepts from this block: <b>blinding</b> (single/double), <b>randomisation</b>, and the ethical limit — where an effective treatment exists, an <b>active comparator</b> replaces placebo. The Ax bank also asks about <b>orphan drugs</b>: those for <b>rare diseases, with limited potential for profitability</b>, which is why they receive regulatory incentives.","o":{"A":"An older drug used as an active comparator","B":"A drug given at a sub-therapeutic dose","C":"A preparation identical in appearance to the real drug but containing no active ingredient","D":"A drug given without the patient's knowledge","E":"A drug that has failed to show efficacy"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"An older drug is an <b>active comparator</b>, used when withholding treatment would be unethical.","B":"A sub-therapeutic dose still contains active drug — that is a dose-ranging arm, not a placebo.","C":"Correct. A placebo matches the active preparation in appearance, taste and route but has <b>no active ingredient</b>. Its purpose is to <b>blind</b> the trial, so that the measured difference reflects the drug rather than expectation.","D":"Concealment from the patient is a matter of consent, not the definition of a placebo.","E":"Failing to show efficacy does not make a drug a placebo."},"also":"AX [56] — 'orphan drug' definition, from the same section","img":"slides_p/L4_16.webp"},{"id":"P|AX|[57,58] L31-1","k":"mcq","src":"past","y":"AX","n":"[57,58] L31-1","t":"Prescribing in the elderly","s":"What dose should generally be recommended for elderly patients?","lec":"L2","sl":22,"c":"The four age-related changes that all point the same way: <b>renal clearance ↓</b> (the biggest factor) · <b>hepatic metabolism and liver blood flow ↓</b> · <b>albumin ↓</b>, raising free acidic drug · <b>body fat ↑ and body water ↓</b>, so lipophilic drugs have a larger Vd and a longer half-life while water-soluble drugs have a smaller Vd. Add <b>polypharmacy</b>, which is the Ax bank's answer to why the elderly are non-compliant.<br>⚠️ Prof DA said objective 6 — pregnant, paediatric and geriatric populations — is <b>'ไม่เน้น'</b> (not emphasised), twice. Know the principle; do not spend long here.","o":{"A":"More than average, because renal function is decreased","B":"Less than average, because biotransformation is increased","C":"More than average, because plasma protein binding capacity is increased","D":"Less than average, because renal function and excretion are decreased","E":"Totally unpredictable"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The reasoning contradicts itself: decreased renal function means <b>less</b> drug is cleared, so less is needed.","B":"Biotransformation <b>decreases</b> with age, not increases.","C":"Albumin tends to be <b>lower</b> in the elderly, raising the free fraction of acidic drugs — another reason to reduce the dose.","D":"Correct. <b>Renal function declines with age</b> (glomerular filtration falls steadily from about 40), so renally cleared drugs accumulate. Hepatic metabolism and liver blood flow also decline. The prescribing rule is <b>'start low, go slow'</b>.","E":"It is entirely predictable in direction, even if the exact magnitude varies."},"img":"slides_p/L2_22.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|1","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"1","t":"IV push vs IV drip","s":"A house officer is asked to give intravenous potassium chloride to a patient with hypokalaemia. Why must it be given as an <b>IV drip</b> and never as an IV push?","lec":"L1","sl":15,"c":"<b>IV push (bolus)</b> — the whole dose in seconds; fastest possible onset; used in emergencies. <b>IV drip (infusion)</b> — delivered over time; required whenever a sudden peak concentration is itself the danger. Slide 15's box names <b>vancomycin</b> (red man syndrome), <b>potassium chloride</b> (arrhythmia) and <b>phenytoin</b> (hypotension, arrhythmia) among the drugs that must never be pushed. The lecturer drills IV push vs IV drip specifically.","o":{"A":"A sudden high plasma potassium can cause fatal cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Potassium is destroyed by first-pass metabolism if given rapidly","C":"Rapid injection would cause red man syndrome","D":"Potassium precipitates in the syringe unless it is diluted slowly","E":"IV push would exceed the 10 mL volume limit for intravenous administration"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. An IV push delivers the whole dose into the circulation within seconds. A sudden rise in plasma potassium depolarises cardiac myocytes and can cause <b>asystole</b>. KCl is on slide 15's 'never IV push' list for exactly this reason.","B":"First-pass metabolism applies to drugs absorbed into the portal circulation. Nothing given intravenously undergoes first pass, and potassium is not metabolised at all.","C":"Red man syndrome is <b>vancomycin</b>, from direct mast-cell histamine release during rapid infusion.","D":"Precipitation is not the concern; the danger is pharmacological and immediate.","E":"The 10 mL limit belongs to <b>intramuscular</b> injection, not intravenous."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L1_15.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|2","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"2","t":"Inhalation particle size","s":"A child with asthma is prescribed an inhaled corticosteroid. Which particle size will deposit best in the <b>alveoli</b>?","lec":"L1","sl":16,"c":"Slide 16's particle-size ladder: <b>&gt; 10 µm → oropharynx</b> (swallowed; the source of the steroid's systemic absorption and of oral thrush) · <b>2-10 µm → conducting airways</b> (the target for bronchodilators) · <b>&lt; 2 µm → alveoli</b>. This is why <b>device and technique change bioavailability</b> as much as the drug does, and why inhalation is listed on slide 11 as 5-100% bioavailable — one of the widest ranges of any route.","o":{"A":"Less than 2 µm","B":"2-10 µm","C":"10-20 µm","D":"20-50 µm","E":"Above 50 µm"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Only particles <b>smaller than about 2 µm</b> stay airborne long enough to reach the alveoli and deposit there.","B":"Particles of 2-10 µm deposit in the <b>conducting airways</b> — bronchi and bronchioles. For a bronchodilator that is actually where you want them.","C":"Particles above 10 µm impact in the <b>oropharynx</b> and are swallowed, which is where inhaled steroid causes oral candidiasis and why patients are told to rinse their mouths.","D":"Even more oropharyngeal deposition.","E":"Never reaches the airway at all."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L1_16.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|3","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"3","t":"OTC vs prescription","s":"A patient asks to buy a medicine for a headache directly from a pharmacy, without seeing a doctor. Which Thai drug classification allows this?","lec":"L1","sl":4,"c":"Slide 4's Thai classification, in increasing order of control: <b>household remedy / OTC</b> (self-purchase) → <b>dangerous drug</b> (pharmacist) → <b>specially controlled drug</b> (doctor's prescription) → <b>narcotics and psychotropics</b> (separate law). ⚠️ Do not confuse this with the <b>NLEM sub-lists (บัญชี ก-จ)</b>, which are about <i>reimbursement and formulary access in hospitals</i>, not about who may sell the drug. Two different classification systems, both examinable, and both on L1.","o":{"A":"Specially controlled drug (ยาควบคุมพิเศษ)","B":"Dangerous drug (ยาอันตราย)","C":"Household remedy / over-the-counter drug","D":"Narcotic drug","E":"Psychotropic substance"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"A <b>specially controlled drug</b> requires a doctor's prescription and is dispensed only in a hospital or clinic setting. The lecturer's off-slide examples were <b>sildenafil</b> and the <b>GLP-1 pens</b>.","B":"A <b>dangerous drug</b> must be dispensed by a pharmacist in a licensed pharmacy; most antibiotics and most prescription medicines sit here.","C":"Correct. <b>Household remedies and OTC drugs</b> can be purchased for self-use without a prescription — paracetamol and simple antacids are the everyday examples.","D":"Narcotics are under separate, far stricter legislation.","E":"Psychotropics likewise."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L1_4.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|4","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"4","t":"Rectal route","s":"A 3-year-old with a febrile seizure is vomiting and cannot take anything by mouth. A rectal preparation is chosen. Which statement about this route is correct?","lec":"L1","sl":13,"c":"Rectal administration is the answer whenever a stem combines <b>cannot swallow</b> (unconscious, vomiting, seizing, or a small child) with <b>needs a systemic effect</b> and no IV access. Hold two facts: it is <b>enteral</b>, and it has <b>less first pass than oral but variable absorption</b>. Compare with sublingual, which is also enteral and also escapes first pass, but needs a cooperative patient.","o":{"A":"It is an enteral route with less first-pass metabolism than the oral route","B":"It is a parenteral route and completely avoids the liver","C":"It is an enteral route with the same first-pass metabolism as the oral route","D":"Its bioavailability is fixed at 100%","E":"It is only ever used for local effect, never systemic"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Rectal delivery is <b>enteral</b> — via the gastrointestinal tract — but the lower rectum drains into the <b>inferior and middle rectal veins</b>, which go to the systemic circulation rather than the portal vein. So it partly escapes first pass. Slide 11 gives rectal bioavailability as <b>30-100%, with less first-pass effect than oral</b>.","B":"Parenteral means injection. Rectal is enteral, and drainage from the <b>upper</b> rectum still goes portal, so the liver is not completely avoided.","C":"It is specifically <b>less</b> than oral — that is the route's main pharmacokinetic advantage.","D":"Only IV is 100% by definition. Rectal absorption is notoriously variable, which is the route's main weakness.","E":"It is used for both — local (haemorrhoids, proctitis) and systemic (antipyretics, antiemetics, anticonvulsants)."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L1_13.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|5","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"5","t":"Prescription structure","s":"In a prescription, which part is marked with the symbol <b>Rx</b>?","lec":"L1","sl":18,"c":"Slide 18's anatomy of a prescription: <b>1 superscription (Rx)</b> → <b>2 inscription</b> (drug, strength, amount) → <b>3 subscription</b> (dispensing instruction) → <b>4 transcription / sig.</b> (patient directions, where the Latin abbreviations appear) → <b>5 prescriber's details and signature</b>. The abbreviation questions the papers actually ask all live in the <b>sig.</b> line: <b>od · bid · tid · qid · hs · prn · stat · ac · pc · po · #</b>.","o":{"A":"The signature of the prescriber","B":"The inscription","C":"The subscription","D":"The transcription (sig.)","E":"The superscription"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The prescriber's signature is a separate, final element.","B":"The <b>inscription</b> is the body — the drug name, strength and quantity.","C":"The <b>subscription</b> is the instruction to the pharmacist about how to dispense.","D":"The <b>transcription</b> is the directions to the patient, abbreviated <b>sig.</b> or <b>S</b> — this is where <i>1 tab po tid ac</i> lives.","E":"Correct. The <b>superscription</b> is the opening of the order, marked <b>Rx</b> (from Latin <i>recipe</i>, 'take thou')."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L1_18.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|6","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"6","t":"Bioavailability equation","s":"A drug is 90% absorbed across the gut wall. 20% of what is absorbed is metabolised in the gut wall, and the hepatic extraction ratio is 0.5. What is its oral bioavailability?","lec":"L2","sl":8,"c":"Slide 8's equation, term by term: <b>F = Fa × Fg × Fh = Fa × Fg × (1 − EH)</b>. <b>Fa</b> fraction absorbed across the gut wall · <b>Fg</b> fraction escaping <b>gut</b> metabolism · <b>Fh</b> fraction escaping <b>hepatic</b> metabolism, where <b>EH</b> is the hepatic extraction ratio. The commonest error is using EH where 1 − EH belongs. A drug with a high extraction ratio (propranolol, morphine, nitroglycerin, lidocaine) has poor oral bioavailability and a large oral:IV dose difference.","o":{"A":"0.36","B":"0.72","C":"0.45","D":"0.90","E":"0.18"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>F = Fa × Fg × Fh</b> = 0.90 × 0.80 × (1 − 0.5) = 0.90 × 0.80 × 0.50 = <b>0.36</b>, i.e. 36%.","B":"0.72 = 0.9 × 0.8 — this stops after gut metabolism and forgets the liver entirely.","C":"0.45 = 0.9 × 0.5 — this forgets the gut-wall metabolism step.","D":"0.90 is Fa alone, the fraction absorbed, which is not bioavailability.","E":"0.18 would follow from using EH = 0.5 as Fh rather than 1 − EH, and then halving again."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L2_8.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|7","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"7","t":"Zero-order kinetics","s":"A patient stable on phenytoin has his dose increased by 20%. His plasma level rises by 200% and he becomes ataxic. What explains this?","lec":"L2","sl":27,"c":"Slide 27's contrast, and the clinical meaning of each: <b>First order</b> — rate ∝ concentration; a constant <b>fraction</b> per unit time; <b>constant half-life</b>; predictable dose-level relationship; most drugs at most doses. <b>Zero order</b> — rate independent of concentration; a constant <b>amount</b> per unit time; <b>no fixed half-life</b>; small dose changes cause large level changes. <b>Aspirin, phenytoin, ethanol.</b> Ethanol makes it intuitive: you metabolise roughly one unit an hour whether you have had two drinks or ten.","o":{"A":"Phenytoin's half-life shortened at the higher concentration","B":"Phenytoin displaced itself from albumin","C":"The higher dose induced its own metabolism","D":"The larger dose increased the volume of distribution","E":"Metabolising enzymes are already saturated, so elimination is zero-order"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The half-life <b>lengthens</b> as concentration rises in zero-order kinetics — the opposite.","B":"Self-displacement does not produce a three-fold rise in total level.","C":"Induction would <b>lower</b> the level, and it takes days to weeks in any case.","D":"Vd is a property of the drug's tissue distribution and does not change with dose here.","E":"Correct. Phenytoin's metabolism saturates at therapeutic concentrations. Once the enzymes are working flat out, a constant <b>amount</b> is cleared per hour regardless of level — <b>zero-order kinetics</b> — so any extra dose accumulates and the level rises disproportionately. This is why phenytoin has no fixed half-life and needs level monitoring."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L2_27.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|8","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"8","t":"Renal excretion","s":"A drug is 95% bound to plasma proteins and is eliminated entirely by the kidney. Its renal clearance is measured at 120 mL/min. What must be happening?","lec":"L2","sl":25,"c":"Slide 25's three renal processes: <b>glomerular filtration</b> — only <b>unbound</b> drug, and capped at GFR (~120 mL/min) · <b>active tubular secretion</b> — carrier-mediated, saturable, works on bound drug too, and can push clearance <b>above</b> GFR · <b>tubular reabsorption</b> — passive, favours un-ionised lipophilic drug, and is what pH manipulation targets. The classic secretion interaction: <b>probenecid blocks penicillin's tubular secretion</b>, raising and prolonging its level — the potentiation example from the Ax bank.","o":{"A":"The drug must in fact be unbound","B":"Glomerular filtration alone, since 120 mL/min is the normal GFR","C":"Extensive tubular reabsorption","D":"Active tubular secretion, because clearance far exceeds what filtration alone could achieve","E":"The measurement must be an error, since renal clearance cannot exceed GFR"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"The binding is given in the stem; the question is what the number implies.","B":"120 mL/min is the normal GFR for <b>unbound</b> substances such as inulin. A 95%-bound drug cannot reach it by filtration.","C":"Reabsorption would <b>reduce</b> clearance, not raise it.","D":"Correct. <b>Only the unbound drug is filtered.</b> With 95% bound, filtration could clear at most 5% of the GFR — about <b>6 mL/min</b>. A measured 120 mL/min is twenty times that, so an additional mechanism must be adding drug to the urine: <b>active tubular secretion</b>.","E":"Renal clearance <b>can</b> exceed GFR — that is precisely what tubular secretion allows, and para-aminohippuric acid is the classic example."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L2_25.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|9","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"9","t":"Enzyme inducer time course","s":"A patient on warfarin starts rifampicin for tuberculosis. When should the INR be most closely watched for a fall?","lec":"L3","sl":20,"c":"<b>The two directions differ in speed as well as effect, and stems are often built around the timing:</b><br><b>INHIBITION</b> — immediate (hours), because existing enzyme is blocked. Substrate level <b>rises</b> → toxicity.<br><b>INDUCTION</b> — slow (days to weeks), because new enzyme must be made; equally slow to reverse. Substrate level <b>falls</b> → treatment failure, and <b>rebound toxicity on withdrawal</b>.<br>This is the mechanism behind the Ax bank's tranquilliser-and-warfarin vignette, and behind BM30's phenobarbital-and-losartan item.","o":{"A":"After 6 months of treatment","B":"Within hours of the first rifampicin dose","C":"Only after rifampicin is stopped","D":"Over the following 1-2 weeks, as new enzyme protein is synthesised","E":"The INR will not change, because rifampicin does not affect warfarin"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Far too late; the effect is fully established long before.","B":"That is the time course of <b>inhibition</b>, which is immediate because the existing enzyme is simply blocked.","C":"The danger on stopping is the <b>opposite</b> — induction fades, warfarin accumulates, and the patient bleeds. That also needs monitoring, but the fall in INR happens on starting.","D":"Correct. <b>Induction requires synthesis of new enzyme protein</b>, so it builds over roughly <b>1-2 weeks</b> and wears off over a similar period. The INR drifts down during that window and the warfarin dose has to be raised.","E":"Rifampicin is one of the strongest CYP inducers known and this interaction is clinically significant."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_20.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|10","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"10","t":"Vd and dialysis","s":"A patient has taken an overdose of a drug with a volume of distribution of 20 L/kg. Would haemodialysis be effective?","lec":"L2","sl":12,"c":"Read Vd as <b>'where is the drug?'</b>, and the clinical consequences follow. <b>Small Vd (≤ 5 L)</b> — confined to plasma; <b>dialysable</b>; loading dose small. <b>Large Vd (&gt; 15 L, and especially &gt; 5 L/kg)</b> — tissue-bound; <b>not dialysable</b>; long half-life, because only the plasma fraction is exposed to clearing organs; loading dose large. Drugs that <i>are</i> worth dialysing share small Vd, low protein binding and low molecular weight: lithium, salicylate, methanol, ethylene glycol.","o":{"A":"No — a very large Vd means almost all the drug is in tissue, and dialysis can only remove what is in plasma","B":"Yes — a large Vd means there is a large amount of drug available to remove","C":"Yes — Vd has no bearing on dialysability","D":"No — because a large Vd always means the drug is not water-soluble enough to cross the dialysis membrane","E":"No — because a large Vd means the drug has already been eliminated"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A Vd of 20 L/kg (about 1400 L in a 70 kg adult) is many times total body water, so the drug is overwhelmingly bound in <b>tissue</b>. Dialysis can only clear the small plasma compartment, and removing it barely dents the total body burden — the tissue simply re-equilibrates.","B":"The total amount is large, but the <b>accessible</b> amount is not, which is what matters.","C":"Vd is one of the main determinants of whether dialysis is worth attempting.","D":"The reasoning is wrong: dialysability depends on molecular size, protein binding and <b>Vd</b>, and a large Vd usually reflects tissue binding rather than an inability to cross the membrane.","E":"A large Vd says nothing about how much has been eliminated."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L2_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|11","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"11","t":"Spare receptors","s":"In an isolated tissue, a full agonist produces its maximal response when only <b>20%</b> of receptors are occupied. What does this demonstrate?","lec":"L3","sl":7,"c":"Slide 7 names <b>spare receptors</b> alongside Kd and receptor occupancy. The consequence worth understanding: with spare receptors, <b>EC50 &lt; Kd</b> — half-maximal <i>effect</i> occurs at a lower concentration than half-maximal <i>occupancy</i>. It also means an <b>irreversible</b> antagonist can at first look competitive: while spare receptors remain, the maximum is still attainable and the curve merely shifts right; only once the reserve is used up does Emax start to fall.","o":{"A":"Spare receptors are present","B":"The agonist is a partial agonist","C":"The receptors have low affinity","D":"A non-competitive antagonist is present","E":"The tissue is desensitised"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. When maximal response is reached well before full occupancy, the remaining receptors are <b>spare (reserve) receptors</b>. Their functional point is to increase <b>sensitivity</b>: the tissue responds to a lower agonist concentration than receptor occupancy alone would predict, so EC50 falls below Kd.","B":"A partial agonist cannot reach the maximal response at any occupancy — the stem says maximal response was achieved.","C":"Low affinity would require <i>more</i> agonist, not less.","D":"A non-competitive antagonist reduces the maximum; here it was reached.","E":"Desensitisation reduces the response; here it is full."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_7.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|12","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"12","t":"Allosteric modulation","s":"Two drugs act on the same receptor but bind at <b>different sites</b>, and one changes the other's affinity. What is this interaction called?","lec":"L3","sl":12,"c":"Slide 12 names <b>allosteric modulator</b> explicitly alongside the non-competitive antagonists. The full antagonism taxonomy, sorted by <i>where</i> the interaction happens — <b>same site</b> on the same receptor → competitive/irreversible · <b>different site</b> on the same receptor → allosteric · <b>different receptor</b>, opposite effect → physiological/functional · <b>no receptor</b>, direct reaction → chemical · <b>concentration</b> altered → pharmacokinetic. The Ax bank adds the nuance that non-competitive binding at the <b>active</b> site is irreversible only, whereas <b>allosteric</b> binding can be reversible or irreversible.","o":{"A":"Physiological antagonism","B":"Competitive antagonism","C":"Chemical antagonism","D":"Allosteric modulation","E":"Pharmacokinetic antagonism"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Physiological antagonism involves <b>different receptors</b> and opposite effects on one endpoint.","B":"Competitive antagonism requires binding at the <b>same</b> site as the agonist.","C":"Chemical antagonism involves no receptor at all — the molecules react with each other.","D":"Correct. <b>Allosteric</b> means 'other site'. A modulator binding away from the orthosteric (agonist) site changes the receptor's conformation and thereby the agonist's <b>affinity or efficacy</b>. It can be positive or negative — benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators at the GABA-A receptor.","E":"Pharmacokinetic antagonism is one drug altering the other's concentration."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|13","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"13","t":"Gasotransmitters","s":"Which enzyme-and-messenger pair correctly matches its gasotransmitter?","lec":"L3","sl":15,"c":"Prof DA's slide 15 covers the three gaseous signalling molecules, and he examines his <b>own published work</b> on two of them — Moringa items have appeared in four cohorts.<br><b>NO</b>: L-arginine —(<b>NOS</b>)→ NO → <b>soluble guanylyl cyclase</b> → <b>cGMP</b> → PKG → vasodilation.<br><b>H₂S</b>: cysteine —(<b>CSE / CBS</b>)→ H₂S → K<sub>ATP</sub> channels → vasodilation.<br><b>CO</b>: haem —(<b>haem oxygenase</b>)→ CO.<br>In the exam, identify the <b>gas</b> named in the stem first, then answer only from that gas's own pathway — the distractors are always taken from the other two.","o":{"A":"Nitric oxide — haem oxygenase","B":"Nitric oxide — cystathionine-γ-lyase","C":"H₂S — soluble guanylyl cyclase","D":"Carbon monoxide — nitric oxide synthase","E":"H₂S — cystathionine-γ-lyase"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"<b>Haem oxygenase</b> is the CO-generating enzyme, not NO's.","B":"NO is made by <b>nitric oxide synthase</b> from L-arginine.","C":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase is <b>NO's</b> downstream effector, converting GTP to cGMP.","D":"Nitric oxide synthase makes NO, not CO.","E":"Correct. <b>Cystathionine-γ-lyase (CSE)</b> — and cystathionine-β-synthase — generate <b>hydrogen sulfide</b> from cysteine in the vascular endothelium."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_15.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|14","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"14","t":"Tachyphylaxis vs tolerance","s":"A patient with nasal congestion uses a topical decongestant spray several times over a few hours. Each dose works less well than the last. What is this?","lec":"L3","sl":17,"c":"⚠️ <b>Tachyphylaxis and tolerance are the pair the examiners rely on you confusing, and the discriminator is always TIME.</b><br><b>Tachyphylaxis</b> — minutes to hours, a few doses, rapid receptor desensitisation, often mediator depletion. Decongestant sprays, nitrates given continuously, amphetamine.<br><b>Tolerance</b> — days to weeks, receptor down-regulation, needs dose escalation. Opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol.<br>Slide 17 lists both, along with resistance and intolerance. Read the stem for a duration and the answer usually falls out.","o":{"A":"Drug intolerance","B":"Drug tolerance","C":"Drug resistance","D":"Down-regulation","E":"Tachyphylaxis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Intolerance means an exaggerated response at an unusually small dose.","B":"<b>Tolerance</b> develops over days to weeks. The sleeping-pill-for-2-months item in three cohorts is the tolerance version of this question.","C":"Resistance applies to organisms and tumours.","D":"Receptor down-regulation is one <i>mechanism</i> underlying tolerance, not the name of this rapid clinical phenomenon.","E":"Correct. <b>Tachyphylaxis</b> is a <b>rapid</b> loss of response over minutes to hours with a few closely spaced doses. Topical sympathomimetic decongestants are the classic example — repeated use depletes the mediator and desensitises the receptor, and continued use produces rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa)."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_17.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|15","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"15","t":"Prodrugs","s":"A patient with severe liver disease needs a corticosteroid. Why is <b>prednisolone</b> preferred over prednisone?","lec":"L7","sl":6,"c":"<b>Prodrugs are worth collecting as a set, because 'it must be activated' is a recurring answer:</b> <b>prednisone → prednisolone</b> (hepatic 11-β-HSD) · <b>cyclophosphamide</b> (hepatic CYP; its metabolite <b>acrolein</b> causes the cystitis) · <b>6-mercaptopurine</b> (activated by HGPRT, inactivated by xanthine oxidase) · <b>amifostine</b> (activated by alkaline phosphatase) · <b>enalapril → enalaprilat</b> · <b>codeine → morphine</b> (CYP2D6). A prodrug that is not converted does nothing — which is why the site of activation matters clinically.","o":{"A":"Prednisone is more potent and would be dangerous in liver disease","B":"Prednisone is a prodrug that must be converted to prednisolone by the liver","C":"Prednisolone has no mineralocorticoid activity, unlike prednisone","D":"Prednisone is eliminated by the kidney, not the liver","E":"Prednisolone is short-acting and therefore safer"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The two are essentially equipotent (4 / 0.8 on the potency table) — they are the same molecule before and after activation.","B":"Correct. <b>Prednisone is a prodrug.</b> It must be converted by hepatic 11-β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase to the active <b>prednisolone</b>. In severe hepatic impairment that conversion is unreliable, so the active drug is given directly. The lecturer stressed that an unconverted prodrug has <b>no effect at all</b>.","C":"Both carry the same modest mineralocorticoid activity (0.8).","D":"Both are hepatically metabolised.","E":"Both are intermediate-acting, 12-36 hours."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_6.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|16","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"16","t":"Type A vs type B reactions","s":"A patient on a beta-blocker develops bradycardia. How is this adverse reaction classified?","lec":"L3","sl":4,"c":"Slide 4 opens the pharmacodynamics lecture with this split, and it frames the whole drug-allergy chapter:<br><b>Type A (Augmented)</b> — dose-related, <b>predictable</b> from the drug's pharmacology, common, low mortality, managed by reducing the dose. Includes side effects, toxicity, overdose.<br><b>Type B (Bizarre)</b> — <b>not</b> dose-related, <b>unpredictable</b>, uncommon, higher mortality, requires stopping the drug. Includes <b>drug allergy</b>, anaphylaxis and idiosyncratic reactions.<br>The exam's favourite type-A/type-B decision point is the carbamazepine SJS vignette, where 'type A reaction' is always offered as a distractor.","o":{"A":"Type B — bizarre and immunologically mediated","B":"Type A — augmented, dose-related and predictable from the drug's pharmacology","C":"A type I hypersensitivity reaction","D":"An idiosyncratic reaction","E":"A type IV hypersensitivity reaction"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Type B reactions are <b>not</b> predictable from pharmacology and are not dose-related — drug allergy, and idiosyncratic reactions.","B":"Correct. Bradycardia is simply <b>too much of what the drug is meant to do</b> — β1 blockade slows the heart. It is dose-related, predictable, common, and it resolves on dose reduction. That is a <b>type A (augmented)</b> reaction.","C":"Type I hypersensitivity is IgE-mediated anaphylaxis, occurring within minutes.","D":"Idiosyncratic reactions are a subset of type B — unexpected and unrelated to known pharmacology.","E":"Type IV is the delayed, T-cell mediated reaction behind SJS/TEN and contact dermatitis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3_4.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|17","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"17","t":"Gell and Coombs classification","s":"Fifteen minutes after an intravenous antibiotic a patient develops urticaria, wheeze and hypotension. Which hypersensitivity type is this?","lec":"L3B","sl":3,"c":"<b>Timing separates the four types more reliably than the immunology does:</b><br><b>Type I</b> — minutes to 1 hour. IgE. Urticaria, angio-oedema, bronchospasm, anaphylaxis.<br><b>Type II</b> — days. IgG/IgM against cells. Haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia.<br><b>Type III</b> — 1-3 weeks. Immune complexes. Serum sickness, vasculitis.<br><b>Type IV</b> — days to weeks. T cells. Subdivided: <b>IVa</b> Th1/macrophage, <b>IVb</b> Th2/eosinophil (<b>DRESS</b>), <b>IVc</b> cytotoxic T cell (<b>SJS/TEN</b>), <b>IVd</b> neutrophil (<b>AGEP</b>). The chapter notes that this classification is hard to apply at the bedside, which is why the international consensus splits drug reactions simply into <b>immediate</b> and <b>non-immediate</b>.","o":{"A":"Type III — immune complex","B":"Type II — cytotoxic, IgG/IgM against cell surfaces","C":"Type I — IgE-mediated","D":"Type IVa — Th1-mediated","E":"Type IVc — cytotoxic T-cell mediated"},"a":"C","r":{"A":"Type III is immune-complex deposition — serum sickness, vasculitis. Onset 1-3 weeks.","B":"Type II is antibody against a cell surface — drug-induced haemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia. Onset is over days.","C":"Correct. Onset within minutes with urticaria, bronchospasm and hypotension is <b>anaphylaxis</b> — <b>type I</b>, IgE-mediated mast-cell and basophil degranulation releasing histamine, leukotrienes and PAF.","D":"Type IVa is Th1/macrophage-driven, as in contact dermatitis and tuberculin reactions. Delayed by days.","E":"Type IVc is CD8 cytotoxic T-cell mediated and underlies <b>SJS/TEN</b>. Delayed by 1-3 weeks."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3B_3.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|18","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"18","t":"Skin prick testing","s":"A patient reports a rash after amoxicillin some years ago and now needs a penicillin. Where does <b>skin prick testing</b> sit in the assessment?","lec":"L3B","sl":10,"c":"The diagnostic ladder, in ascending order of risk — which is the whole logic of the chapter: <b>history</b> → <b>skin prick test</b> (immediate reactions, quick, safe) → <b>intradermal test</b> (more sensitive, more risk) → <b>patch test</b> (delayed reactions) → <b>in vitro tests</b> (specific IgE, basophil activation) → <b>drug provocation test</b>, the <b>gold standard</b> but the most dangerous, done only under supervision with resuscitation available. ⚠️ Skin testing is <b>absolutely contraindicated</b> after SJS, TEN or DRESS.","o":{"A":"It is the quick first-line skin test, done before intradermal testing","B":"It is the definitive test and makes drug provocation unnecessary","C":"It is performed only after a drug provocation test has been positive","D":"It is used to diagnose delayed T-cell mediated reactions such as SJS","E":"It should be done during the acute reaction for maximum sensitivity"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. The <b>skin prick test (SPT)</b> is the fast, low-risk first step of skin testing. If it is negative, <b>intradermal testing</b> follows, and only then — if still negative and the need is real — a <b>drug provocation test</b>.","B":"A negative SPT does not exclude allergy. The <b>drug provocation test is the gold standard</b>, which is exactly why it sits last.","C":"The order is the reverse: least risky first.","D":"SPT detects <b>immediate, IgE-mediated</b> reactions. Delayed reactions need <b>patch testing</b> or delayed-reading intradermal tests.","E":"Testing during the acute episode is unsafe and unreliable; it is deferred, usually by 4-6 weeks."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3B_10.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|19","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"19","t":"Haptens","s":"Most drugs are too small to be recognised by the immune system on their own. How does a small drug molecule provoke an allergic response?","lec":"L3B","sl":5,"c":"Three routes by which a drug engages the immune system: <b>1. Hapten</b> — small molecule binds covalently to protein (penicillin is the archetype). <b>2. Prohapten</b> — requires metabolic activation before it can bind (sulfamethoxazole). <b>3. The p-i concept</b> — <i>pharmacological interaction with immune receptors</i>: the drug binds T-cell receptors or MHC <b>non-covalently</b>, with no processing at all, which explains why some reactions occur on <b>first</b> exposure. Risk factors: larger molecular size, parenteral and topical routes, repeated or intermittent courses, female sex, certain HLA alleles.","o":{"A":"It stimulates B cells directly without any T-cell involvement","B":"It directly cross-links IgE on mast cells without any protein involvement","C":"It is recognised because small molecules are inherently more immunogenic","D":"It must first be excreted and then reabsorbed as a larger metabolite","E":"It acts as a hapten, binding covalently to a body protein to form a complete antigen"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"T-cell help is central to drug allergy, and the p-i concept describes drugs stimulating T-cell receptors directly.","B":"Cross-linking IgE is what happens on <b>re-exposure</b>, after sensitisation. It does not explain how sensitisation occurred.","C":"The opposite is true — small size is why they cannot act as antigens alone.","D":"Not a recognised mechanism.","E":"Correct. A molecule under roughly <b>1,000 daltons</b> cannot by itself be immunogenic. It acts as a <b>hapten</b>: binding <b>covalently</b> to a carrier protein, the hapten-carrier conjugate is processed and presented, and an immune response follows. Some drugs are <b>prohaptens</b> — they must be metabolised before they can bind."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3B_5.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|20","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"20","t":"Anaphylaxis treatment","s":"A patient in anaphylactic shock needs adrenaline. Which is correct?","lec":"L3B","sl":12,"c":"Anaphylaxis is where L3B and L5 meet, and it is also the cleanest clinical example of <b>physiological antagonism</b>: adrenaline reverses histamine's effects through entirely different receptors — <b>α1</b> vasoconstriction (reversing the hypotension), <b>β1</b> inotropy and chronotropy, <b>β2</b> bronchodilation and mast-cell stabilisation. Remember the four numbers: <b>1:1000 · 0.3-0.5 mg · IM anterolateral thigh · repeat every 5-15 min</b>.","o":{"A":"0.3-0.5 mg of 1:10,000 intravenously as a first-line bolus","B":"0.3-0.5 mg of 1:1000 intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh, repeated every 5-15 minutes if needed","C":"1 mg of 1:1000 subcutaneously, as a single dose","D":"An intravenous antihistamine first, with adrenaline reserved for non-response","E":"Intravenous hydrocortisone first, as it acts fastest"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"The <b>1:10,000</b> concentration and the <b>IV</b> route are reserved for cardiac arrest or profound refractory shock with monitoring — giving 1:1000 IV, or IV adrenaline routinely, risks fatal arrhythmia. Concentration and route are the two things most often confused here.","B":"Correct. <b>IM adrenaline into the anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis) is first-line</b>, at <b>0.3-0.5 mg of 1:1000</b> for an adult, repeatable every <b>5-15 minutes</b>. The thigh gives faster, more reliable absorption than the deltoid or the subcutaneous route.","C":"Subcutaneous absorption is slow and unreliable in shock, when peripheral perfusion has failed; and a single dose is not enough.","D":"Antihistamines are <b>adjuncts only</b>. They do nothing for the airway or the circulation, and delaying adrenaline for them costs lives.","E":"Steroids act over <b>hours</b> — they may reduce the biphasic response but are useless acutely."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L3B_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|21","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"21","t":"Bioequivalence criteria","s":"A generic manufacturer submits bioequivalence data. What must be shown for the product to be declared bioequivalent to the originator?","lec":"L4","sl":45,"c":"Why <b>80-125%</b> rather than a symmetric 80-120%? Because the analysis is done on the <b>log scale</b>, where 0.8 and 1.25 are symmetric (1/0.8 = 1.25). And why the <b>90%</b> CI? Because bioequivalence uses <b>two one-sided tests</b>, each at 5%. The three parameters compared are <b>AUC</b> (total exposure), <b>Cmax</b> (peak) and <b>Tmax</b> (time to peak). ⚠️ The commonest misconception, which slides 46-47 address directly, is that bioequivalence means the two products are <i>identical</i> — it means their exposure profiles are statistically indistinguishable within these limits.","o":{"A":"A randomised controlled trial shows non-inferior clinical outcomes","B":"The 95% confidence interval for the AUC ratio lies within 80-120%","C":"The mean AUC is within 80-125% of the originator's","D":"The 90% confidence interval for the AUC and Cmax ratios lies within 80-125%","E":"The generic contains the identical excipients as the originator"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"A clinical outcome trial is not required for a generic; that is what bioequivalence <i>replaces</i>.","B":"Both figures are wrong. It is the <b>90%</b> CI, corresponding to two one-sided tests at α = 0.05, and the range is <b>80-125%</b>, not 80-120%.","C":"The point estimate is not enough — it is the <b>confidence interval</b> that must lie within the limits. That is the whole statistical point.","D":"Correct, and these are the two numbers prof SN said were worth memorising: the <b>90% confidence interval</b> of the geometric mean ratio (test/reference) for both <b>AUC</b> and <b>Cmax</b> must fall entirely within <b>80-125%</b>.","E":"Excipients may differ. Only the active ingredient and the pharmacokinetic performance must match."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L4_45.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|22","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"22","t":"Biosimilars","s":"Why can a biosimilar not be approved by the same bioequivalence route as a small-molecule generic?","lec":"L4","sl":56,"c":"The <b>four pillars of totality of evidence</b> from slides 56-60: <b>1. analytical</b> studies (structure and function — the foundation) · <b>2. pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic</b> studies · <b>3. clinical</b> studies · <b>4. immunogenicity</b> studies. Two derived concepts the lecture stresses: <b>extrapolation of indications</b> — approval in one indication may be extended to others without separate trials, if justified by the totality of evidence — and <b>interchangeability</b>, a higher standard than biosimilarity that permits substitution without the prescriber's involvement. Prof SN named the last three topics (BE, generic vs originator, biosimilars) as the <b>emphasised</b> ones.","o":{"A":"Biologics are large, complex and heterogeneous, so approval rests on a totality of evidence rather than pharmacokinetics alone","B":"Biosimilars are always less effective than the originator","C":"Biologics have no measurable pharmacokinetics","D":"Biosimilars are exempt from clinical testing altogether","E":"The 80-125% criterion cannot be calculated for injectable products"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. A biologic is a large protein produced in living cells; it cannot be reproduced exactly, only made <b>highly similar</b>. Approval therefore rests on a <b>totality of evidence</b>: analytical, pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic, clinical and immunogenicity data, built up as a pyramid with analytical characterisation as the foundation.","B":"A biosimilar must show <b>no clinically meaningful difference</b> in efficacy or safety — that is the approval standard.","C":"Biologics do have measurable pharmacokinetics; PK/PD studies are one of the four pillars. They are just not sufficient on their own.","D":"Clinical studies are one of the four pillars, though they may be abbreviated where analytical similarity is strong.","E":"Injectable generics can and do use bioequivalence criteria; the issue is molecular complexity, not the route."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L4_56.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|23","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"23","t":"Narrow therapeutic index generics","s":"Which situation calls for the <b>most caution</b> when switching a patient from an originator to a generic product?","lec":"L4","sl":51,"c":"Slides 50-52 give three clinical scenarios, and this is the shape of the reasoning: <b>1. Switch freely</b> — wide therapeutic index, effect easily monitored clinically (most antibiotics, analgesics, antihypertensives). <b>2. Switch with caution and monitoring</b> — <b>narrow therapeutic index</b> (warfarin, phenytoin, digoxin, ciclosporin, levothyroxine, lithium) or a modified-release formulation whose release mechanism differs between products. <b>3. Consider individual factors</b> — the patient's stability, comorbidity, and ability to be monitored. The examinable principle is that <b>bioequivalent does not mean identical</b>, and how much that residual difference matters depends on the therapeutic index.","o":{"A":"A patient stabilised on warfarin","B":"A patient taking paracetamol for occasional headache","C":"A patient starting amoxicillin for a chest infection","D":"A patient on a proton pump inhibitor for reflux","E":"A patient using a topical emollient"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Warfarin has a narrow therapeutic index.</b> A shift in exposure that is perfectly acceptable under the 80-125% bioequivalence rule can still move a stabilised patient's INR into a bleeding or clotting range — so any switch needs monitoring.","B":"Paracetamol has a wide margin at therapeutic doses and is taken intermittently.","C":"Amoxicillin has a wide therapeutic index, and it is a new course rather than a switch mid-therapy.","D":"PPIs have a wide margin and their effect is not titrated to a laboratory value.","E":"A topical emollient has essentially no systemic exposure."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L4_51.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|24","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"24","t":"Phase IV surveillance","s":"A newly marketed antihypertensive is being prescribed widely. Why can a rare adverse effect emerge now that Phase III did not detect?","lec":"L4","sl":23,"c":"Slide 23 is titled <b>ข้อจำกัดของ Phase 3 ที่ Phase 4 ต้องมาแก้ไข</b> — the limitations of Phase III that Phase IV exists to correct: <b>too few</b> patients (rare events missed), <b>too short</b> (delayed effects missed), <b>too narrow</b> (excluded populations), <b>too simple</b> (no polypharmacy). Phase IV tools, from slide 24: <b>spontaneous reporting</b> (the mainstay, but under-reports), <b>active surveillance</b>, <b>registries</b> and <b>database studies</b>. <b>Mibefradil</b> on slide 21 is the worked example of a drug withdrawn post-marketing.","o":{"A":"Regulators do not review safety data before approval","B":"Phase III does not assess safety at all","C":"The drug's chemistry changes after marketing","D":"Phase III enrols too few patients, for too short a time, under too restrictive entry criteria","E":"Phase III studies use only healthy volunteers"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Regulators review the entire safety dossier before approval — that is the NDA review.","B":"Phase III assesses safety extensively; it simply cannot detect rare or delayed events.","C":"The molecule does not change.","D":"Correct, and all three limitations matter. Phase III typically enrols a few thousand patients — too few to detect an event occurring in 1 in 10,000 — over months rather than years, and with <b>exclusion criteria</b> that keep out the elderly, the pregnant, children and patients with comorbidities or polypharmacy. Real-world use has none of those protections.","E":"Healthy volunteers are <b>Phase I</b>. Phase III studies patients."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L4_23.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|25","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"25","t":"AUC, Cmax and Tmax","s":"Two formulations of the same drug have <b>identical AUC</b>, but formulation B has a lower Cmax and a later Tmax. What does this indicate?","lec":"L4","sl":33,"c":"Read the concentration-time curve as answering two separate questions: <b>AUC → how much</b> (extent of absorption, bioavailability, total exposure) and <b>Cmax / Tmax → how fast</b> (rate of absorption). ⚠️ Two formulations can be equal in extent and quite different in rate — which is why bioequivalence requires <b>both</b> AUC and Cmax to fall within 80-125%, not AUC alone. Clinically the distinction matters: for a single-dose analgesic the rate is what the patient notices; for a chronic maintenance drug the extent matters more.","o":{"A":"Lower bioavailability of formulation B","B":"The same total exposure, but slower absorption from formulation B","C":"Higher clearance of formulation B","D":"Formulation B is not absorbed at all","E":"Formulation B has a larger volume of distribution"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Bioavailability is read off <b>AUC</b>, which is identical.","B":"Correct. <b>AUC measures total exposure</b>; identical AUC means the same amount of drug reached the circulation, so bioavailability is the same. <b>Cmax and Tmax describe the RATE</b> — a lower, later peak means slower absorption. That is exactly what a sustained-release formulation is designed to do.","C":"Clearance is a property of the drug and the patient, not of the formulation.","D":"It is fully absorbed — the AUC proves it.","E":"Vd is likewise a drug property, unaffected by formulation."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L4_33.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|26","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"26","t":"Histamine receptor subtypes","s":"Which histamine receptor mediates the <b>negative feedback</b> control of histamine release in the brain?","lec":"L5","sl":6,"c":"⚠️ <b>Slide 6 is IMAGE-ONLY — the H1-H4 receptor table is a picture, invisible to any text search of the PDF.</b> The four receptors: <b>H1</b> — allergy, bronchoconstriction, itch, CNS wakefulness (Gq) · <b>H2</b> — gastric acid (Gs) · <b>H3</b> — presynaptic autoreceptor, negative feedback in brain (Gi) · <b>H4</b> — immune-cell chemotaxis. The lecturer said the <b>H3 laboratory tools (thioperamide, clobenpropit) and H4 are NOT emphasised</b> — know that H3 is the autoreceptor and that betahistine antagonises it, and move on.","o":{"A":"H1","B":"H3","C":"H2","D":"H4","E":"Both H1 and H2 equally"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"H1 mediates allergy, bronchoconstriction, itch, vasodilation and CNS wakefulness.","B":"Correct. <b>H3</b> is a presynaptic <b>autoreceptor</b> in the CNS. Histamine acting on it <b>inhibits its own further release</b> — negative feedback. Betahistine's H3 <i>antagonism</i> is part of how it works, by removing that brake.","C":"H2 mediates gastric acid secretion from parietal cells.","D":"H4 is on immune cells and mediates chemotaxis. The lecturer said explicitly that <b>H4 is not emphasised</b>.","E":"Neither H1 nor H2 is an autoreceptor."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L5_6.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|27","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"27","t":"Leukotriene modifiers","s":"A patient with mild persistent asthma asks whether montelukast can be his only regular medicine. What is the correct position?","lec":"L5","sl":23,"c":"The two ways to interrupt the leukotriene arm, and where each sits: <b>Zileuton</b> — inhibits <b>5-lipoxygenase</b>, so no leukotrienes are made; ⚠️ hepatotoxic. <b>Montelukast / zafirlukast</b> — block the <b>CysLT₁ receptor</b>; leukotrienes are still made but cannot act. Slide 23 lists what leukotrienes do in asthma: bronchoconstriction, mucus secretion, oedema and eosinophil recruitment. Their role also explains <b>AERD</b>: blocking COX with aspirin shunts arachidonic acid down the lipoxygenase pathway, and a steroid — acting above the branch point at <b>phospholipase A2</b> — is what suppresses both arms at once.","o":{"A":"No — zileuton should be used instead as first-line","B":"Yes — montelukast is first-line for all asthma severities","C":"Yes, provided the dose is doubled","D":"No — an inhaled corticosteroid is first-line; montelukast is an add-on or alternative","E":"No — a long-acting β2 agonist alone is first-line"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"<b>Zileuton</b> is a 5-lipoxygenase <b>inhibitor</b>, is <b>hepatotoxic</b> and requires liver monitoring — the lecturer flagged this. It is not first-line.","B":"It is less effective than an inhaled steroid as monotherapy.","C":"Dose escalation does not change its place in therapy.","D":"Correct, and the lecturer stated it explicitly off-slide. <b>Inhaled corticosteroids are first-line controller therapy</b> in persistent asthma. Montelukast is a leukotriene <b>receptor</b> antagonist used as an add-on, or as an alternative when steroids cannot be used — particularly where allergic rhinitis or exercise-induced symptoms coexist.","E":"A LABA must <b>never</b> be used alone in asthma — monotherapy increases the risk of severe exacerbations and death. It is always combined with an inhaled steroid."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L5_23.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|28","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"28","t":"Prostaglandin physiology","s":"A patient on long-term NSAIDs develops a gastric ulcer but must continue the NSAID. Which drug specifically replaces what the NSAID has removed?","lec":"L5","sl":18,"c":"<b>What the prostaglandins were doing before the NSAID blocked them</b> is the key to every NSAID adverse effect: <b>gastric</b> PGE₂ maintains mucus, bicarbonate and mucosal blood flow → NSAID causes ulcers; <b>renal</b> PGE₂ and PGI₂ maintain afferent arteriolar dilation → NSAID causes acute kidney injury in the volume-depleted; <b>platelet</b> TXA₂ drives aggregation → aspirin's antiplatelet action; <b>ductus arteriosus</b> patency depends on PGE₁ → NSAIDs close it. ⚠️ <b>Misoprostol is contraindicated in pregnancy</b> — it induces uterine contraction, which is the very property BM29 examined from the obstetric side.","o":{"A":"Misoprostol","B":"Omeprazole","C":"Famotidine","D":"Sucralfate","E":"Ondansetron"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. NSAIDs cause ulcers by blocking <b>COX-1</b>, and therefore the <b>PGE₂</b> that maintains gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow. <b>Misoprostol is a PGE₁ analogue</b>, so it puts back the missing prostaglandin — it is the only option that addresses the actual mechanism.","B":"A <b>PPI</b> is in practice the usual choice, and is better tolerated — but it works by suppressing <b>acid</b>, not by replacing prostaglandin. The stem asks specifically for replacement.","C":"An H2 blocker also suppresses acid, and less effectively than a PPI.","D":"Sucralfate coats the ulcer base physically; it does not restore prostaglandin.","E":"Ondansetron is an antiemetic with no gastroprotective role."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L5_18.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|29","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"29","t":"Serotonin and the gut","s":"Which statement about serotonin correctly matches receptor to clinical use?","lec":"L5","sl":15,"c":"⚠️ <b>Every distractor here is right about the drug and wrong about the direction.</b> The examiners do this repeatedly, so learn each pairing with its direction attached:<br><b>5-HT₁B/₁D AGONIST</b> — sumatriptan, migraine<br><b>5-HT₁A partial agonist</b> — buspirone, anxiety<br><b>5-HT₂ ANTAGONIST</b> — ketanserin<br><b>5-HT₃ ANTAGONIST</b> — <b>ondansetron, granisetron</b>, chemotherapy emesis<br><b>5-HT₄ AGONIST</b> — cisapride, tegaserod, prokinetic<br><b>Reuptake inhibitor</b> — fluoxetine (SSRI), also emphasised in this lecture","o":{"A":"5-HT₃ agonists such as buspirone treat anxiety","B":"5-HT₃ antagonists such as ondansetron control chemotherapy-induced vomiting","C":"5-HT₄ antagonists such as cisapride are prokinetic","D":"5-HT₁ antagonists such as sumatriptan treat migraine","E":"5-HT₂ agonists such as ketanserin lower blood pressure"},"a":"B","r":{"A":"Buspirone is a <b>5-HT₁A</b> partial agonist, not 5-HT₃.","B":"Correct. <b>5-HT₃ receptors</b> are on vagal afferents and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone; chemotherapy causes enterochromaffin cells to release serotonin onto them. <b>Ondansetron and granisetron are 5-HT₃ ANTAGONISTS</b>.","C":"Cisapride is a 5-HT₄ <b>AGONIST</b> — agonism at 5-HT₄ is what makes it prokinetic.","D":"Sumatriptan is a 5-HT₁B/₁D <b>AGONIST</b>. Agonism constricts the dilated cranial vessels; an antagonist would do nothing useful.","E":"Ketanserin is a 5-HT₂ <b>ANTAGONIST</b>."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L5_15.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|30","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"30","t":"NSAID cardiovascular safety","s":"An elderly patient with ischaemic heart disease needs a regular NSAID for osteoarthritis. Which is the safest choice with respect to cardiovascular risk?","lec":"L5","sl":20,"c":"The prostacyclin/thromboxane balance explains the whole class effect: <b>COX-2 in endothelium makes PGI₂ (antithrombotic, vasodilator)</b>; <b>COX-1 in platelets makes TXA₂ (prothrombotic, vasoconstrictor)</b>. A COX-2-selective drug removes the brake and leaves the accelerator — hence the thrombotic risk that withdrew rofecoxib. The lecturer's practical ranking: <b>naproxen safest for cardiovascular risk</b>; diclofenac and ibuprofen carry reports; <b>coxibs contraindicated outright</b> in established cardiovascular disease. She also told the class not to memorise the NSAID chemical-group headings — 'ไม่ต้องจำกลุ่มนะคะ' — only the highlighted drug names.","o":{"A":"Ibuprofen","B":"Etoricoxib","C":"Diclofenac","D":"Celecoxib","E":"Naproxen"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Ibuprofen also carries reported risk, and it additionally <b>interferes with low-dose aspirin's antiplatelet effect</b> by competing for platelet COX-1.","B":"Etoricoxib is a coxib. <b>COX-2 selectivity raises thrombotic risk</b>: it suppresses antithrombotic prostacyclin while leaving platelet thromboxane intact. Contraindicated in established cardiovascular disease.","C":"Diclofenac now carries thrombotic reports comparable to the coxibs.","D":"Celecoxib is a coxib — the same objection.","E":"Correct. <b>Naproxen carries the most reassuring cardiovascular profile</b> of the NSAIDs — the lecturer's off-slide teaching, and she called it the drug of choice in the elderly."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L5_20.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|31","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"31","t":"Cell-cycle specificity","s":"Which group of anticancer drugs acts <b>mainly on dividing cells</b> (cell-cycle specific)?","lec":"L6","sl":3,"c":"Slide 3 opens the lecture with this division, and it has real consequences: <b>Cell-cycle SPECIFIC (CCS)</b> — taxanes, vinca alkaloids (M phase), antimetabolites (S phase), bleomycin (G2), topoisomerase inhibitors. Most effective against <b>rapidly dividing</b> tumours; their effect is <b>schedule-dependent</b>, so prolonged infusion beats a single bolus. <b>Cell-cycle NON-specific (CCNS)</b> — alkylating agents, platinum compounds, anthracyclines, nitrosoureas. Kill resting cells too; their effect is <b>dose-dependent</b>. Combination regimens deliberately mix the two, which is why ABVD and CHOP look the way they do.","o":{"A":"Anthracyclines","B":"Nitrogen mustards","C":"Nitrosoureas","D":"Platinum compounds","E":"Taxanes"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Anthracyclines intercalate DNA and inhibit topoisomerase II — largely non-specific.","B":"Nitrogen mustards are <b>alkylating agents</b> — cell-cycle <b>non</b>-specific. They damage DNA whether or not the cell is dividing.","C":"Nitrosoureas are also alkylating agents, non-specific, and lipid-soluble enough to enter the CNS.","D":"Platinum compounds cross-link DNA and are non-specific.","E":"Correct. <b>Taxanes act in M phase</b> — they poison the mitotic spindle, so they can only affect a cell that is actually dividing. Slide 3 lists taxanes among the cell-cycle-specific (CCS, phase specific) drugs."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L6_3.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|32","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"32","t":"Methotrexate rescue","s":"A patient receives high-dose methotrexate. Which drug is given as rescue, and how does it work?","lec":"L6","sl":12,"c":"Slide 12's methotrexate toxicities and their management: <b>nephrotoxicity</b> at high dose (prevented by hydration and urinary alkalinisation, since MTX precipitates in acid urine), <b>hepatotoxicity and hepatic fibrosis</b> on long-term use, <b>mucositis</b> and <b>myelosuppression</b> — the last two rescued by <b>folinic acid</b>. Learn the four protectants as a set, because the examiners ask them as one map: <b>mesna → cyclophosphamide · dexrazoxane → doxorubicin · amifostine → cisplatin · folinic acid → methotrexate</b>.","o":{"A":"Folinic acid, which supplies reduced folate downstream of the blocked enzyme","B":"Folic acid, which competes with methotrexate for its receptor","C":"Allopurinol, which reduces methotrexate's toxic metabolites","D":"Mesna, which binds methotrexate in the urine","E":"Dexrazoxane, which chelates methotrexate"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. Methotrexate inhibits <b>dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR)</b>, so dihydrofolate cannot be reduced to tetrahydrofolate. <b>Folinic acid (leucovorin) is already in the reduced form</b>, so it enters the pathway <b>downstream of the block</b> and rescues normal cells without needing DHFR at all.","B":"Plain <b>folic acid</b> would still require DHFR to be reduced — which is the very enzyme that is blocked. This is the key distinction and the reason the rescue agent must be <i>folinic</i>.","C":"Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor; its chemotherapy interaction is with 6-mercaptopurine.","D":"Mesna inactivates <b>acrolein</b> from cyclophosphamide.","E":"Dexrazoxane protects the heart from <b>doxorubicin</b>."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L6_12.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|33","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"33","t":"Alkylating agent late effects","s":"A patient cured of Hodgkin lymphoma with an alkylating-agent-containing regimen develops acute myeloid leukaemia 6 years later. What explains this?","lec":"L6","sl":4,"c":"Separate <b>acute</b> from <b>late</b> toxicity, because both get examined:<br><b>Acute</b> (shared by most cytotoxics, from killing rapidly dividing normal tissue) — myelosuppression, mucositis, alopecia, nausea and vomiting.<br><b>Late</b> — <b>secondary malignancy</b> (alkylating agents → AML), <b>infertility</b> (alkylating agents), <b>organ-specific irreversible damage</b> (doxorubicin cardiomyopathy, bleomycin pulmonary fibrosis).<br>This is the reasoning behind cure-versus-late-harm trade-offs in young patients, and it is why regimens for curable young-adult cancers have been progressively redesigned.","o":{"A":"Alkylating agents are mutagenic and carcinogenic, and can cause secondary leukaemia","B":"The original lymphoma transformed","C":"The regimen suppressed immune surveillance permanently","D":"It is coincidental and unrelated to the treatment","E":"Bleomycin causes secondary leukaemia"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. <b>Alkylating agents damage DNA directly</b>, and that damage is not confined to tumour cells. Surviving haematopoietic stem cells can carry mutations forward, and <b>secondary acute leukaemia</b> is a recognised late complication, typically 5-10 years after treatment. The L6 handout flags secondary leukaemia on its cell-death slide.","B":"Transformation of Hodgkin lymphoma to AML is not the mechanism here.","C":"Immunosuppression during treatment is real but transient, and it is not the accepted explanation for therapy-related AML.","D":"The association is well established, not coincidental.","E":"Bleomycin's signature toxicity is <b>pulmonary fibrosis</b>."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L6_4.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|34","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"34","t":"Cyclophosphamide activation","s":"Cyclophosphamide causes haemorrhagic cystitis. Which statement about the mechanism is correct?","lec":"L6","sl":5,"c":"⚠️ <b>A handout/video conflict to be aware of:</b> the recorded lecture says cyclophosphamide is excreted 'urine &gt;&gt;&gt; bile', while the handout's text layer reads 'bile &gt;&gt;&gt; urine'. <b>Answer urine</b> — it is consistent with acrolein reaching the bladder, which is the examinable fact. Slide 5 also names <b>mesna</b> and <b>N-acetylcysteine</b> as the sulfhydryl donors, and <b>amifostine</b> as the phosphatase-activated antioxidant prodrug. Note that <b>ifosfamide</b>, cyclophosphamide's close relative, causes both cystitis and a distinctive <b>encephalopathy</b> treated with methylene blue.","o":{"A":"It is concentrated in the bladder by active tubular secretion of the intact drug","B":"The parent drug is directly toxic to the bladder wall","C":"It crystallises in acid urine and abrades the bladder","D":"It causes thrombocytopenia, and the bleeding is purely haematological","E":"It is a prodrug; hepatic metabolism yields acrolein, which is excreted in urine and injures the urothelium"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The problem is the metabolite, not the intact drug.","B":"The parent drug is inactive until metabolised.","C":"Crystallisation in acid urine is <b>methotrexate</b>'s renal problem.","D":"Thrombocytopenia can occur, but the cystitis is a direct chemical injury, and mesna prevents it without affecting platelets.","E":"Correct. Cyclophosphamide is a <b>prodrug</b> activated by hepatic CYP enzymes. Among its metabolites is <b>acrolein</b>, which has no anticancer activity but is excreted in urine and is directly toxic to the bladder urothelium. <b>Mesna</b> — a sulfhydryl donor concentrated in urine — binds acrolein and inactivates it."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L6_5.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|35","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"35","t":"Tumour lysis syndrome","s":"Shortly after starting chemotherapy for a bulky lymphoma, a patient develops hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury. Which drug is given to prevent this?","lec":"L6","sl":11,"c":"Slide 11 of the handout has <b>allopurinol</b> as one of its eleven deliberate blanks — the worksheet the professor told the class to complete from the video. ⚠️ Note the connection the examiners exploit: giving allopurinol for tumour lysis is exactly what makes <b>6-mercaptopurine</b> dangerous, since xanthine oxidase also inactivates 6-MP. Three cohorts have asked that interaction. So allopurinol appears in this lecture twice — once as the solution to one problem, once as the cause of another.","o":{"A":"Amifostine","B":"Mesna","C":"Dexrazoxane","D":"Folinic acid","E":"Allopurinol"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Amifostine protects against cisplatin nephrotoxicity.","B":"Mesna prevents cyclophosphamide-induced haemorrhagic cystitis.","C":"Dexrazoxane protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity.","D":"Folinic acid rescues normal cells from methotrexate.","E":"Correct. Massive tumour cell lysis releases nucleic acids, which are catabolised to <b>uric acid</b>; urate crystals precipitate in the renal tubules and cause acute kidney injury. <b>Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase</b>, so less uric acid is formed. It is given with aggressive hydration before and during treatment. (Rasburicase is the alternative for high-risk cases.)"},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L6_11.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|36","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"36","t":"Potency table","s":"Which glucocorticoid is the most potent on the anti-inflammatory scale, and how does its mineralocorticoid activity compare?","lec":"L7","sl":6,"c":"⚠️ <b>Slide 6 is IMAGE-ONLY — the potency table is a picture and cannot be found by text search.</b> Read as glucocorticoid / mineralocorticoid, relative to hydrocortisone:<br><b>Short-acting (1-12 h)</b> — hydrocortisone <b>1 / 1</b>, cortisone 0.8 / 0.8<br><b>Intermediate (12-36 h)</b> — prednisone 4 / 0.8, prednisolone 4 / 0.8, methylprednisolone 5 / 0.5, triamcinolone 5 / 0<br><b>Long-acting (36-55 h)</b> — betamethasone 25 / 0, <b>dexamethasone 30 / 0</b><br><b>Mineralocorticoid</b> — <b>fludrocortisone 10 / 125</b><br>The lecturer said to classify on <b>two axes at once</b>: duration <i>and</i> activity profile.","o":{"A":"Dexamethasone — about 30 times hydrocortisone, with essentially no mineralocorticoid activity","B":"Hydrocortisone — the reference standard, with the highest potency","C":"Fludrocortisone — 10 times hydrocortisone, and the most potent glucocorticoid","D":"Prednisolone — 4 times hydrocortisone, with strong mineralocorticoid activity","E":"Triamcinolone — 5 times hydrocortisone, with the highest mineralocorticoid activity"},"a":"A","r":{"A":"Correct. On slide 6's table, <b>dexamethasone is 30 / 0</b> — the most potent glucocorticoid listed and essentially free of mineralocorticoid activity, which is why it is chosen when salt and water retention would be a problem (cerebral oedema, for instance). It is also long-acting, 36-55 h.","B":"Hydrocortisone is the <b>reference at 1 / 1</b> — the baseline, not the most potent.","C":"<b>Fludrocortisone is 10 / 125</b> — a modest glucocorticoid but an extremely potent <b>mineralocorticoid</b>, which is why it is used for Addison's disease.","D":"Prednisolone is 4 / 0.8 — mineralocorticoid activity is <b>low</b>, not strong.","E":"Triamcinolone is 5 / <b>0</b> — no mineralocorticoid activity at all."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_6.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|37","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"37","t":"HPA axis suppression","s":"A patient has taken prednisolone daily for 6 months. Why must the drug be tapered rather than stopped?","lec":"L7","sl":16,"c":"Slide 19's HPA axis figure (⚠️ also <b>image-only</b>) is what this rests on: <b>hypothalamus → CRH → pituitary → ACTH → adrenal cortex → cortisol</b>, with cortisol feeding back negatively on both. Three practical consequences the lecturer emphasised: <b>taper</b> after more than about 2 weeks of therapy; use <b>alternate-day (วันเว้นวัน) dosing</b> to limit suppression in the first place; and give <b>stress dosing</b> during intercurrent illness or surgery, because the atrophied adrenal cannot mount its own response. Recovery of the axis can take <b>months</b>.","o":{"A":"Abrupt withdrawal causes hyperglycaemia and hypertension","B":"Abrupt withdrawal causes rebound up-regulation of glucocorticoid receptors","C":"Prednisolone accumulates in tissue and must be washed out gradually","D":"Tapering prevents the underlying disease from relapsing, but there is no endocrine risk","E":"Exogenous steroid suppresses CRH and ACTH, so the adrenal cortex has atrophied and cannot resume cortisol production immediately"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"Hyperglycaemia and hypertension are effects of steroid <b>excess</b>. Withdrawal produces the opposite.","B":"Receptor up-regulation is not the mechanism; adrenal atrophy is.","C":"Prednisolone does not accumulate in that way; the problem is endocrine, not pharmacokinetic.","D":"Disease relapse is a real concern, but the <b>endocrine</b> risk is the reason tapering is mandatory.","E":"Correct. Sustained exogenous steroid switches off hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH by negative feedback, and without ACTH the <b>adrenal cortex atrophies</b>. Stopping the drug abruptly leaves the patient unable to make cortisol at all — <b>acute adrenal insufficiency</b>, with hypotension, dehydration, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia and, in the extreme, adrenal crisis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_16.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|38","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"38","t":"Glucocorticoid effect on white cells","s":"A patient started on high-dose prednisolone has a <b>rising neutrophil count</b> but falling lymphocytes. How is this explained?","lec":"L7","sl":10,"c":"⚠️ <b>A counter-intuitive point worth holding on to: a steroid raises the neutrophil count while being immunosuppressive.</b> The rise reflects redistribution, not enhanced function — those neutrophils are less able to migrate into tissue, so the patient is <i>more</i> susceptible to infection despite the higher count. The full immune effect: lymphocytes ↓, monocytes ↓, eosinophils ↓, basophils ↓, <b>neutrophils ↑</b>; cytokine production suppressed; and <b>increased risk of infection</b>, including reactivation of latent tuberculosis. Infection risk is on the adverse-effect grid the lecturer called the spine of the topic.","o":{"A":"The finding indicates a laboratory error, since steroids suppress all white cells","B":"Steroids stimulate the bone marrow to overproduce neutrophils only","C":"The neutrophilia indicates an occult bacterial infection and is unrelated to the steroid","D":"Steroids increase lymphocyte margination while reducing neutrophil production","E":"Steroids demarginate neutrophils from vessel walls and delay their apoptosis, while causing lymphocyte redistribution and apoptosis"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"The dissociated pattern is real and characteristic.","B":"Marrow production is not the main driver of the acute rise; redistribution is.","C":"Steroid-induced neutrophilia is expected and can be substantial. Assuming infection here is the classic clinical error — though steroids do also mask genuine infection.","D":"Exactly the wrong way round for both cell types.","E":"Correct, and the two arms have different mechanisms. <b>Neutrophils rise</b> because steroids release them from the marginated pool along vessel walls, reduce their adhesion to endothelium so they cannot leave the circulation, and delay their apoptosis. <b>Lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils and basophils fall</b>, through redistribution out of the circulation and, for lymphocytes, apoptosis."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_10.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|39","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"39","t":"Glucocorticoid receptor","s":"Which statement about the glucocorticoid receptor is correct?","lec":"L7","sl":9,"c":"Slide 9's mechanism, step by step: <b>lipophilic steroid crosses the membrane</b> → binds the <b>cytoplasmic</b> receptor → hsp90 dissociates → <b>homodimerisation</b> → nuclear translocation → binds <b>GRE</b> → transactivation (lipocortin, anti-inflammatory proteins) and transrepression (NF-κB, AP-1, so cytokines fall). Note that the delay explains the clinical rule: steroids are <b>controller</b> therapy, never rescue. Contrast with L3's adapalene item, where <b>heterodimeric</b> is the word used — same 'regulates mRNA' answer, different dimer partner.","o":{"A":"It is a ligand-gated ion channel","B":"It is a membrane receptor coupled to Gs","C":"It is nuclear and heterodimerises with RXR","D":"It is cytoplasmic, and the drug-receptor complex must form a homodimer before it can enter the nucleus and bind the GRE","E":"It acts within seconds, which is why steroids relieve acute asthma immediately"},"a":"D","r":{"A":"Ion channels act in milliseconds; this receptor acts over hours.","B":"It is explicitly <b>not</b> a membrane receptor — a point she made directly.","C":"<b>Heterodimerisation with RXR</b> is what retinoid, thyroid and vitamin D receptors do. The glucocorticoid receptor <b>homo</b>dimerises. This is the near-miss the exam relies on.","D":"Correct. The unbound receptor sits in the <b>cytoplasm</b> complexed with hsp90. On binding steroid it sheds hsp90, <b>homodimerises</b>, translocates to the nucleus and binds the <b>glucocorticoid response element (GRE)</b>. The lecturer stressed this and examined it: a complex that <b>cannot dimerise produces no effect at all</b>.","E":"Genomic steroid effects take <b>hours</b>, because new protein must be synthesised — which is exactly why a β2 agonist, not a steroid, is the rescue drug in acute asthma."},"f":["author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_9.webp"},{"id":"P|AUTH|40","k":"mcq","src":"author","y":"AUTH","n":"40","t":"Osmosis SDL — dexamethasone suppression test","s":"A patient has Cushingoid features. A low-dose dexamethasone suppression test fails to suppress cortisol; a high-dose test does suppress it. What does this indicate?","lec":"L7","sl":11,"c":"⚠️ <b>This is Osmosis SDL content, not slide content.</b> The lecturer assigns the Osmosis playlist as optional self-directed learning; bingo's rule is that <b>the slides are the spine</b> and Osmosis fills real gaps only. It is included because the differential diagnosis of Cushing's makes the ketoconazole and mifepristone items on the slides make sense.<br>The pattern to remember: <b>low dose suppresses → normal</b> · <b>low no, high yes → pituitary (Cushing DISEASE)</b> · <b>neither, ACTH low → adrenal tumour</b> · <b>neither, ACTH high → ectopic ACTH</b>.<br>And the terminology: <b>Cushing DISEASE = pituitary adenoma</b>; <b>Cushing SYNDROME = the clinical picture from any cause</b>, most commonly exogenous steroids.","o":{"A":"Normal HPA axis function","B":"An adrenal cortisol-secreting tumour","C":"Ectopic ACTH secretion, typically from a small-cell lung carcinoma","D":"Exogenous steroid use","E":"Cushing disease — a pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma"},"a":"E","r":{"A":"A normal axis suppresses fully at the <b>low</b> dose.","B":"An <b>adrenal</b> tumour makes cortisol autonomously, independent of ACTH, so <b>neither</b> dose suppresses it. ACTH would also be low.","C":"<b>Ectopic ACTH</b> has no feedback control at all, so neither dose suppresses. ACTH would be high, and the picture is often rapid with marked hypokalaemia.","D":"With exogenous steroid, endogenous cortisol and ACTH are both <b>already suppressed</b> — the patient is Cushingoid with low measured cortisol.","E":"Correct. A <b>pituitary adenoma retains some negative feedback</b> — it resists the low dose but is suppressed by the high dose. That pattern defines <b>Cushing DISEASE</b>."},"f":["osmosis","author"],"img":"slides_p/L7_11.webp"}];
window.META_P={"lecOrder":["L1","L2","L3","L3B","L4","L5","L6","L7"],"lecName":{"L1":"Introduction to Pharmacology","L2":"Pharmacokinetics","L3":"Pharmacodynamics","L3B":"Drug Allergy","L4":"Drug Development & Regulatory Issues","L5":"General Properties of Autacoids","L6":"General Principles of Cancer Chemotherapy","L7":"General Properties of Glucocorticoids"},"lecShort":{"L1":"L1","L2":"L2","L3":"L3","L3B":"L3b","L4":"L4","L5":"L5","L6":"L6","L7":"L7"},"yearOrder":["FPF","BM32","BM31","BM30","BM29","BM28","AX"],"yearMeta":{"FPF":["FPF · Formative 2569","the faculty’s FundPharm formative site · all 22 items, keyed and explained by the site itself"],"BM32":["BM32 · 2568","Summative I + the L6/L7 items from its Summative II · student-recalled, no key"],"BM31":["BM31 · 2567","Summative I (46 of 52 recovered) + Summative II L6/L7 · keyed by BM32"],"BM30":["BM30 · 2566","Summative I, Formative 1 & 2, and Summative II L6/L7 · keyed by BM31"],"BM29":["BM29 · 2565","Summative I + the NSAID item from its Summative II · keyed"],"BM28":["BM28 · 2564","Summative I from the combined I+II paper"],"AX":["AX · หลักสูตร 2554","SCID246-2 bank, years 55-58 · deepest PK/PD source, keys unverified"]},"authorMeta":["✦ Author-made drill","40 hard vignettes, 5 per lecture — NOT past papers"],"built":"Pharmacology Summative I · BM33 L1-L7","lecBlurb":"8 BM33 buckets, L1 → L7","aboutTitle":"Pharmacology Summative I — past-paper trainer","aboutBody":"Every item names the <b>cohort and question number</b> it came from. Answers are verified against the <b>BM33 L1–L7</b> decks and standard pharmacology, not copied from the student keys — BM32 has no key at all, and the AX bank's compiler warns that its keys were never checked. Where a key looks wrong, or more than one answer is defensible, it is said openly (⚠).<br><br>Chemotherapy and glucocorticoids sat in the OLD cohorts' <b>Summative II</b>, so those items are harvested from their SUM 2 papers and re-mapped onto BM33's L6 and L7. The sympathomimetic / parasympatholytic blocks inside these same papers are BM33 Summative II material and are deliberately excluded.<br><br><b>FPF · Formative 2569</b> is not a past paper — it is the faculty's own <i>FundPharm Formative</i> practice site, which serves a random 7 of its 22 items per attempt. All 22 are here, with the site's own key and its own Thai explanation folded into the concept box; the per-option reasons are written here, because the site gives none. Two of them (Reye's syndrome and AERD) are not taught on any BM33 slide and say so."};
